/* Machine translations of Metaphysik K3 (uncorrected OCR), in the manner of Guyer and Wood: see translation-briefs.js. Not the translators' own work. */
window.KANT_TR = window.KANT_TR || {};
KANT_TR['metaphysik-k3/guyer'] = {"metaphysik-k3-s01-001":["1.) Metaphysics belongs to the material part of philosophy, or rather comprehends it under itself, and therefore, since it presupposes actual objects, rests on laws, i.e. on grounds of cognition (*principiis*) of that, and about that, which belongs to the existence of things. From it there is therefore separated off the merely formal part of philosophy, or the laws of thinking delivered in logic, since these abstract from the objects themselves. It is distinguished already by this from mathematics, since the latter does not rest, like philosophy, on laws of the cognition of things, but on made concepts of things through the construction of them.",["Philosophie rendered 'philosophy' throughout (the German has Weltweisheit in the glossary, but the text reads 'Philosophie').","'Erkenntniß Gründen' = 'grounds of cognition'; Erkenntniß = cognition throughout.","'Objecte' and 'Gegenstände' (in later passages) are both 'object(s)'; Object is the Latinate member here.","'deßen, und über dasjenige' is awkward in the German and kept awkward; 'that, and about that, which' follows it literally.","'Sie' in the third sentence ('It is distinguished') could refer to metaphysics or to philosophy as a whole; the ambiguity is left as in the German.","'Daseyn' = existence; 'Begriffe' = concepts."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-002":["Metaphysical cognitions must therefore be absolutely cognitions of reason, arising *a priori*, through pure concepts of reason, i.e. the *principia* or grounds of cognition are so constituted that one combines with the cognition itself the necessity of that which one cognizes, and the concepts are directed at objects that are not only cognized independently of all experience, but that also can never, possibly, become an object of experience. E.g. God, freedom, immortality. They therefore differ *e diametro* from all empirical appearances, and from principles derived from them: metaphysics therefore has no *principia a posteriori*, but only *a priori* alone: they are given and are cognized through reason alone, but not made.",["'Vernunft Erkenntniße' = 'cognitions of reason'; 'Erkenntniß Gründe' = 'grounds of cognition'.","'Grundsätzen' = 'principles', distinguished from Latin *principia*, which is left in Latin.","'beschaffen' rendered 'constituted' (cf. Beschaffenheit = constitution).","'möglicher Weise' stands between commas after 'never'; it could modify 'können' or 'Gegenstand der Erfahrung'. Kept as placed ('can never, possibly, become').","'ZE.' (= z. E., zum Exempel) rendered 'E.g.'","'a priori entstehen' could attach to 'Erkenntniße' or to the verb; kept as 'arising *a priori*'.","The final 'sie' ('they are given') has no clear antecedent (principia, Begriffe or Gegenstände); left ambiguous."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-003":["*Generaliter*, therefore, metaphysics is the system of pure philosophy, and therefore, in order to express the specific difference from all empirical cognitions, as well as to separate it from pure mathematics, metaphysics is the system of pure cognitions of reason through concepts. The objects of the same are the material part of philosophy, namely physics and ethics, i.e. the laws of nature and of freedom. Hence it divides itself into",["'Gegenstände' and 'Objecte' both 'object(s)'.","'derselben' rendered 'of the same'; antecedent ambiguous (metaphysics or system), left so.","'Physic' and 'ethic' given in English as 'physics' and 'ethics'; the German has Latinate spelling 'physic', 'ethic'.","'Gesetze' = laws; the passage breaks off before the divisions that follow in the next passages."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-004":["a.) Metaphysics of nature, i.e. the cognition of the constitution of the objects *ex principiis a priori*.",["'Beschaffenheit' = constitution.","'Objecte' = objects."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-005":["b.) Metaphysics of morals, i.e. the cognition of the rules or grounds of determination of our actions *ex principiis a priori*.",["'Sitten' rendered 'morals'.","'Bestimmungs Gründe' = 'grounds of determination' (Bestimmung = determination; Grund = ground)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-006":["One also calls it *physicam generalem*, because it is cognized only from *principiis a priori*. The cognition of nature, or doctrine of nature in general, is namely either doctrine of bodies, which is called in *specie* doctrine of nature, insofar as it is an object of outer sense, or doctrine of the soul, insofar as it is an object of inner sense, and consequently abstracts from all corporeal alterations.",["'Naturlehre' = 'doctrine of nature' (Lehre = doctrine); 'Körperlehre' = 'doctrine of bodies'; 'Seelenlehre' = 'doctrine of the soul'.","'Sinn' = sense; 'Gegenstand' = object.","'sie' in 'insofar as it is an object' follows Natur / Naturlehre; antecedent left as in German. The subject of 'abstrahirt' is likewise not marked.","'Veränderungen' rendered 'alterations'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-007":["It is, however, in this entirely different from an empirical doctrine of morals or of nature.",["'Sittenoder' (OCR run-together) read as 'Sitten- oder'.","'Naturlehre' = 'doctrine of nature'; rendered 'doctrine of ... nature' here."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-008":["Here the rules of cognition determine themselves from experience in accordance with the appearances that morals or nature have delivered, in order to derive *principia* from them: e.g. the laws of the police are laws of morals, which however determine pragmatically, or rather practically, the laws of security and of the comfort of life. Further the doctrine of nature of the chemists. Their *principia* are obviously built on experience, and cognized from it. So the doctrine of nature too can be cognized *ex principiis a posteriori*. But from all these ways of cognitions the metaphysical cognition is entirely different: it does not at all occupy itself with the objects as they are, since thereby the necessity of experience, if metaphysics were built on it, would not be had insight into. Just as little does it lay as ground the intuitions *a priori* from which mathematics alone builds its cognitions of reason: namely space and time. The definition of the author (§ 1.), which is taken from Wolff, is false in that he demands *prima principia cognitionis* for metaphysics, for that can after all only mean the first concepts in the abstraction of the same, consequently in the series of concepts: according to this, however, metaphysics would occupy itself only with the concept of a thing, since this is the highest concept of reason, and would then have to descend: in descending, however, beginning and end could not be determined, nor which is to be the first or last concept: and then every empirical concept would connect itself with it, which however is faulty, in that it lies outside the boundaries of metaphysics, e.g. *ens* — substance — composition, whereas body is already an empirical concept in space.",["OCR '\\\\ olf' read as 'Wolff'; 'hegt' read as 'liegt' ('lies'); 'Noth wendigkeit' read as 'Nothwendigkeit'; 'lste Saeculum' is in the next passage.","'Sitten Gesetze' = 'laws of morals'; 'Gesetze der Policey' rendered 'laws of the police'.","'Arten' = 'ways' (Art = way; kind; manner).","'Gränzen' = 'boundaries' (Grenze = bound(ary)); 'Anschauungen' = 'intuitions'; 'Vernunft-Erkenntniße' = 'cognitions of reason'.","'Verfasser' (author) here is Baumgarten; 'definition' = Erklärung/Definition, rendered 'definition'.","'eingesehen werden würde' kept literal ('would not be had insight into'); 'Corpus' left as 'body' though in Latin form in the German.","'daß er ... verlangt': 'er' = the author; 'es' in 'er außer den Gränzen' (the empirical concept) is ambiguous between 'concept' and 'every empirical concept'; kept as 'it'.","'darauf gebauet', 'daraus erkannt' refer to experience; 'derselben' (of the same) is ambiguous and kept so."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-009":["It would be as if one cut off history by periods of time: one could then e.g. extend ancient history to the 1st *saeculum* or up to the present day.",["OCR 'lste' read as '1ste' (1st).","'Zeiträumen' rendered 'periods of time' (Raum = space; Zeit = time).","'extendiren' = 'extend' (Ausdehnung = extension)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-010":["The concept — first laws — is therefore too indeterminate, in that all pure cognitions of reason through concepts belong here; without one's being able to call them the first.",["'unbestimmt' rendered 'indeterminate' (bestimmen = determine).","'ohne daß man sie die ersten nennen kann' kept literal."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-011":["2.) According to the sense of the word, metaphysics is a science of nature, which restricts itself to the rational part and to it insofar as it can be cognized without experience. Metaphysics is physics beyond the empirical cognition of nature: here one expected a great field, without determining it, or being able to determine it according to its boundaries. One met here three objects, which lay beyond the boundaries of cognition of nature, and were discovered and cognized solely *a priori*, or through human reason alone. These are",["'Wortverstande' rendered 'sense of the word' (Verstand here not 'understanding' in the technical sense).","'Wißenschaft' = science (*scientia*); 'Grenzen' = boundaries; 'Erfahrung' = experience.","'Meta-physic' read as 'Metaphysic'.","'darauf einschränkt, insoweit' is ambiguous; kept as 'to the rational part and to it insofar as'.","'hier erwartete man' and 'man traf' both with indefinite 'man'; 'Natur Erkenntniß' = 'cognition of nature'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-012":["1.) God, i.e. the first beginning of all things.",["'Dinge' = things."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-013":["2.) Freedom, i.e. a faculty of the human being, independent of all influence of nature, of acting in accordance with reason, with resistance against all sensible impulses and forces of nature.",["'Vermögen' = faculty; 'sinnlich' = sensible; 'Kräfte' = forces.","'Menschen' rendered 'human being'.","The German word order puts 'der Vernunft gemäß zu handeln' after the resistance clause; kept in that order approximately, as English allows.","'Antriebe' rendered 'impulses'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-014":["3.) Immortality, i.e. the object of the investigation of the understanding, to what extent the soul, as a being of its own, will survive the physical human being.",["'Verstand' = understanding; 'Wesen' = being; 'Gegenstand' = object.","'eignes Wesen' rendered 'a being of its own'; could also be 'its own being'.","'inwiefern' rendered 'to what extent'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-015":["All three are pure concepts of reason, which absolutely cannot be exhibited in appearance, which consequently can only be thought. One can therefore call them supersensible objects, *noumena* i.e. objects of the understanding, and oppose them to the *phaenomena*.",["'Erscheinung' = appearance; 'übersinnliche' = supersensible; 'Verstand' = understanding.","'darstellen' rendered 'exhibit'.","'phaenomenis opponiren' is dative/ablative plural Latin; rendered 'oppose them to the *phaenomena*'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-016":["The attempt now to investigate these objects more closely was the origin of metaphysics. Chance was therefore not the ground of the origin. It is alleged that the founder of metaphysics, Aristotle, did not have the intention of writing a system of pure philosophical cognitions that lie beyond physics, and also did not write one, but that he ordered something to be filed *meta physicam*, i.e. *post physicam*. This is as empty a tale as that about the origin of the term philosophy, according to which Pythagoras did not want to call himself a *sophon*, but only a *philo-sophon*. It lay, surely, in the nature of the things themselves that one should take pains over a closer development of these supersensible objects. It is only striking that the human being (and this is innate in every human being) found, and still finds, an interest in it. For metaphysics contributes nothing to the extension of the empirical *principia*, of the science of empirical physics. The knowledge is, with regard to physics, entirely unnecessary, in that the *principia* of metaphysics are set entirely to one side, and one proceeds from established appearances, and the principles derived from them are sufficient to explain everything from them. Experience confirms, e.g., the rational propositions: in all alterations substance never perishes, but only the form of things, or: every alteration has its cause; so much so that one merely assumes them, without inquiring into their ground, and one is already made certain of their truth under all circumstances through experience. On the other hand it is certain that all our sensible cognitions are also only sensibly conditioned, hence, like the things themselves, alterable, and contingently certain. Therein lies the ground that the human being finds no satisfaction for his reason in this, insofar as he takes pains to cognize and to attain his *summum bonum*, i.e. the highest final end of all his ends, the highest degree of worthiness to be happy, combined with the greatest morality. This object of his endeavor lies beyond nature, he cannot find all his empirical knowledge sufficient for it, he must find it merely through reason in its laws: he feels it as necessary that this alone be the highest end and vocation for reason: he may, e.g., direct his investigation to the determination of duty and right, to the reward of his actions in that life, to the determination of himself, etc.; and therein lies the ground that metaphysics absolutely must be cultivated, because otherwise the whole end of all theoretical and practical cognitions of reason cannot be fulfilled. Hence it is also certain that every human being occupies himself with his own metaphysics, and about this there is properly no doubt, *an metaphysica enucleanda sit*; on the other hand what matters is the method according to which it is treated. Hence all despisers of the metaphysical sciences punish themselves in this, that they despise the science itself, and yet esteem their own method of thinking about supersensible truths. In short, without metaphysics no human being can be.",["Wißenschaft = science; Kenntniß = knowledge (not Erkenntniß = cognition); Erkenntniß = cognition; Gegenstand and Object/objecte both 'object'.","Veränderung rendered 'alteration' (not in glossary); Endzweck = 'final end'; Zweck = 'end'; Bestimmung = 'determination' in 'Bestimmung von Pflicht und Recht' and 'Bestimmung seiner selbst', but 'vocation' in 'Zweck und Bestimmung'.","'sie' in 'bestätigt ... so sehr, daß man sie blos annimmt' is plural, referring to the propositions; 'ihren Grund' ambiguous between the propositions' ground and (singular) ground of each; left as in German. 'Darin liegt der Grund, daß' and 'insofern ... als' kept literal.","'Würdigkeit glücklich zu seyn' rendered literally; 'zufällig-gewiß' = 'contingently certain'.","'sophon', 'philo-sophon' are Greek in Latin letters, italicized as the foreign words; 'termini' (genitive of terminus) rendered 'term'. 'findt' = 'findet' (original spelling). 'an metaphysica enucleanda sit' left in Latin."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-017":["3.) The Critique of pure reason is the propaedeutic to transcendental philosophy.",["OCR '( ritic' and 'transzendentalph ilosophie' silently corrected to 'Critic' and 'transzendentalphilosophie'. 'Propaedeutic' kept as the Latinate transliteration."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-018":["One calls, namely, a science that occupies itself with the possibility of having insight into a cognition *a priori* the Critique of pure reason. All cognitions of reason, and which are thought independently of all experience *a priori*, belong here, they may concern mathematical or philosophical objects, e.g. every alteration must have its cause.",["'vor aller Erfahrung' rendered 'of all experience' as literally 'before' = 'vor' for 'von'; the German reads 'vor', possibly for 'von'; the sense 'independently of' is retained as printed. Doubtful reading.","'Alle Erkenntniße der Vernunft, und die ... gedacht werden' is syntactically awkward in the German ('und die' = 'and which'); kept awkward.","'Möglichkeit ... ein Erkenntniß a priori einzusehen' is ambiguous between the possibility of having insight into a cognition a priori and the possibility of having insight a priori; 'a priori' may attach to 'Erkenntniß' or 'einzusehen'; the English keeps the placement after 'cognition'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-019":["The cognizing reason stands here as subject with regard to its faculty before the examining judgment-seat, as to how far it can occupy itself with regard to such cognitions, the critique of pure reason is in this regard to be regarded as a higher logic, in that it gives reason rules to hand as to how it is to cognize objects *a priori*, and these rules are nothing but *principia a priori*: it is therefore different from logic itself in this, that the latter abstracts from all objects, and states the rules of thinking in general. It is called Critique because it does not proceed dogmatically, but engages in the investigation of errors and the examination of our presumed judgments. *In specie*, therefore, that philosophy which occupies itself with the possibility of cognitions *a priori* in our reason is the Critique of pure reason; but the system of those cognitions themselves which contain the *elementa* of pure reason is transcendental philosophy, which therefore differs from the Critique in this, that the latter investigates the possibility of cognizing *a priori*, whereas transcendental philosophy has before its eyes the objective sum total of all those cognitions that are cognized *a priori*, and that are regarded as *elementa* or first *principia* of our cognition. One calls transcendental philosophy also ontology, and it is the product of the Critique of pure reason.",["Richterstuhl = 'judgment-seat'; Urtheile = 'judgments'; Inbegriff = 'sum total'; Vermögen = 'faculty'; Irthümer = 'errors'; angemaaßten = 'presumed'.","'in wie weit sie sich ... beschäftigen kann' kept as in German; 'dabei' rendered 'in this regard'.","Comma splices of the German retained. 'Philososphie' (OCR/orig.) silently corrected."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-020":["4.) All concepts are acquired, but not all from the senses. The faculty, on the other hand, of acquiring concepts through development of marks, is innate. E.g. the hare seeks, when pursued, to outwit the dog, the dog learns through practice the tricks of the hare, and seeks to become superior to it. Here, to be sure, no concepts are to be assumed, since animals have no faculty of reason, but instinct forms through the many similar cases an experience that serves the dog as a guideline. Concepts, after all, already presuppose representations of objects, and are abstracted from the marks, they therefore cannot be innate, but are acquired, or made, though this cannot happen otherwise than through the faculty of the soul that operates in this.",["Begriff = concept; Merkmal/Merckmalen = mark; Vorstellung = representation; Vermögen = faculty.","'so hiebei operirt' is read as a relative clause ('that operates in this'); 'so' = 'which'. 'Haase' = Hase. 'Richtschnur' = 'guideline'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-021":["There was, however, with all this a great problem:",["'Problem' kept as cognate (Aufgabe is the glossary's German 'problem', but the German here has Latinate Problem)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-022":["whether many concepts are innate to us, or whether all concepts *sive a priori sive a posteriori* were acquired.",["The 'I' before 'a priori' in the OCR is a stray mark and is left out. 'wären' subjunctive rendered 'were'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-023":["Plato assumed the *ideas connatas*, Aristotle on the other hand the *principium nihil est in intellectu, quod antea non fuerit in sensu*, and defended, according to the sense of this principle, that we acquire all concepts through the senses. Plato indeed also assumed sensible representations, but he separated this source of cognition as uncertain from the pure concepts of understanding as the *ideis connatis*, which he assumed alone to be certain. Plato asserted, namely, that the concepts *a priori* or pure concepts of reason are produced merely from the *fond* of reason, and that for the consciousness of them it is not necessary to take objects of the senses to help, and to presuppose experience in their origin. For the mathematical truths and their so fruitful consequences and irrefutable certainty put him in astonishment, e.g. the propositions that one drew from the figure of the circle in *mathesi pura*.",["Verstandes Begriffe = 'concepts of understanding'; Satz/Sätze = 'proposition(s)'; Folgen = 'consequences' (Folge in glossary = 'sequel; sequence'; here the context is plainly 'consequences'); Gewisheit = 'certainty'.","'Vernnuft' silently corrected to 'Vernunft'. 'fond' left in the original French form. Latin 'ideis connatis' (sic, ablative for ideas) left as printed.","'Prinzip' rendered 'principle' where in the German 'Princips' stands, and Latin *principium* left in Latin."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-024":["In the circle all intersecting lines give correct relations of the lines, angles, triangles, e.g.",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-025":["a : b : c = b : d : e",["Formula carried over as printed; may be garbled by OCR."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-026":["He assumed that the human understanding had not found these concepts and propositions through sensible perception, but that in the human being a supersensible faculty of cognition is present, i.e. a *noumenon*, in which cognitions could form themselves purely abstracted, without taking the senses to help. One can call this faculty and the cognition by means of it at least supersensible. Only his hypothesis was mystical. He laid, namely, a pure intuition of the non-sensible or = a supersensible intuition at the ground and assumed that the soul, before it came into the bodily constitution, had the faculty for the intuition of the Godhead, and that even if it no longer partakes of this in this life, a consciousness of those pure ideas of understanding can nevertheless be awakened in human beings, and that this consciousness is the source of cognition *a priori*.",["Anschauung = 'intuition'; Wahrnehmung = 'perception'; Erkenntniß Vermögen = 'faculty of cognition'; Verfaßung = 'constitution' (Beschaffenheit is also 'constitution').","'so sich Erkenntniße rein abgezogen bilden konnten' is obscure; 'so' read as relative 'in which'; 'rein abgezogen' may modify the cognitions or the manner of forming. Left obscure.","'zum Grunde' rendered 'at the ground' (literal for 'zum Grunde legen'); 'deßen ... theilhaftig' rendered 'partakes of this'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-027":["Aristotle, on the other hand, assumed that through reason alone we could attain no concepts at all, denied the *ideas connatas*, and asserted rather that all our concepts have their source solely in sensible reception, and hence would be acquired through experience, and denied the *ideas a priori* and posited only *sensualia*; hence the famous dispute over the certainty of our cognition.",["OCR 'Erd fahrung' read as 'Erfahrung' (experience).","Begriff = concept; Erkenntniß = cognition; sinnlich = sensible; behaupten = assert; statuiren rendered 'posit' (the glossary's setzen is not the German word here).","'ideas connatas', 'ideas a priori', 'sensualia' are Latin in the original and kept in italics; the accusative forms are as printed.","'Gewisheit' (certainty) is not in the glossary; plain cognate used."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-028":["The defenders of the *noumenon* (or of the pure concepts of the understanding, i.e. that through the pure faculty of reason we would be in a position to obtain, without experience, concepts, which are called innate) asserted: *phaenomenorum s. sensibilium non datur scientia* (i.e. the senses offered no material for the truth or certainty of cognition; *phaenomena* namely are called all cognitions a priori whose object is possible, *noumena*, on the other hand, cognitions, or transcendent concepts, that have no object in possible intuition). The defenders of the *phaenomena*, on the other hand, rejected the pure concepts of the understanding: there is only certainty and truth in external things.",["Verstand = understanding; Vermögen = faculty; Stoff = material; Anschauung = intuition; Gegenstand = object; Ding = thing.","'Erkenntniße' appears twice in the parenthesis ('noumena dagegen Erkenntniße, oder überschwengliche Begriffe'); the text is kept as it stands, though the sense of 'phaenomena heißen ... Erkenntniße a priori' is odd.","'überschwenglich' rendered 'transcendent' (not in the glossary; a choice).","'a priori' left unitalicized in the German-text position as printed; the sentence 'wären ... zu bekommen' is kept in the subjunctive as 'would be'.","Latin 'phaenomenorum s. sensibilium non datur scientia' kept in Latin; 'scientia' here Latin, corresponding to Wissen/Wissenschaft."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-029":["Epicurus posited concepts only insofar as the objects of them could be presented visibly — hence he also flatly denied a deity.",["'statuirte' rendered 'posited'; 'Gegenstände' = objects; 'dargestellt' = presented."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-030":["The skeptics assumed, neither in the case of cognitions of the understanding nor of empirical ones, a certainty, and the existence.",["The sentence is kept as awkward as the German: 'die existenz' has no clear construction with 'annehmen' and may be corrupt or elliptical.","Erkenntnißen = cognitions; 'Verstandes- noch empirischen Erkenntnißen' kept in parallel form."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-031":["One must, in the question: *an ideae connatae existant*, distinguish between the *modum acquirendi* and the *modum habendi*. All cognition begins from experience, and all cognition of reason also cannot be obtained otherwise than from experience. But on that account not all cognitions that we have stem immediately from experience. The way and manner in which cognitions are present in us, or the *modus habendi*, can produce such cognitions as are grounded only in reason, abstracted from all experience. Something similar lies in the derivation of the *Conceptus originarii — derivativi —*; both are, like every concept, by their nature *factitii*; but it is true that the *originarii* are grounded, in their origin, not on experience but merely on reason.",["Art und Weise = way and manner (Art = way).","'Vernunft Erkenntniß' rendered 'cognition of reason'.","'abstrahirt von aller Erfahrung' may attach to 'Erkenntniße' or to 'Vernunft'; left as ambiguous as the German.","The dashes after 'originarii' and 'derivativi' are in the source; Latin kept in italics."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-032":["In order to determine the foregoing more closely, and to clarify it, it is certain:",["bestimmen = determine; erläutern = clarify. The clause 'ist gewiß :' is kept with its colon."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-033":["1.) All representations and concepts in the human being begin initially from objects of experience. This, however, means nothing more than: our faculty of cognition, in order to obtain cognitions, even concepts of the understanding, must be awakened by objects of experience, the faculty of reception of the senses be set into activity.",["Vorstellung = representation; Vermögen = faculty; Object = object (Gegenstand and Object both 'object').","'receptions Vermögen der Sinne' rendered 'faculty of reception of the senses'; 'Tätigkeit' = activity."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-034":["2.) All concepts are acquired, and there can be no *idea connata*. For concepts presuppose a thinking, are made, or thought, through the apprehension of marks, and abstraction of the universal. Thoughts thus arise through an *operatio mentis* to be undertaken beforehand.",["Merkmale = marks; 'allgemeinen' rendered 'universal' (allgemein not in the glossary; 'gemein' is 'common').","'gedacht' (thought) and 'Gedanken' (thoughts) are cognate; kept so."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-035":["3.) Notwithstanding this there are concepts a priori, there are *intuitus a priori*, there are propositions and judgments a priori. Thus the concept of cause and of causality, i.e. of the constitution of the object, to be a cause, is a *conceptus a priori*; the representation of space is a concept of something that may not be derived from experience or arise from it; rather one must grasp the representation of a space before one thinks an object within it; this representation is grounded on an intuition a priori, which precedes all experience. The proposition and the judgment: *ex nihilo nihil fit* or, in all alterations nothing (substantial) arises and perishes, is a judgment a priori. The criterion of all concepts a priori on the part of their source lies in this, that they take their origin not from experience but from the faculty of cognition of reason itself, hence the whole attention rests only on investigating in what way we arrive at, and are capable of, cognizing a priori, and this the Critique of Pure Reason teaches, as it does the limitations of cognition. Moreover, in the acquisition of concepts a priori the circumstance that we apply our faculty of cognizing to objects of experience changes nothing: for from this there does not yet follow the necessity that the acquired concepts must also stem from experience and not merely from the faculty of reason.",["'Caussalitet' read as Causalität; rendered 'causality'. 'Verunft' read 'Vernunft'. 'gründt' read 'gründet'.","Beschaffenheit = constitution; Satz = proposition; Urtheil = judgment; Schranken = limitations; Gegenstände = objects; object (lower case, Latin-derived 'object') = object.","'Ursprung' = origin; 'Quelle' = source; 'Veränderungen' rendered 'alterations' (not in glossary).","'zu erkennen' = to cognize; 'a priori' after 'erkennen' may attach to 'erkennen' or to 'gelangen' ('arrive at'); left ambiguous.","'aus der Erfahrung abgeleitet werden darf oder daraus entspringt': 'darf' rendered 'may', as in the German, which is ambiguous between permission and necessity-negation."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-036":["4.) The way of acquiring the concepts a priori one can think, *analogice*, as in natural right, in a twofold manner.",["Art = way; zwiefach = twofold; 'Natur Recht' = natural right. 'analogice' kept in Latin."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-037":["a.) *Originarie*, i.e. insofar as the concepts a priori are taken forth from the nature of the faculty of cognition or from the faculty of thinking alone. E.g. the representation of space. These concepts have so little arisen through experience that they are rather present before all experience, without one being able nonetheless to call them *ideas connatas*: perhaps the concept of space in general could belong to the latter.",["Erkenntnißvermögen = faculty of cognition; Vermögen zu denken = faculty of thinking.","'zu letztem' kept as 'to the latter' (may refer to the *ideas connatas* or to the last-named); 'originarie' is Latin-derived adverb kept in italics."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-038":["If, besides the capacity to cognize, still",["Fähigkeit = capacity. The sentence breaks off and continues in the next passage (b.)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-039":["b.) something empirical is mixed in, the concept hence not pure, then it is *derivativus*.",["rein = pure; 'beygemischt' = mixed in.","Continues the previous passage: 'Ist ... noch b.) etwas empirisches beygemischt'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-040":["5.) Just as it is now certain that no cognition could well be present of which there should not, although it has taken its origin from experience, nevertheless be present in the latter a corresponding object, through which the concept could be presented, so the cognitions a priori are accordingly distinguished into a.) sensible cognitions *vel phaenomena*, i.e. all cognitions a priori that can be presented and applied in the case of objects of the senses; from these one goes over to the b.) *noumena*, or supersensible cognitions, pure cognitions of the understanding, of which the object cannot be given in experience and presented to the senses.",["The first clause is syntactically tangled in the German ('davon nicht ... ein correspondirender Gegenstand vorhanden sein sollte'); kept as it stands.","übersinnlich = supersensible; Verstandes Erkenntniße = cognitions of the understanding; 'darstellen' = present.","'Gegenstand' (object) and 'Object' (object) both 'object'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-041":["6.) From the difference of the sources from which one derived cognitions *originarie* arose the difference between a.) empiricism, i.e. the principle of regarding each and every concept, according to its origin, as derived from experience. One accuses Epicurus of this principle, at least so much as is clear from the works of his pupils, since one possesses nothing from him himself. He assumed that one can attribute no certainty to any concept of which the object cannot be shown in experience, and it be confirmed thereby. It does not in itself follow from this that he contested all concepts a priori, in that for very many the proof results in experience. A consequence of this principle is that one must reject metaphysics entirely, as one also accuses Epicurus. For metaphysics must absolutely rest on pure concepts of reason, and can therefore never, according to its source, take them from experience; the error of empiricism therefore rests on this, that, because our faculty of cognition can be set into activity only through objects of experience, it concludes from this that no concept could exist and be made that did not have its origin in experience. b.) Rationalism, the principle of the possibility of representing to oneself cognitions a priori. This principle is the first proposition of all metaphysical truths.",["'Ur-Sprunge' hyphen kept in sense as 'origin'. 'Irthum' read 'Irrthum' (error). 'folgert' = concludes (folgern); 'Folge' = consequence here (glossary: sequel; sequence); 'Beweis' = proof.","'wie man auch den Epicur beschuldigt' kept as elliptical as the German.","'dem princip der Möglichkeit' is in the dative in the source (probably corrupt for 'das Princip'); translated as it stands in apposition.","'sich ... ergiebt' rendered 'results'; 'von sehr vielen' = for very many (referring to concepts)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-042":["Between both principles, empiricism and rationalism, stood in a certain way in the middle c.) mysticism, or the presupposition of an *intellectus intuitivus* or *intuitus intellectualis*, i.e. the possibility that *conceptus a priori mere intellectuales* rest on immediate intuition of the understanding. This mystical hypothesis thus assumed that the understanding could operate like the senses, have *intuitus puros*; now, however, the faculty of intuition cannot be attributed to the understanding, which rather belongs solely to the senses, hence this hypothesis of Plato falls apart of itself. One says, to be sure, that the understanding of God is pure intuition; these are, however, words without a concept, which we humans at least cannot make of an intuiting understanding, and by which one probably only wanted to set the operation of the divine being in relation to the thinking-faculty of the human being; but not even in the case of God can an analogous thinking be assumed.",["Anschauung = intuition; Verstand = understanding; Vermögen = faculty; Wesen = being; 'anschauend' rendered 'intuiting'.","'das vielmehr allein den Sinnen zukommt': 'das' may refer to 'Vermögen' or to 'Anschauung'; left ambiguous.","'Verstände' (dative) read as 'Verstande'; 'Denkvermögen' rendered 'thinking-faculty'.","'conceptus a priori mere intellectuales' kept as printed (case agreement irregular)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-043":["Plato's principle, that human beings namely by virtue of their formerly possessed faculty of an intuitive understanding still now have the power to recollect through their understanding the concepts had at that time, probably rests on the error into which the excellent clarity and astonishing fruitfulness of many mathematical propositions, in which he was a master, seduced him, and consisted in this, that he took *intuitus a priori puros* and *conceptus a priori puros* to be one and the same.",["Anschauung/anschauend rendered \"intuition/intuitive\" per glossary; anschauenden Verstand = \"intuitive understanding\" (cf. intuitus).","\"besehenen\" is as printed (probably an OCR or transcript slip for \"besessenen\"); I translate \"possessed\" on that assumption, which is a doubtful reading.","Satz (Sätze) = \"proposition\"; Begriffe = \"concepts\"; Kraft = \"power\", Vermögen = \"faculty\".","Latin kept in italics as in the source."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-044":["Aristotle improved him to the extent that he also assumed *intellectualia*, but at the same time *per conceptus*, and rejected the *ideas connatas ex intuitu puro*: meanwhile his principle: *nihil est in intellectu* etc. also sufficiently shows his error, by virtue of which his *conceptus* were not intellectual but *sensitivi*, grasped through the senses, and generally formed through the understanding — hence empirical, e.g. the concept of warmth.",["\"\\ erstand\" is an OCR slip, read as \"Verstand\" (understanding).","\"Irthum\" = Irrthum (error). \"pp.\" (perge, perge) rendered \"etc.\"","\"ideas connatas\" is the transcript's Latin as printed (a grammatical oddity, left as is).","Grundsatz = \"principle\" (glossary); Sinne = \"senses\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-045":["4.) Our faculty of cognition, with regard to supersensible cognitions, certainly requires a closer examination and determination of its power and boundaries. Already in the times of the oldest philosophy, of a Parmenides, Xenocrates, Pythagoras, where concepts were still clothed in a language of images, people thought about the development of the same concepts as now, about freedom of the will in acting, about causality, about the beginning of the world, about whose origin and author everyone is brooding. One regarded these objects as ones that had to be cognized a priori, i.e. through reason: quite forgot that in experience there was no object that could possibly correspond to the concepts, judgments and inferences. Now one saw the fortunate progress that mathematics made, in that by virtue of its intuition a priori it raised itself above the boundaries of experience into the field of possibility. One concluded, since this was a way of cognitions a priori, and it succeeded with mathesis to extend its cognitions beyond sensible objects, one could also venture it with philosophy, and now one abandoned oneself, in one's investigations about supersensible objects, to a free unrestricted path.",["\"I hilosophie\" and \"Fortschi itte\" are OCR slips, read as Philosophie and Fortschritte.","Gränzen = \"boundaries\" (Grenze); Gegenstand = \"object\"; Schluß (Schlüßen) = \"inference\".","\"Art\" rendered \"way\" per glossary; \"Mathesis\" kept as in the source (Mathematic = \"mathematics\").","\"über deren Entstehung und Urheber von jedermann gebrütet wird\" is syntactically loose in the German; I have kept it loose. \"Man sähe\" = \"one saw/regarded\" (sähe for sah).","\"Möglichkeit\" = \"possibility\"; the ambiguity of \"Art der Erkenntniße a priori\" is preserved."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-046":["Dogmatism guided the investigators, i.e. that principle according to which one believed, with regard to the metaphysical, i.e. cognitions of reason a priori, that one did not need a critique of reason in order to get cognitions a priori, and to extend one's sensible cognitions beyond the sensible. The fault of dogmatism lay here therefore in this, that one was not attentive to the fact that one here passed from physical objects to metaphysical ones, (NB and indeed from sensible to supersensible objects) that the latter can have no object in experience on which the truth of the representations, concepts and judgments obtained could be tested, that therefore, because of the lack of the object in intuition, the concepts would have no reality, hence, if the investigation went further than the concepts a priori could lead, empty judgments were possible. One could not appeal to mathematics; for since it constructs its concepts according to intuitions a priori, and hence the concepts of the objects must always possibly present themselves in experience or intuition, it always has a secure guiding thread, by which it does not overstep the boundaries of reason, it is secure in discovering its errors in attempts made; but not so in philosophy, which here has to do merely with cognitions of reason from pure concepts, and there is nothing that lays fetters on the understanding in the progress of the investigation.",["The transcript repeats \"also wegen Mangel des objects in der Anschauung die Begriffe keine\" (dittography); I have translated it once.","\"Ansehnung\" read as \"Ansehung\" (regard). \"Critic\" = \"critique\"; \"realitaet\" = \"reality\" (Realität).","Grundsatz = \"principle\"; Vorstellung = \"representation\"; Fortschritt = \"progress\"; Object/Gegenstand both \"object\" (Gegenstand in most places, Object/objects in \"Mangel des objects\" and \"Objecte\").","\"Vernunft Erkenntniße\" = \"cognitions of reason\"; \"Leitfaden\" = \"guiding thread\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-047":["Hence the result too was that all attempts of the philosophers were thrown into disorder by skepticism, which demonstrated the uncertainty of the product of the supersensible cognitions, and now for its part declared uncertain all cognitions that rested on pure concepts of reason.",["\"über den Haufen geworfen\" rendered literally-idiomatically as \"thrown into disorder\" (lit. thrown on a heap).","\"beruheten\" = \"rested\"; \"products\" kept as \"product\" (Latin-derived word in the source)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s01-048":["Hence dogmatism is the ground of the origin of skepticism. This too went too far, because indeed concepts a priori are really not to be disputed. It was therefore certainly necessary, before one rejected the possibility of having such cognitions of reason, first to examine the faculty of the understanding, its domain, boundaries and limitations, and to find a standard, or *criteria* of truth, according to which one can test the genuineness of such pure concepts to which no object in experience corresponds: then one could with ground arouse doubts. This now is the business of criticism.",["Gränzen = \"boundaries\" (Grenze) and Schranken = \"limitations\" (Schranke), per glossary.","\"Citeria\" is an OCR/transcript slip for Criteria (Kriterium = criterion); I have printed it in italics as \"*criteria*\".","\"Umfang\" = \"domain\"; \"mit Grund\" rendered literally \"with ground\".","\"Criticißmus\" = \"criticism\"; \"Ächtheit\" = \"genuineness\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s02-001":["Division of metaphysics.",["Eintheilung = Einteilung = \"division\" (glossary)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s02-002":["5.) Metaphysics or the system of pure cognitions of reason divides into 2 main sections: I.) Transcendental metaphysics, or that part of metaphysics that presents the elementary concepts in order to cognize objects a priori that can be given: this system of metaphysical cognitions is called ontology and rests on the dissection of reason according to all the elementary concepts contained in it, e.g. magnitude, quality, substance, cause, effect etc. II.) The *metaphysica propria*, so one calls metaphysics when it is applied to objects themselves: these objects are",["Größe = \"magnitude\"; Wirkung = \"effect\"; Qualitaet = \"quality\"; Ursach = \"cause\" (Ursache).","\"Zergliederung\" = \"dissection\" (literal); the passage breaks off mid-sentence, continued in the next passage.","\"Gegenstände\" and \"objecte\" both \"objects\"; \"darstellt\" = \"presents\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s02-003":["a.) either sensible, and then",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s02-004":["1.) the system treats either objects of inner sense or of the soul. Hence doctrine of the soul, *psychologia rationalis*",["\"betritt\" is as printed (probably \"betrifft\", concerns); I render \"treats\" and flag the reading as doubtful.","Lehre = \"doctrine\"; Seelenlehre = \"doctrine of the soul\"; Körperlehre (next passage) = \"doctrine of bodies\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s02-005":["2.) or objects of the outer senses, hence doctrine of bodies, *physica rationalis*",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s02-006":["b.) or objects of mere reason, i.e. *ideae* or concepts of mere reason = cognitions whose objects cannot be given through the objects. These are the objects of supersensible cognition, and such are presented by",["\"ideac\" is an OCR slip for *ideae* (Idee = idea).","\"durch die Objecte nicht gegeben werden können\" is as printed (odd: objects given through objects); preserved, possibly corrupt.","\"stellt ... dar\" is split across passages: \"stellt\" here, \"dar\" in s02-009."]],"metaphysik-k3-s02-007":["1.) the *cosmologia rationalis*, or cosmology of pure reason and \"",["The stray quotation mark after \"und\" is as in the OCR; kept, possibly an apparatus or OCR mark."]],"metaphysik-k3-s02-008":["2.) the *theologia rationalis vel naturalis*, the theology of pure",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s02-009":["reason.",["Completes \"stellt ... dar\" with \"dar\" omitted: the source reads \"Vernunft dar\"; the translation here gives \"reason\" and \"dar\" belongs to the verb \"stellt ... dar\" (\"presents\") in s02-006. Translated as \"reason\" only with \"dar\" absorbed."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-001":["History of metaphysics.",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-002":["6.) It is striking: nature calls us to investigate cognitions of reason; it has for the human being especially an important interest to get to know the objects of the supersensible more exactly, and yet all effort hitherto has brought little fruit, since it is at present still doubtful what of the metaphysical truths one can and ought to assume as certain. Already in the oldest times of philosophy one occupied oneself with metaphysics sooner than with physics, and especially directed one's investigation sooner to the supersensible than to the sensible. One can indeed not state distinctly the theorems and theories of Parmenides, Xenocrates, Pythagoras, since no writings of their own have remained to us, but only later writers, e.g. Diogenes Laertius and others, have set forth their opinions, but not determined enough expounded them; meanwhile it is certain from this that they directed the investigation to the origin of the world, to the cause of the world, as the highest being, to the constitution of the human soul, to its existence after death, or to the future life.",["The final sentence has no finite main verb for \"die Untersuchung gerichtet\" (lacks \"haben\" in position as printed, \"gerichtet\" with \"hat\" earlier); I kept the German's sequence: \"daß sie ... die Untersuchung gerichtet\" rendered \"directed the investigation\".","Lehrsatz = \"theorem\"; Meinung = \"opinion\"; Wesen = \"being\"; Beschaffenheit = \"constitution\"; Daseyn = \"existence\" (Dasein); deutlich = \"distinct(ly)\"; bestimmt = \"determined\".","\"Welt Ursache\" = \"cause of the world\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-003":["It is certain that one, in the investigation of such objects, was seduced by mathematics to make fortunate progress a priori, and believed that, since this as a genus of cognitions of reason had succeeded in this, it would also apply in philosophy, without considering that mathematics in its investigations built on intuitions that could be given through the senses, namely space and time: but here, where mere concepts of reason without intuition lay at the ground, a like result could not be expected, at least not on like grounds.",["\"reußiret\" = \"succeeded\" (from French réussir). \"Untersuchngen\" is an OCR slip for Untersuchungen.","Gattung = \"genus\" (glossary); \"Fortschritte\" = \"progress\"; \"zum Grunde lagen\" = \"lay at the ground\"; Grund = \"ground\".","\"Philosophie\" here rendered \"philosophy\" (Weltweisheit would be \"philosophy\" too)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-004":["Plato especially contributed preeminently to this error by attributing to the pure concepts of reason a power of intuition, just as to the sensible ones a faculty of reception, and therefore deriving cognitions of reason here just as in mathematics through the construction of intuition a priori.",["\"abieitete\" is an OCR slip for \"ableitete\" (derived).","\"Anschauungs-Kraft\" = \"power of intuition\"; \"receptions Vermögen\" = \"faculty of reception\" (Vermögen = faculty).","\"den sinnlichen\" is ambiguous in the German (dative plural, possibly \"to the sensible [concepts]\" or \"to the senses\"); I kept \"the sensible ones\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-005":["As long as the systems built upon this were not challenged, one could continue building, but still did not become certain of the truth of its presuppositions and consequences, because it was impossible to test on objects the cognitions brought forth through the attempts, since the latter could not be given, and thus a comparison with them did not take place. All judgments and every whole system were valid, if one only remained consistent and did not contradict oneself. But there arose a conflict of the philosophers among one another over the asserted propositions as consequences of their systems, in that one part believed to prove clearly that they were grounded, and the other part just as clearly refuted them, and showed that the opposite could be grounded just as clearly. Namely, this dispute concerned the transcendental propositions or overreaching problems in philosophy, e.g. one proved: every space and the bodies consisted of the simple parts of matter — others denied just as clearly the impossibility of the simple parts, because all *composita* consisted of parts and these again of parts — the proposition: the world has a beginning, for the absolute world-whole is only an idea of reason, but the world itself is bounded — others denied this and asserted: there can be no last boundary of the world, only we could not determine the boundary — etc.",["überschwenglich is rendered \"overreaching\" to keep it distinct from transcendent(al) (transcendent, transcendental), which appears in the same passages; the glossary does not list it.","\"seiner Voraussetzungen und Folgerungen\": the singular \"its\" is kept as in the German (the antecedent is not made explicit). Folgerung = consequence; Widerspruch = contradiction, Widerstreit = conflict; Gränze = boundary; Aufgabe = problem; Satz = proposition.","\"die behauptete Satze\" is as printed (number disagreement); rendered \"the asserted propositions\". \"zE.\" = zum Exempel, rendered \"e.g.\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-006":["As soon as, therefore, the contradiction and the existence of entirely conflicting propositions was equally clear, there arose the party that doubted of the certainty of both; this took occasion from this to declare all truths of reason uncertain, and adopted as principle that certainty is lacking to us in all our cognitions: it even contradicted itself, and admitted that even the question whether everything were uncertain was itself uncertain. This now killed all progress of the investigation, in that dogmatism was overthrown, and skepticism laid down no *principia* from which one could proceed. The interest of human beings suffered under this and both *opposita* rendered no use.",["OCR \"ander Gewisheit\" read as \"an der Gewißheit\"; \"zweifelte an der Gewißheit von beyden\" kept literal (\"doubted of the certainty of both\").","statuirte rendered \"laid down\" (not in glossary; not setzen = posit). Gewißheit = certainty; Fortschritte = progress; Nutzen = use (a different German word from Gebrauch)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-007":["It was necessary, and one should have, before one had undertaken investigations about the supersensible objects themselves, begun with this:",["übersinnlich = supersensible. The sentence is as it stands (\"Es war nöthig, und man hätte …\"); the continuation is in the next passage."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-008":["to treat reason, as the investigating subject, like an object that would have to be explored more closely, to investigate its faculty to cognize *a priori*, and to determine its boundary and domain. Up to the closer critique of pure reason one can now classify the *principia* that have been dominant, on which one built, according to their genus and shape thus:",["Vermögen = faculty; Gränze = boundary; Umfang = domain; Gattung = genus; Gestalt = shape (not in glossary). The infinitive clause continues the previous passage."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-009":["a.) Aristotle and later Locke have set up a (so-called) physiology of reason, in that they regarded cognition *a priori* as one that could be acquired empirically; and what we raised from the determinations of things drawn from experience, through making-universal, to a cognition *a priori*. They assumed, therefore, with regard to origin, all cognition as sensible (*nihil est* etc., says Aristotle) and believed that the more the proposition was brought up high through abstraction, the more nearly it approached a proposition *a priori*. Aristotle and Locke therefore assumed the *systema influens physici* or the origin of our representations from the senses and their influence, as well as the principle that we attained to the general cognitions *a priori* only through abstraction. The contradiction is clear. Reason is supposed to supply the cognition and yet it is supposed to have been acquired through the senses.",["OCR \"sinnlieh\" corrected to \"sinnlich\"; \"Erkenn tniß\" rejoined. \"je mehr nähere er sich\" is as printed (garbled); rendered \"the more nearly it approached\".","\"und was wir … erhoben\" is syntactically incomplete in the German and is left so. \"so empirisch erworben werden könne\": \"so\" is read as a relative (\"as one that\").","Verallgemeinmachung rendered \"making-universal\" (literal compound); Bestimmung = determination; Satz = proposition; Erkenntniß = cognition."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-010":["b.) Plato and later Leibnitz seem to assume a *systema harmoniae praestabilitae* with regard to cognitions of reason. They namely laid innate ideas at the ground, which would be placed in us before we were acquainted with the objects themselves, and which agreed with the objects precisely because the author had placed them in us. Plato now assumed, as source of acquisition of all pure ideas of reason, that they descended from the intuition of God; this Leibnitz altered to the effect that he assumed certain inborn predispositions of reason as existing in us, which had only the use that we, in trust in them, found the objects themselves agreeing with these ideas.",["bekannt sein = \"be acquainted with\" (kennen/Kenntnis group); Anschauung = intuition; Anlagen = predispositions (not in glossary); Gebrauch = use; Urheber = author.","\"Erwerb Quelle\" (OCR/print: two words) rendered \"source of acquisition\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-011":["Both found themselves, in order to establish such hypotheses, surprised, by the conviction that often truths forced upon them so evidently.",["Awkward German kept: \"überrascht, durch die Überzeugung, die oft Wahrheiten ihnen so evident aufdrangen\"; \"die\" could be taken as a relative pronoun with \"Überzeugung\" as antecedent (\"the conviction which truths often forced upon them\") or otherwise; the English leaves it open. Überzeugung = conviction."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-012":["c.) Wolf, and in his old age, his Antigon, Crusius. Crusius indeed contested such a united effect of the soul on the body as Leibnitz had assumed by virtue of the *harmonia praestabilita*, laid down on the contrary, however, that the criterion of truth is to be sought only in the ideas that the creator had placed in us for the reason that he could not have trusted our reason that it would find these ideas itself; he assumed therefore an inner revelation in human beings, and with it the necessity of it, in order to bring him to conviction. Wolf, whom he refuted, had in itself not at all investigated the origin of the ideas, but acted as a dogmatist. He built his success in philosophy on mathematical presuppositions at random, and by application of the mathematical method confounded the cognitions *a priori ex puris ideis* with the mathematical cognitions, in that he believed he could operate with them through the construction of concepts from intuition *a priori* just as in mathematics. 1st part of transcendental philosophy, or metaphysics, which contains ontology.",["\"sein Antigon\" kept as printed (\"his Antigon\"); \"in deßen Alter\" is ambiguous as to whose old age; \"ihn\" (\"to bring him to conviction\") has no clear referent and is left as \"him\".","\"an sich\" in \"hatte an sich den Ursprung … nicht untersucht\" is rendered by the glossary's \"in itself\"; its attachment is uncertain (possibly \"by himself\"). \"aufs gerathe wohl\" = aufs Geratewohl, \"at random\".","The last sentence (\"1. Theil der transcendental Philosophie …\") is a heading that runs on in the OCR text; translated as it stands. Noth Wendigkeit = Notwendigkeit, necessity; Überzeugung = conviction; Anschauung = intuition."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-013":["7.) It is striking to think representations and concepts *a priori* whose object itself cannot be given through possible intuition. E.g. a spirit is an idea of pure reason, whose object absolutely cannot be exhibited: it is indeed true that this concept and all similar to it, taken theoretically, are empty, yet the idea is nevertheless possible, and is qualified to render practical use: but these ideas, which are so overreaching, and the elementary principles through which we cognize these representations *a priori*, of supersensible kind, but of which no object can be exhibited in intuition, do not belong here either, but the subject matter of ontology, or of transcendental philosophy, insofar as it is treated here, consists only in those elementary principles of such concepts *a priori* in which the objects themselves can be exhibited, or cognized. They are indeed also called transcendent, but they are at the same time immanent; precisely because the objects of the cognitions are possible.",["The numeral \"7.)\" is as read from the OCR (original sign uncertain). Geist = spirit (the glossary also gives mind); darstellen = exhibit; Vorwurf = subject matter (not in glossary); Art = kind; überschwenglich = \"overreaching\", distinct from transcendent.","\"aber diese Ideen, so überschwengliche sind, …\" is syntactically irregular in the German (\"so\" as relative); kept irregular as far as English allows. Gegenstand and Object are both \"object\"; here \"Object(e)\" occurs in the second sentence and \"Gegenstand\" in the first."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-014":["8.) *ad Sect* 1. §7 — 11 *auctoris*",["Latin abbreviations kept in Latin; the numerals are as printed."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-015":["Of an object as supreme concept. The supreme concept, under which all remaining elementary concepts can be subordinated, is the",["\"Von einem Gegenstände als obersten Begriff\" is a marginal heading that runs into the sentence; the full stop after it is added only to mark the break (the German has none). Gegenstand = object."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-016":["concept of an object in general, which lies at the ground of the representation. The author, on the contrary, holds the concept of possibility to be the first concept, and opposes to it at the same time the concept of nothing. The representation however",["Object/Gegenstand: \"Gegenstand\" in the previous passage and the verb \"zum Grunde liegt\" kept literal. The sentence breaks off at \"Die Vorstellung aber\" and continues in the following fragments."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-017":["— of the *possibili impossibili*",["Fragment as printed (apparently one of several items of a list that is part of the sentence continued in the next passages); the Latin forms are kept unaltered."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-018":["— a — *a non-a*",["The \"ein\" before the dash is left as printed; the OCR spacing of \"a non - a\" is normalized."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-019":["— a something nothing evidently indicates a division, in which the members must be grounded on a higher concept, and this is the concept of object in general, which can then be a something, or a nothing. It seems striking to think an object that includes a nothing: but a nothing also presupposes only a thought that cancels itself again, (i.e. that contradicts itself) and therefore never has an existing object at its ground.",["\"ein etwas nichts\" kept as a bare juxtaposition (\"a something nothing\"). Eintheilung = division; Glieder = members; Widerspruch = contradiction; Object = object; existirend = existing."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-020":["The nothing is now either",["Breaks off; the enumeration follows in the next passage."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-021":["1.) a *nihilum logice tale s. logicum s. negativum*, or one to which no thought or representation at all corresponds. It is ordinarily constituted so that it *involves* an inner contradiction in the representation.",["\"s.\" = *sive* kept as the abbreviation in the Latin phrase. \"Gedancke\" (print/OCR) read as \"Gedanke\". \"involvirt\" rendered as the Latin-derived *involves* (italicized as a Latin-derived technical word). Vorstellung = representation."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-022":["2.) a *nihilum privativum* is one to which, although it can be thought, nevertheless nothing existing corresponds; e.g., the aether in physics is a feigned concept, which has no reality *intuitu objecti*, but which can be thought without contradiction. Likewise a positive cold, as absolute cause of cold, contains nothing contradictory in thinking it; but has no existence. All fictions belong here, since they are not found in nature *quoad objectum*, e.g., complete contentment, against which the existence speaks of the constant propensity of the human being to raise himself above his state, and to transfer himself into another, which he holds to be better. Thus *nihil negativum et privativum* differ from one another as thought and thing: the one or the other does not correspond to the representation. Or",["'Begriff' = concept; 'Vorstellung' = representation; 'Widerspruch' = contradiction; 'Zustand' = state; 'existenz' rendered 'existence' (Existenz/Dasein both = 'existence' in the glossary).","'wider die die existenz spricht der beständige Hang' is syntactically awkward in the transcript; kept as it stands, with 'the existence' and 'the constant propensity' both able to be read as the subject of 'speaks'.","'Erdichtungen' = fictions; 'fingirter' = feigned. 'eins oder das andere correspondiren' has a singular subject with a plural verb; kept. The passage ends with 'Oder' and breaks off.","'Hang' = propensity (not in the glossary)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-023":["3.) A nothing is also a *conceptus inanis*, empty concept, i.e., a concept to which no object can correspond or (more properly) = to which one can give no object in intuition. One must guard against calling such a concept false on that account, one cannot become conscious whether a real object can correspond to the empty concept, but on that account it can nevertheless be thought, i.e., it does not contradict the possibility of the mere representation of it: between that which can be thought and that of which the object can at the same time be presented, i.e., given, there is (according to 1 and 2) a difference. Concepts of this kind occur among the supersensible ideas. E.g., the concept of spirit has nothing contradictory in the representation, but whether it is possible that such an immaterial being could exist, that one cannot see. With problematic propositions nothing of contradiction is to be found, but thereby one does not settle whether the concept can receive a corresponding object in intuition, whether a case can be placed under it, it indicated in existence, and applied. Such *conceptus inanes* are not usable, but on that account not false.",["'Gegenstand' and 'Object/object' both = 'object' (Gegenstand occurs once, 'correspondirenden Gegenstand').","'Anschauung' = intuition; 'Geist' = spirit; 'Wesen' = being; 'Satz' = proposition; 'Ideen' = ideas.","OCR corrected silently: 'ei' to 'er', 'Widerspruc' to 'Widerspruch'.","'dem man kein Object ... geben kann' follows 'oder (eigentlicher) ='; the sign '=' is kept as in the text.","'ob ihm ein Fall untergelegt, er in der existenz angedeutet, und angewandt werden könne': the subjects of the participles are left as in German ('it' = the concept, in the second and third members)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-024":["4.) A merely formal concept is the nothing, insofar as the lack of everything material in intuition is found with it. It is thus only a *nihilum in sensu materiali*, but certainly a something *in sensu formali*, e.g., the concept of space in itself is according to the form a concept that can be cognized in intuition, but *in materiali* it has no intuition, so long as it is thought without thing or matter; and is relatively therefore a nothing. From this it follows that all concepts belong here which lack all empirical intuition. For empirical intuiting rests essentially on sensation of the object, and consequently empirical intuition alone gives only empirical concepts.",["'Raum' = space; 'an sich' = in itself; 'Empfindung' = sensation; 'erkannt' = cognized; 'Ding' = thing; 'Materie' = matter.","'Anschauen' (verbal noun) rendered 'intuiting' to keep it distinct from 'Anschauung'.","'dabei' in 'sich dabei findet' is rendered 'with it'; its referent (the concept) is left as in the German."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-025":["5.) Nothing is called impossible because on it no concept at all, not even a thought, rests. This impossible nothing one cognizes merely by contradiction. — A thought that cancels itself, a concept that falls apart through inner contradiction. — - Objects in themselves can nevertheless be impossible which one assumes to be possible, because the concept of them undergoes no contradiction: on the other hand the thought of an actual or possible object cannot be impossible, insofar as it corresponds to it.",["'an sich' = in itself; 'erkennt' = cognizes; 'wirklich' = actual; 'möglich/unmöglich' = possible/impossible.","'auf dem' is read as 'auf ihm beruht' (the word 'darauf'); 'ihm' in the last clause is left unresolved as in the German (the object).","The dashes '— - ' are kept as in the OCR text."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-026":["Hence one may well assume as correct:",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-027":["everything that contradicts itself is impossible, but not conversely deny:",["'verneinen' ('deny') stands after the colon and seems to belong to the transcript's construction ('not conversely deny'); kept as it stands, possibly corrupt or misplaced."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-028":["everything that does not contradict itself is possible, because otherwise it would have to contradict itself. Thus only the impossibility, but not the possibility, rests on contradiction.",["'Widerspruch' = contradiction; 'sich widersprechen' = 'contradict itself'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-029":["It matters indeed only that I see the contradiction and that becomes impossible which I held to be possible. Contradiction is only the means to cognize the impossibility. But possibility corresponds with the existence of the object. One can therefore very well think predicates of a thing without contradiction, but from this the possibility of the thing itself does not yet follow, because the possibility of the thought does not yet involve the possibility of the object in intuition. Hence an empty concept is also possible, so long as no inner contradiction is there, on the other hand one knows no object that corresponds to it, consequently from this there does not yet follow the possibility or impossibility of the thing itself.",["'Mittel' = means; 'erkennen' = cognize; 'kennt' = knows; 'Gegenstand/Object' = object.","'Umnöglichkeit' silently corrected to 'Unmöglichkeit'. 'Es kommt ja nur darauf an' rendered 'It matters indeed only'.","'so ihm correspondirt': 'so' read as a relative ('that'); 'ihm' left unresolved. 'involvirt' = 'involve'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-030":["6.) *ad eosd. §* — The *principium contradictionis* and the equally prevailing *principium identitatis* is the supreme principle with regard to all analytic judgments, i.e., those judgments where the concept of the predicate and subject can be cognized through dissection; it is on the other hand not sufficient for all and every kind of judgments, e.g., for synthetic judgments, where one goes beyond the concept of the subject, and says more through the predicate than was contained in the subject. The formula under which one grasps it is:",["'phos' after '§' is garbled OCR (probably a section number); left out of the translation as unreadable. 'eosd.' is an abbreviation (*eosdem*?), kept as printed.","'Principium' = principle (Latin kept); 'Grundsatz' is not used here. 'Urtheile' = judgments; 'Zerghederung' read as 'Zergliederung' = dissection (OCR).","'Art' = kind; 'zureichend' = sufficient; 'synthetisch' = synthetic; 'formul' = formula."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-031":["*nulli subjecto competit praedicatum sibimet ipsi oppositum.* One usually defines it in the manner:",["'Art' = manner; 'definirt' = define."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-032":["*quod idem simul esse et non esse potest*: i.e., to no thing can a predicate, and at the same time (*eodem tempore*) the opposite of it, belong. One easily sees that herein the determination of a relation of time, i.e., of the existence of the thing in time, is indicated by the word *simul*. According to this, then, a contradiction can prevail between subject and predicate only under equal condition of time: consequently, if 2 predicates are ascribed to the same subject not at the same time, but one after another, it is possible that, although they would contradict the subject *eodem tempore*, nevertheless each predicate alone could be ascribed to it *diverso tempore*, e.g., a learned man is not learned, herein the contradiction is clear, because both cannot be at the same time: to be learned, and unlearned, consequently the predicate and the subject cancel each other and vice versa. But the learned man can forget what he knew, and then he can well afterwards pass as unlearned. There are thus here 2 contradictory determinations in alteration of time: so also in the two concepts of the understanding: a quiet — moved human being thought at the same time contains a contradiction, but with regard to space and time not at all.",["The Latin formula is given as printed ('quod idem simul esse et non esse potest'), apparently an incomplete citation.","OCR corrected silently: 'eodern' to 'eodem', 'cont.radictorische' to 'contradictorische', 'vergeben' read as 'vergessen' (OCR), 'gewust' = 'gewusst'; 'vergeben' is doubtful and rendered as 'forget'.","'Bestimmung' = determination; 'Verhältnis' = relation; 'Verstand' = understanding; 'Veränderung' = alteration; 'Raum' = space; 'Zeit' = time; 'Bedingung' = condition.","'vv.' is read as 'vice versa'; 'passiren' rendered 'pass'. 'mithin' = 'consequently'; 'Gegentheil' = 'opposite'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-033":["This determination of concepts one calls the alterability of the thing: how this comes about is to be developed below, and here only the consequence is drawn that the determination of time does not belong to the formula of contradiction.",["'Veränderlichkeit' = alterability; 'Folge' = consequence here (the glossary gives 'sequel; sequence' for Folge, but 'die Folge ziehen' reads as 'draw the consequence'; the choice is flagged).","'Bestimmung' = determination; 'Begriff' = concept; 'Widerspruch' = contradiction; 'Formel' = formula."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-034":["NB. One must by all means let the formula *cuidam subjecto competit praedicatum praedicato sui ipsius oppositum* also hold alongside the above formula of the *principium contradictionis*. For the judgment has, besides the subject and predicate, also a word of connection. If this indicates merely the *copula*, then the above formula of the *principium contradictionis*: *nulli subjecto competit* etc., holds universally; inasmuch as the two concepts directly cancel each other. But if it also indicates existence determined in time, then 2 *opposita* can occur according to the 2nd formula, e.g., the learned Caius is unlearned is a contradiction, but not so: the Caius who is learned is unlearned; for this means: Caius exists at one time as learned, but exists at another time as unlearned. Hence it is to be regarded as a mistake that in the concept of the *principium contradictionis*: *nihil est, quod simul* etc., a concept of existence has been brought in. Besides the cited negative formula *nulli* etc., one can also determine the *principium* of analytic judgments positively: *omni subjecto competit praedicatum ipsi identicum*. One then calls it the *principium identitatis*. E.g., Every learned person has knowledge [*Kenntnisse*]: it is a contradiction to think the opposite of this, and knowledge lies directly in the concept of the learned person: it is therefore equal to the proposition: a learned person is a learned person.",["'Verbindungswort' rendered 'word of connection'; 'Verbindung' elsewhere = 'combination'.","'Urtheil' = judgment; 'Satz' = proposition; 'Begriff' = concept; 'Kenntniße' = knowledge/acquaintance (Kenntnis), distinct from Erkenntniß = cognition.","'existential Begriff' rendered 'concept of existence'; 'existirt' = 'exists' (Existenz = existence).","OCR corrected: 'compet.it' to 'competit', 'Formul' to 'Formel' (kept as 'formula'), 'Ixenntniße' to 'Kenntniße'.","'Gelehrter' rendered 'learned person' where it is a noun, 'der gelehrte Cajus' 'the learned Caius'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-035":["The author seeks, § 11, to prove this *principium identitatis* through the *principium contradictionis*, but one cannot carry out the proof for the reason that the *principium contradictionis*, just as it is supposed to prove the *principium identitatis*, can also demonstrate it [the reverse], hence proves the *principium contradictionis* through itself.",["The German is obscure: 'ebenso... auch darthun kann' has no stated object; I supplied only a bracketed '[the reverse]' minimally—reading doubtful, possibly corrupt.","'Beweis' = proof; 'beweisen' = prove."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-036":["If one denies that the predicate belongs to the subject, then the *oppositum* would belong to it, and this would not contradict, but would only prove that the *idem* was not *idem*: so long as the contradiction in and for itself had not been proved: both *principia* are indemonstrable. One can say: what contradicts itself is nothing; this is an affirming identical proposition. These are the two supreme formal *principia* of our cognitions, yet the *principium contradictionis* is used more than the *principium identitatis*. That is due to the fact that in the *principium contradictionis* lies the necessity which forces upon one the truth of that which is to be proved. The proof of the truth, by the opposite of it being impossible, is indirect, but it is apodictic and combined with the concept of necessity, in place of which the mere truth does not carry the impression with it; meanwhile the *principium identitatis* is always understood along with it.",["OCR: 'widersrpäche' read as 'widerspräche'; 'nichtsdies' read as 'nichts; dies'; 'hegt' read as 'liegt'; 'aufdrängt' kept as 'forces upon'.","'Eindruck' = impression; 'Erkenntniße' = cognitions.","'solange, bis... erwiesen wäre' is syntactically awkward in the German and is kept so; 'an und vor sich' rendered 'in and for itself'.","'darunter verstanden' rendered 'understood along with it' (literally 'understood thereunder')."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-037":["The author defines the *impossibile* through the *principium contradictionis*, but this is false for the reason that the *reciprocitas* is lacking in the concepts: for were this so, the proposition: impossible is what contradicts itself, would have to be able to be converted *per contrapositionem*, hence the proposition: what is not impossible = possible, does not contradict itself; it can, as already mentioned, well be possible in the concept, something which shows itself as impossible as soon as one has become aware of the contradiction, hence the definition is incorrect. It is only true that the *principium contradictionis* is necessary for cognizing the impossibility, but the impossibility is not thereby refuted.",["OCR: 'den bey den Begriffen' read as 'denn bei den Begriffen'; 'definition' left as in source.","'Erklärung' not present; 'definirt'/'definition' kept as cognates.","'möglich' = possible; 'Unmöglichkeit' = impossibility; 'erkennen' = cognize.","The clause 'was nicht unmöglich = möglich ist, widerspricht sich nicht' is syntactically loose in the transcript and is kept loose."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-038":["10.) *ad § 12 et 13 autoris*: *posito impoßibili oritur contradictio*. A *contradictio in adjecto* it is when in one and the same concept 2 attributed concepts run directly counter to each other, or in a judgment subject and predicate directly contradict each other. The contradiction is *apparens*, as e.g. a visible darkness, i.e., approach of light to darkness, as Milton says of hell. — Visible lunar eclipses: the negations of light. The contradiction is *patens* (*explicita*), and *latens sive implicita*, according as an analysis of the concepts is required in order to cognize it, or it does not need the dissection: yet one must, with regard to the one judging himself, assume in both cases that the contradiction was hidden from him, and it must therefore be proved to him.",["'beygelegte Begriffe' rendered 'attributed concepts'; 'Zergliederung' = dissection, 'Analyse' = analysis.","'Annäherung des Lichts an Finsterniß' kept literal.","'Urtheilenden' rendered 'the one judging'; 'fällen' read as 'Fällen' (cases)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-039":["11.) *ad § 10 autoris*: There are 3 logical *principia* of all judgments, which determine the correctness and certainty of the latter; they are logical formulas of our judgments, but not metaphysical *principia* of objective cognition. These are",["'letztem' read as 'letzteren' (the latter); 'Gewisheit' = certainty.","'objectiven Erkenntniß' rendered 'objective cognition'.","The passage ends with 'Dies sind', continued by the next passages."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-040":["1.) the *principium contradictionis et identitatis*",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-041":["2.) *principium rationis logicum*",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-042":["3.) *principium exclusi intermedii inter duo contradictoria sive principium divisionis sive principium disjunctionis*. NB Under this are understood all analytic judgments, inasmuch as predicates are presupposed which are thought in the concept of the subject, proved through the ground of its cognition, and whose necessary attribution is demonstrated. The *principium contradictionis* and *identitatis* are the two supreme and universal *principia* to which all such judgments are subject, and indeed to the latter the affirming and to the former the denying ones.",["'durch deßen Erkenntniß Grund' is awkward; rendered literally 'through the ground of its cognition' (reading of 'deßen' as referring to the subject or predicate is ambiguous and preserved).","'letztem'/'erstern' read as 'letzteren'/'ersteren'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-043":["All 3 *principia* run out with the *principium contradictionis* into one concept, inasmuch as one cannot judge without presupposing them, and the *principium contradictionis* contains, so to speak, the two other *principia*. For",["'Begriff' = concept; the phrase 'auf einen Begriff hinaus' is obscure and kept literal.","The passage breaks off at 'Denn', continued by the next passage."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-044":["a.) the *principium contradictionis* is applied in all problematic judgments. Here it matters only that the predicate in itself not contradict the subject, e.g., a divisible soul: There lies in the concept of soul an *oppositum*, which cannot unite the presupposition in the judgment or concept with the predicate divisible. If this is so, the judgment is not at all thinkable. Every thought, on the other hand, which does not fall apart in itself, is in accordance with this *principium*, since in so far it does not yet matter about the truth of it. This enters",["'an sich' = in itself; 'zerfällt' = falls apart.","'die Voraussetzung... nicht vereinigen kann' is obscure in the German (the subject of 'vereinigen' is the *oppositum*) and is kept literal.","'deßelben' (of it) is ambiguous between the thought and the judgment; kept ambiguous.","The passage breaks off at 'Diese tritt', continued by 'b.)'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-045":["b.) in assertoric judgments or *propositiones*: for every proposition must be grounded by virtue of the *principium rationis* to be applied here, which however already presupposes that it is thinkable at all, whereupon then in accordance with this 2nd *principium* the predicate must be connected with the subject by the proof-ground.",["'Satz' = proposition; 'Beweis Grund' rendered 'proof-ground' (two words in OCR, doubtful whether a compound).","'verknüpft' = connected (Verknüpfung = connection); 'begründet' = grounded.","'wonächst' rendered 'whereupon then'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-046":["c.) the *principium disjunctionis* can only make the judgment into an apodictic judgment, and give it certainty through the disjunction of the members of the concept. The latter ensues through every concept being divided into 2 *opposita*, of which only one member can belong to the subject, and it is proved that the *oppositum* cannot belong to it, just as besides both members no third can be there, which would have to be ascribed to it at the same time. This is therefore the principle of all logical division of concepts, because these are only dichotomous.",["'princip' rendered 'principle' (Grundsatz/Prinzip not distinguished); 'Eintheilung' = division.","'so ihm zugleich zugeschrieben werden müßte' kept literal; 'so' stands for 'das' or 'welches' (OCR/transcript irregularity).","'Glieder' = members."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-047":["12.) All our cognition consists of concepts, and of intuition.",["'Erkenntniß' = cognition; 'Anschauung' = intuition (*intuitus*), singular as in the source."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-048":["Intuition gives the concept objective reality, i.e. without intuition every concept falls under the suspicion that it is empty. If it is therefore indeed thought, but the object cannot be given to it in intuition, then one says of it: objective reality is lacking to it: yet it does not matter here as necessary that the object be presented sensibly, but only that its presentation be possible. Intuition gives the object to be cognized, and an immediate representation of it lies at its basis; the marks that are abstracted through intuition give mediately the representation which the concept includes, and through which the object is cognized. Through the concept the object is thought: now if I could place no object under the concept, then I can indeed think the concept, but this thinking alone yet gives no cognition, because objective reality is lacking to it; intuition must be added, so that the concept may have an object that corresponds to it, and then for the first time does it have objective reality, and can it be cognized. Thus just as little as cognition is possible without intuition, so little is it already acquired through intuition alone. For this contains only the immediate relation to the object.",["Anschauung = intuition; Begriff = concept; Erkenntniß = cognition; erkennen = cognize; Merkmal = mark; Vorstellung = representation; Realitaet = reality.","'Darstellung' rendered 'presentation' (not in the glossary); 'dargestellt' = 'presented'.","'Object' (roman type in the original) and 'Gegenstand' (later passages) both rendered 'object'.","'einschheßt' is an OCR slip, read as 'einschließt'; 'noth wendig' read as 'nothwendig'.","'könnte ich nun kein object dem Begriff unterlegen' is kept in the German subjunctive-then-indicative (könnte ... so kann) and may be a slip for 'könnte ich ... so könnte'; the English preserves the 'if I could ... then I can'.","'bey ihr liegt ... zum Grunde': 'ihr' taken as 'Anschauung'; 'diese' in the last sentence is likewise feminine, 'intuition'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-049":["In cognition, finally, no relation to the object is immediately present; but only to the intuition of the object, and in the relation of the concept to the intuition cognition is acquired. All intuition is sensible, and to cognize something without it would be, if it were possible, supersensible cognition, which would also have to rest on supersensible intuition: all supersensible concepts are of such a kind that no intuition can be given to them, so that, even if one may otherwise well have ground to think them, objective reality is nevertheless lacking to them, which would be directly combined with the concept, e.g. the concept of spirit.",["Erkenntniß = cognition; erkennen = cognize; Anschauung = intuition; Grund = ground; Geist = spirit (the glossary also allows 'mind').","'übersinnlich' rendered 'supersensible'.","'wäre ... übersinnliches Erkenntniß, so auch auf ... beruhen müßte': the 'so' is ambiguous between 'which' (relative to Erkenntniß) and a correlative 'so'; the English keeps 'which would also have to rest'.","'die directe mit dem Begriff verbunden wäre': 'die' could refer to 'Realitaet' or, less likely, to something else; 'directe' kept as 'directly' modifying 'verbunden'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-050":["13.) In order now to set up the *principia* of transcendental philosophy itself, what matters first of all is the development of the difference between analytic and synthetic judgments. 1. Analytic judgments are those in which the *praedicat*, thought as a subject, can be cognized through the dissection of the concept. The *praedicat* thus lies *implicite* in the *subiect* or is contained in the *subiect*; the concept of the *subject* need only be analyzed, i.e. *explicirt*, in order to cognize it, e.g. A body is divisible, is cognized through the dissection of extensibility. Analytic judgments must therefore be able to be thought and cognized solely in the concept of the subject; in this they already distinguish themselves essentially from the synthetic. To cognize the latter judgments through the subject alone, without adding a concept not lying in it, is impossible. E.g. all mathematical propositions of every kind are synthetic; it is impossible, even in algebra, to develop even a single proposition through analysis.",["Urtheil = judgment; Begriff = concept; Zergliederung = dissection; erkennen = cognize; Satz = proposition (in 'Der Satz' in later passages also 'proposition').","Latin/roman-type words kept in italics: *principia*, *praedicat*, *implicite*, *subiect*, *subject*, *explicirt* (the last a Latinized German form, 'explicated').","'als ein subiect gedacht' is obscure and kept literally; 'es' in 'um es zu erkennen' could refer to the predicate or to the judgment.","'Dehnbarkeit' rendered 'extensibility' (cf. Ausdehnung = extension); the example ('theilbar ... durch die Zergliederung der Dehnbarkeit') is kept as it stands.","'Letztere Urtheile zu erkennen, durch das subject allein' is awkward; 'entwicklen' read as 'entwickeln'. 'subject s' read as 'subjects'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-051":["The proposition: all bodies are heavy, will not result from the analysis of the concept of body alone, I must first have cognized through experience that it is heavy, before I can ascribe this *praedicat* to the body.",["Satz = proposition; Erfahrung = experience; analyse = analysis; Praedicat kept in italics as Latin.","'er' in 'daß er schwer sey' refers back to 'Körper' (masculine); the English 'it' keeps this open."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-052":["The proposition: 7 + 5 gives the magnitude of 12, is not possible to develop other than through development of the concept of the magnitude 7+5 alone. The magnitude 12 arises, however, from the two others *per synthesin* very easily, and is therefore a synthetic proposition: namely I have carried out the operation, in order to find 12, with the two magnitudes through experience in such a way that to one magnitude, e.g. 7, I have combined the other (5) by sensible adding-on of the units and thus added it.",["Größe = magnitude; Einheit = unity (here plural, 'units'); Verbindung/verbunden = combination/combined; sinnlich = sensible.","'ist nicht zu entwickeln möglich, wie durch Entwicklung ... allein' is obscure as printed (the 'wie' is preserved as 'other than'; the sense of the comparison is doubtful, possibly corrupt).","'7 -f- 5' read as '7 + 5' (OCR).","'Zuzählung' rendered 'adding-on'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-053":["2. Analytic judgments one can call judgments of clarification. The judgment serves for the consciousness of the manifold that lies in the concept, and was previously thought obscurely, but is now developed. By contrast synthetic judgments = judgments of extension. They go beyond the concept of the subject and contain yet a *praedicat* that has not been thought in the concept of the subject. It now carries itself",["Erläuterungsurtheile = judgments of clarification (Erläuterung = clarification); Erweiterungsurtheile = 'judgments of extension' (Erweiterung not in the glossary; Ausdehnung = extension is a different word).","'Erläutern ngsurt heile' is an OCR break, read as 'Erläuterungsurtheile'.","'dunckel' = 'obscurely' (the glossary gives deutlich = distinct; 'dunkel' not listed).","The passage breaks off at 'Es trägt sich nun'; the idiom ('it now comes about / the question arises') is rendered literally and left incomplete, the continuation being in the next passage."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-054":["3.) how and which of the two kinds of judgments are possible *a priori*? The possibility of cognitions *a priori* is clear in analytic judgments, since here the subject need only be developed according to the concept in order to cognize the predicate. No experience is therefore needed for this, in order to cognize the predicate contained in it. E.g. in order to cognize the divisibility of the body, only the development of the concept of body is necessary, thus one finds its divisibility, without first having to make trials concerning it *a posteriori* and to seek out the product investigated from experience.",["Erkenntniße = cognitions; Urtheilsarten = 'kinds of judgments' (Art = kind).","'wie und welche von beyden Urtheilsarten sind a priori möglich' is kept in German word order, which makes the question's grammar loose; the numeral '3.)' continues from 'Es trägt sich nun' in the previous passage.","'dessen' (its) refers to the body (or the concept of body); 'in ihm' is ambiguous between subject and concept; both kept as 'it'.","'findt' read as 'findet'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-055":["On the other hand synthetic judgments can be judgments *a posteriori*, they can also be judgments, cognized *a priori*. But even *a priori* reason must always add something that did not lie in the concept, and this cannot be grounded on experience. If the synthetic judgment is therefore to be cognized *a priori*, this can only happen through an intuition *a priori*, which is laid at the basis, namely by an intuition being placed under the concept that is to be extended *per synthesin*. This happens either through empirical intuition of the object, which is added to the concept, or through intuition *a priori*, i.e. formal relation to the object and determination of the form in which the subject is affected by the object, and from this intuition the added object is taken on. From this it follows that synthetic judgments are possible only by means of the corresponding intuition in general and the concept formed from it and added, and that this intuition must happen *a priori* if the judgment is to be posited *a priori*.",["The initial '-^agegen' is OCR, read as 'Dagegen'.","'auch können sie Urtheile seyn, a priori erkannt' is elliptical (probably 'a priori erkannte' or 'die a priori erkannt werden'); kept literally. 'Aber auch a priori muß immer Vernunft...': 'a priori' could go with 'auch' or with 'hinzuthun'.","Anschauung = intuition; Bestimmung = determination; Gegenstand and Object both 'object' (here 'Gegenstand' in 'der zugesetzte Gegenstand'; elsewhere 'Object').","'afficirt' = 'affected'; 'gestellt' (a priori gestellt) rendered 'posited' (setzen in glossary; 'stellen' is a different word).","'Diesgeschieht' read as 'Dies geschieht'; 'Object' is in roman type in the original."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-056":["E.g. the 3 angles of the triangle are equal to 2 right angles of 180°, is a synthetic judgment: for from the analysis of a figure enclosed in 3 lines this proposition cannot be brought out, but at least in thought trials must be made to find it, and thereby the proof thought. An intuition *a priori* is however different from empirical intuition, as form and matter, i.e. namely, if not merely the form but also the object of sensation lies at the basis of the intuition, then the intuition is empirical, but if in the intuition the matter, i.e. the object of the intuition, is lacking, and only the form is present, and the object possible, then the intuition is pure. E.g. space in itself is nothing felt, nothing sensible, is therefore merely the form that is intuited: on the other hand something in space that offers resistance, or a filled space, contains something that can be sensibly sensed, and therefore contains a representation of the sense of something existing. A synthetic judgment, therefore, whose corresponding intuition is an intuition *a priori*, has a pure intuition, without being accompanied by the object of the senses.",["Winkel = angle; Beweis = proof; Materie = matter; Form = form; rein = pure; Empfindung = sensation; Raum an sich = space in itself; Sinn = sense (n.), pl. Sinne = senses; Vorstellung = representation; existirend = existing (Existenz/Dasein both 'existence').","'nichts fühlbares' rendered 'nothing felt' for 'fühlbar' (cf. Gefühl = feeling); 'sinnlich empfunden werden kann' = 'can be sensibly sensed'.","'Object' and 'Gegenstand' both 'object'; 'Object der Empfindung' read as in the text.","'Vorstellung des Sinnes' could be 'of the sense' or 'of sense'; singular kept. 'sein' for 'seyn' (as printed 'zu sein')."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-057":["14. One can assume in advance that 1. all propositions of experience are synthetic judgments, and must be cognized *per synthesin*. For insofar as the judgment is already grounded through analysis, the judgment lay in the concept of the subject, and no extension of cognition and of the ground of cognition was therefore needed in order to be able to ascribe the predicate to it. But if the judgment is grounded on experience, then the predicate can be cognized only through the latter, and hence be ascribed to the subject *per synthesin*.",["Erfahrungssätze = propositions of experience; Erkenntniß = cognition; Erkenntniß-Grund = ground of cognition (Grund = ground).","'Erkenn tniß' is an OCR break, read as 'Erkenntniß'.","'ihm' in 'um ihm das praedicat beylegen zu können' refers to the subject or the concept; left as 'it'. 'letztere' = 'the latter' (experience).","The '1.' announces an enumeration whose continuation is not in the passage."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-058":["2) that there are, however, propositions *a priori*, which are synthetic judgments *a priori*, and hence are cognized independently of all experience. How this is possible, *vide infra*. — Upon the latter now rests the science that one calls metaphysics: it is namely that science or system which sets forth all principles of all possible synthetic judgments *a priori*. Transcendental philosophy contains the highest principles of this kind, and that science which determines the boundaries of the possibility of all synthetic cognitions *a priori*, i.e. not through extension but limitation of the faculty of cognition to the principles lying in it, = is the critique of pure reason.",["Satz = proposition; Urtheile = judgments; Erkenntniß = cognition; Erkenntnißvermögen = faculty of cognition; Wissenschaft = science; Vernunft = reason; rein = pure.","'Gränzen' rendered 'boundaries' (Grenze); 'principien' / 'Principien' rendered 'principles'; 'Philosophie' (not Weltweisheit) rendered 'philosophy'.","'Auf letzteren' / 'the latter': the referent is left as ambiguous as in the German.","The sign '=' before 'ist die Critic' is kept as in the text; the syntax of 'und diejenige Wissenschaft ... = ist die Critic' is awkward and has been left so.","'a priori', 'vide infra' are Latin and are italicized."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-059":["15.) Only through pure form of sensibility are pure intuitions = through the latter only extension of our cognition by means of synthetic judgments *a priori* possible, and thus extensions of our cognitions *a priori* in another way do not take place, hence not at all through supersensible intuitions. These propositions are deduced in the following way: 1) What representation is in itself is inexplicable. A definition therefore cannot be given of it, because a representation can be explained only and not otherwise than by representing to oneself a representation again, hence there is a lack of cognitive grounds *in sensu logico*. This action of the mind can be described as something in me that relates itself to something else. This relation of this something other in me is now representation taken *subjective*. The representation goes partly to the object to which I relate myself, partly to that action of the mind through which I compare something in me with the object. Then one occupies oneself with the object in itself and its constitution, which must be wholly distinguished from the way of representing of the subject, as which involves the 2nd, the action of the mind. This latter is called consciousness or the representation of myself, insofar as I present to myself the representation of my representation. One is however not always conscious of the representation, but can nevertheless at any time become conscious of it. Consciousness is also called the *apperception* that accompanies the represented object. 2) The representation as source of cognition can now be effected only through 2 sources, namely through intuition and through concepts.",["Anschauung = intuition; Vorstellung = representation; Gemüth = mind; Gegenstand and Object both 'object' (Latinate 'Object' in 'auf's Object', 'Object an sich').","'Erkenntniß Gründen' = 'cognitive grounds' (literally 'grounds of cognition'); 'Erkenntniß Quelle' = 'source of cognition'. 'Bewußtsein' = consciousness.","'Eine Definition' rendered 'definition' (Erklärung elsewhere would be 'explanation/definition'); 'unerklärbar' = 'inexplicable', 'erklären' = 'explain'.","'Dann beschäftigt man sich...' and 'als welches das 2te ... involvirt' are obscure; kept literal ('as which involves the 2nd').","'vermöge' = 'by means of'; 'Etwas Andern' = 'something other'. OCR 'durchAnschauung' silently corrected to 'durch Anschauung'. 'Beschreiben' = 'describe'.","'Dieser Sätze werden ... deducirt': 'deduced'; 'subjective genommen' kept as Latin-form adverb in italics.","'Diese Beziehung von diesem Etwas Andern in mir' is ambiguous (genitive/dative sense of 'von'); preserved as 'of'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-060":["Both have in themselves a relation to an object, only with the difference that in concepts the object is merely thought, in intuition, however, it is given.",["Gegenstand = object; Begriff = concept; Anschauung = intuition. 'derselbe' refers to the object."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-061":["Intuition is namely an immediate representation of an object, i.e. it contains only one object; for were several present at once, they could be represented only in common through the mark, i.e. thought through concepts. This object can therefore be only singular. E.g. a sun (for several suns would lie under the concept of a self-illuminated body).",["Merkmal = mark; 'einzeln' rendered 'singular'. 'Z.E.' = 'E.g.' ('zum Exempel'). 'läge' (singular subjunctive) kept as 'would lie'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-062":["Concept is mediate intuition or presentation of an object by means of the mark common to several objects as ground of cognition. It therefore always has a representation as its ground that is common to several objects, and the cognizing through the same, as mark, belongs merely to the thinking of the object. Both ways of representations thus differ in this, that in intuition the object can be represented insofar as it is given, but through the concept the object is representable insofar as it can be thought mediately as given. The concept therefore has only reality insofar as an object can be put under it. 3) All human intuition is sensible. But concepts belong merely to the thinking understanding, and sensibility can be mixed in with them only insofar as they rest on intuition, otherwise they can in and for themselves have no sensibility through which they are perceived.",["Gegenstand and Object both 'object'. 'Darstellung' = 'presentation'; 'Erkenntniß Grundes' = 'ground of cognition'; 'Erkennen' = 'cognizing'; 'wahrgenommen' = 'perceived' (Wahrnehmung = perception).","'Realitaet' = reality. 'kerne' in the OCR read as 'keine' ('no').","'Beyde Arten von Vorstellungen' rendered 'Both ways of representations' (Art = way); 'untergelegt' rendered 'put under'.","'vorgestellt' / 'vorstellbar' / 'gedacht' kept as 'represented' / 'representable' / 'thought'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-063":["Sensible is representing insofar as the object is represented by the subject as appearance.",["Erscheinung = appearance; 'Vorstellen' = 'representing' (nominalized infinitive)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-064":["Intellectual, on the other hand, is representing insofar as the object is considered through the representation as it is in itself.",["'betrachtet' = 'considered' (Betrachtung = consideration); 'an sich' = 'in itself'. 'so wie es an sich ist' could attach to 'object' or to the manner of consideration; left as in the German."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-065":["This is to be understood so: The faculty of the mind to be able to receive a representation by the latter's being affected by objects is sensibility. If now an effect arises from the object on the receptivity (i.e. the noted faculty of sensibility), the sensation of the object arises, and if this effects a relation to the object, or representation, the intuition of the object arises, which now, without further closer determination, is called appearance.",["Vermögen = faculty; Wirkung = effect; Empfindung = sensation; Bestimmung = determination; 'receptivitaet' retained as 'receptivity'; 'afficirt' = 'affected'.","'dadurch, daß letzteres ... afficirt wird': 'letzteres' (the latter) is ambiguous between 'mind' and 'faculty'; rendered 'the latter'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-066":["NB Appearance can contain truth, and therefore is distinguished from illusion: i.e. the error from confusion of the thing itself with the mere appearance, or the taking to be true from subjective representation, which one takes for certain as objective.",["Schein = illusion; Fürwahrhalten ('für wahr halten') = 'taking to be true'; 'unterscheidt' read as 'unterscheidet'.","'d. i.' introduces an appositive whose grammatical relation to 'Schein' is loose; left so. 'gewiß' rendered 'for certain'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-067":["It is therefore clear that all intuitions represent the object in appearance, i.e. the intuition is taken on the part of the subject as the way yields in which it has been affected by the object. If now the object is considered merely as *phaenomenon*, i.e. if the representation is merely sensible, one looks only to that which in our representation of the object is merely subjective, and this is the way in which we are affected by the object: in the way the object also appears to us: it is clear, however, that from the way the object appears, and hence how the subject is affected by it, it cannot be concluded that the object in itself is also so; it is rather certain that it is not so in itself, since sensibility can take regard only of the form of the object in appearance. On the other hand there is no intellectual intuition, the understanding can only think, it does not have the source of sensibility or receptivity, but only the capacity to represent through concepts, or the spontaneity, through which it cognizes, and this genus of representations, which go to objects in themselves, are called *noumena*. Accordingly, therefore, is",["Gattung = genus; Fähigkeit = capacity; Art = way; Erscheinung = appearance; Verstand = understanding; Quelle = source; 'Gegenstand' and 'Object' both 'object'.","'so angenommen, als die Art ergiebt, wie es ... afficirt worden' is obscure/possibly corrupt; kept literal ('as the way yields in which it has been affected').","'in der Art erscheint uns auch das Object' rendered literally after the colon; 'Rücksicht nehmen' = 'take regard'. 'schließen' = 'conclude'.","'spontaneitaet' = 'spontaneity'; 'erkennt' = 'cognizes'. Passage breaks off with 'Hiernach ist also' as in the text."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-068":["4) The form of the subject, to be affected in a certain way by the object, the subjective in the representation, through which we are entitled to term the object appearance, and the form of sensibility, to be affected by the object, is *phaenomenon*, in that the way in which the subject is affected nevertheless rests on the form of this faculty (receptivity), and thereby intuits the object as matter. That these can be different one sees already from the difference of the instruments of the outer senses. E.g. The fly has an eye that is a *polyedron* (many-sided), the human a wholly different eye. The human seems to see simply, and yet perceives the object doubly. A jaundiced person sees differently from a healthy eye.",["Form = form; Materie = matter; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Vermögen = faculty; 'benennen' = 'term' (Benennung = term); 'einfach' = 'simply'; 'wahrnehmen' = 'perceive'.","The syntax of the first sentence is irregular (two subjects, one verb 'ist'); kept as in the German. 'Gegenstand' / 'Object' both 'object'.","'dadurch ... anschauet': the subject of 'intuits' is ambiguous (the faculty, the form, or the subject); preserved. 'diese' ('these') has no clear antecedent.","'gelbsüchtiger' rendered 'jaundiced person'; 'ein gesundes Auge' = 'a healthy eye'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-069":["5) If we are now to have pure intuition *a priori*, the particular form of sensibility must lie at the basis of it, which we assume in the appearance of the object. It is supposed, however, to be *a priori*, hence it cannot be determined through the appearance itself. But it is also certain that in our inner sense there lies a particular form of sensibility, which gives the condition through which the objects of appearance can be cognized by us; this is the pure form of sensibility, which precedes all appearances of things, and so, before the objects are cognized through intuition, is present. It is necessary, in order to cognize the objects.",["OCR 'borm' / 'b orm' silently corrected to 'Form'; 'dei Dinge' read as 'der Dinge'.","Grunde = basis ('zum Grunde liegen' = 'lie at the basis'); Bedingung = condition; 'bestimmt' = 'determined'; 'erkannt' = 'cognized'; 'besondere' = 'particular'; 'innern Sinn' = 'inner sense'.","'Sie' in 'Sie soll aber a priori seyn' and the referents of 'it' in 'at the basis of it' are left as in the German; 'Sie ist nothwendig' likewise (form or intuition).","'vorhanden' rendered 'present'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-070":["6) Extensions of our cognitions a priori are possible only through synthetic judgments a priori. — The possibility of synthetic judgments a posteriori suffers no difficulty at all; all synthetic judgments of this kind rest on experience, experiences on appearances, hence on intuition. Thus a concept that is to be extended can be extended only through an appearance connected with it. E.g. the concept of body with the intuition and the experience of weight abstracted from it: but how can one add to a concept a priori, without any experience, more than was contained in it? and to this there is no other answer about the possibility than that it must happen by virtue of the pure form of sensibility, and that only through this form of sensible intuition can synthetic judgments a priori be determined. The possibility of these is shown by the actuality of all propositions of mathematics. E.g. *cordarum maxima est diameter*. The judgment cannot be analyzed out of the concept of the diameter, but only through the addition of a new appearance. Thus something must be added *per synthesin* that did not lie in the concept, and this does not depend on experience, but on the form that is given to intuition.",["Erweiterung = extension (Ausdehnung is also 'extension' in the glossary; here Erweiterung is the German).","Möglichkeit/möglich = possibility/possible; Wirklichkeit rendered 'actuality' per glossary.","'Erfahrungen auf Erscheinungen' kept elliptical, as in the German ('experiences [rest] on appearances').","'Escheinung' read as Erscheinung and 'Z.E.' (zum Exempel) rendered 'E.g.'; 'der Anschauung gegeben wird' could also be read as 'is given to intuition' or 'is given by intuition'—the dative is kept ambiguous.","'und daher abgezogenen Erfahrung': 'abgezogen' rendered 'abstracted'; the construction 'with the intuition and the ... experience' follows the German."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-071":["But synthetic propositions a priori cannot be related to objects otherwise than as they present themselves in the appearance. For otherwise they would be intellectual, and there would be no pure intuition. They are therefore possible only in such a way that the proposition a priori contains nothing other than the object a priori, insofar as it is appearance; and this goes on only in the way that the synthetic judgments are to extend our concept, thus are to contain more than was thought in it, the concept is put into connection with pure intuition, and can be cognized by virtue of the pure form of sensibility.",["Gegenstände/Object both rendered 'object' (Gegenstände; Object/object(en) is Latinate).","The last sentence is syntactically irregular in the German ('in der Art an, daß ... der Begriff ... gesetzt wird'); the irregular construction is preserved.","'Verknüpfung' = connection; 'erkannt' = cognized."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-072":["Of these forms of sensible pure intuition there are only two, namely",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-073":["a) Space as the form for all outer intuitions,",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-074":["b) Time as the form for all inner intuition. These forms of pure intuition therefore need no object itself, but are representable a priori, and capable of determining synthetic judgments a priori.",["'a piioii' read as 'a priori' (OCR).","'Objects' (Object) and Gegenstand both 'object'.","'vorstellbar' rendered 'representable' (Vorstellung = representation)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-075":["That they are present in us is taught by consciousness, and they are cognized as pre-existing before all intuition of sensible objects, hence as necessarily and universally present. Now if synthetic judgments a priori are to be effected, this is not possible without intuition a priori (because no synthetic judgment is possible without intuition). But intuition presupposes an object, hence the faculty of sensibility lies at the basis; but this sensibility lies at the basis a priori, and hence it can be determined in advance through pure intuition how objects will appear before they have come before us, which is possible through the derived principles of the forms of pure intuition. E.g. The different properties of the The principle: every part of space is again a space etc. If now the ground of the possibility of all synthetic intuitions a priori is to be sought only in the inner form of our sensibility, then the latter can be related to an object only in the way in which it affects the subject, i.e. insofar as it appears as *phaenomenon*; from this it now follows that, since we can extend our cognitions only through synthetic judgments, but these require intuitions, all extension of cognition has as its ground phenomena, which must be cognized through the form of sensible intuition. Furthermore we therefore cannot extend our cognition a priori insofar as we consider the things in themselves: for since intuitions belong to this, these would have to be intellectual intuitions, but there are none of that kind. Hence with supersensible concepts no extension of cognition takes place.",["'a jwiori' read as 'a priori' (OCR); 'intellectuclle' read 'intellectuelle'; 'findt' read 'findet'.","'Z.E. Die verschiedenen Eigenschaften des Das princip' is broken in the source; translated as it stands, the gap not filled.","Dinge an sich selbst = 'things in themselves' (an sich = in itself).","'Vorhanden' = 'present'; 'praeexistirend' rendered 'pre-existing'.","'Gemüth' elsewhere = mind; 'Erkenntniß' = cognition, 'Erkenntnißes' for Erkenntnisses.","'letztere' may refer to the form or to the sensibility; left ambiguous as 'the latter'.","'sämtlicher synthetischer Anschauungen a priori' is as printed (the German has 'Anschauungen' where 'judgments' might be expected); not emended.","'phaenomen', 'phaenomena' kept in Latin-form italics."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-076":["16) We can therefore have intuitions only by virtue of the form of our sensibility, and objects will appear in accordance with this form, be it empirically, or a priori. The constitution of objects in the appearance therefore rests merely on the subjective form of intuition, whereby it determines the way of the same in the mind, and according to the difference of the relation, as objects are intuited by us, and affect the mind. Thus even objectively the circle line appears different, even as *oblongum* or straight line, according to the difference of the direction in which we see it. Thus the appearance of the object has nothing more in common with the objective constitution of the thing itself, and our cognition is not capable of extending itself beyond the bounds of appearance (i.e. perception and place of the same), rather we are only in a position to transfer the appearance, as it is determined through the pure form of sensibility, back onto the objects themselves and thus to apply our perception again.",["Beschaffenheit = constitution (glossary also allows 'property'). Grenzen = bounds; Ort = place.","'wodurch sie die Art derselben im Gemüth bestimmt': the antecedent of 'sie' and 'derselben' is unclear; the ambiguity is retained ('it' ... 'the same').","'die Cirkel Linie' rendered 'the circle line'; 'oblongum' kept in Latin.","'extendiren' rendered 'extend'; Wahrnehmung = perception.","'form' lower-case in the source."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-077":["17) Space and time are now the only pure forms of sensible intuition, namely space as the form of all outer sensible intuitions, and time as the form of all inner sensible intuitions: hence they go only to the subjective way of representing things a priori, i.e. they are the subjective condition for being able to cognize objects through sensible intuition as the appearance offers them. For all things in sensible intuition are posited in space and time in the representation, and thereby it becomes possible that we can represent to ourselves or intuit the objects in the appearance. They are therefore the form of sensibility, which are present in us before all experience of the object as subjective condition of intuition. They therefore cannot be things in themselves, or coexist with the objects, for were this so, we would have to cognize space and time, like the objects in and for themselves, hence through the understanding, hence cognition would be only a posteriori and extension in it possible, and could never happen a priori: we would therefore never be able to judge a priori about representations and to have insight into the necessity of the constitution of things in cognition. They are therefore in and for themselves nothing objective.",["Gegenstände/objecten/Objecte all 'object(s)'; Gegenstand is the usual German word.","'Vorstellungs Art' rendered 'way of representing'; Art = way.","'so ... als die Erscheinung sie darbiethet' kept literal.","'die ... vorhanden sind' has plural verb with singular 'Form'; retained as 'which are present'.","'darin Erweiterung möglich' ambiguous ('in it' = in cognition); kept.","'Noth Wendigkeit' read as Nothwendigkeit (OCR break); 'einsehen' rendered 'have insight into'.","'Sinnlichen' capitalized in source; 'Sinnlichkeit' = sensibility."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-078":["1. Both, space and time, are the forms of sensible intuitions, or, which is the same, they are formal sensible intuitions, i.e. they are forms of each individual subject, according to which the given object can be represented only under this form and under no other. They can also be only intuitions, and never concepts: for the immediate representation of objects, and indeed of an individual object, belongs essentially to it, and never can a mark of a concept be laid at the basis in it. —",["The initial 'I' before '1.' is an OCR stray and was omitted.","'einzeln' rendered 'individual'; 'einzelnen Objects' likewise.","'dabei'/'dazu' rendered 'in it'/'to it'; the referents are ambiguous in the German and left so.","Merkmal = mark (nota)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-079":["All spaces, all times are only parts of a single space and of one time. There is only One space and one time. Thus parts of one and the same space and time can well be thought next to one another and respectively after one another, but these parts cannot be subordinated to a general space or time to be assumed. Space and time are therefore each representations of a *res singularis* and not *communis*: if one says: the world has a beginning and an end, this can only mean: the object of the world has a time when it was not, begins to be, and will no longer be, but time itself always remains a representation unalterable in itself.",["'Theü' read as 'Theile' (Teil = part); 'Escheinung' etc. not relevant here.","'re singulär i und nicht communi' (OCR) read as 're singulari ... non communi' and rendered in the nominative as '*res singularis* ... *communis*' for English; the Latin cases are ablative/dative in the source.","'wol' rendered 'well'; 'Welt' = world.","'vor sich' rendered 'in itself'; 'respective' = 'respectively'.","'das object der Welt' kept literal."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-080":["2. Space and time are pure sensible intuitions a priori and consequently independent of all experience, for, in order to have an experience of that which is in space, this cannot be thought otherwise than that one has previously thought a space. All things can be thought as annihilated, and yet space remains. Space and time therefore do not depend on the things in space and in time as *coexistentia rerum* as Wolff believes, but are intuitions subsisting for themselves, when one has abstracted from all things. Space therefore essentially has in itself only the subjective form of outer — as time the subjective form of inner intuitions. But one could not assume them as something formal, if one holds it necessary first to abstract space and time from the things or from matter itself, in order to regard them as something objective, which outside us would have to be thought as something coexisting with the things; they would then be concepts a posteriori, instead of which they are intuitions a priori, and consequently, in order to think them, one need not first assume things to which they are supposed to give determination.",["Raum = space; Zeit = time; Anschauung = intuition; Begriff = concept; Bestimmung = determination; Materie = matter; Ding = thing; Gegenstand/Object: here 'objectiv' = objective.","'Wolf' is the transcript's spelling, rendered Wolff; 'a priori', 'a posteriori' left in Latin as printed, italics not added since the transcript does not mark them.","'äußerer — so wie die Zeit...' : the dash and the missing noun after 'äußerer' are preserved; 'Anschauungen' must be supplied from the following clause by the reader (the text as it stands is elliptical).","'diese nicht anders denken, als daß man...' is awkward in the German; 'so im Raume ist' rendered literally as 'which is in space' ('so' = 'was').","'so außer uns ... gedacht werden müßte': 'so' could relate to 'etwas Objectives' or to the preceding clause; kept ambiguous as 'which'.","'Dinge' translated 'things' throughout; 'formelles' = 'formal'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-081":["3. Properties of space and of time are of such a kind that they must be derived from no concepts but immediately from intuition. Wolff, e.g., derives space and its properties from objective concepts — he says it is *ordo simultaneorum quatenus extra se positae existunt simul*. But then it cannot be explained why space has 3 dimensions, namely length, breadth and height. They cannot be derived from experience; rather one must already have space with its 3 dimensions in thought, before one can represent the body in experience. They therefore already lie as a condition of the representation in the inner intuition; consciousness is compelled to assume them, and consequently they exist a priori as necessary, and abstracted from things, they still cannot be attributed necessarily to the things, if they did not preexist built upon this higher intuition. Thereby the order of things becomes distinct, which Wolff in his definition of space determines as obscure, when he opines that space is a confused representation of the order of things, insofar as they are found outside one another.",["Eigenschaften = properties (Beschaffenheit would also be 'property' in this glossary); Anschauung = intuition; Vorstellung = representation; Bewußtseyn = consciousness; deutlich = distinct (OCR 'deutlieh' corrected); dunkel = obscure; confus = confused.","Abmeßungen rendered 'dimensions' (not in glossary).","OCR: 'alsdenn', 'Abmeßungen . Länge' stray period/'nemlich .' removed silently.","'Sie liegen ... in der innern Anschauung': 'innern' could be read as 'inner' intuition; rendered literally.","'wenn sie nicht auf diese höhere Anschauung gebauet, präexistirten': construction obscure, kept literal; the subject of 'präexistirten' and the referent of 'sie' are not made explicit.","The Latin quotation is as printed in the transcript (grammar of 'extra se positae' as transmitted)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-082":["On the other hand there is no doubt that one can also draw concepts from the pure intuition of space and derive them thereby.",["'leidt' = 'leidet'; 'ziehen' rendered 'draw'; 'ableiten' = 'derive' as in the preceding passages."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-083":["4. From the fact now that space and time are pure intuitions a priori and not concepts abstracted from the objects, it follows that all propositions of geometry and arithmetic, or, those concerning numbers and figures, are synthetic judgments, and indeed a priori and consequently necessary and apodictically certain, which would not be the case if they could not be derived from space and time as pure intuitions. E.g. It can be found without trial that a straight line is perpendicular upon a given point.",["Satz = proposition; Urtheil = judgment; Zahl = number; synthetisch = synthetic; nothwendig = necessary.","'objecten' = 'Objecten', rendered 'objects' (Object, not Gegenstand).","'Arithmetic' left as in the transcript; 'apodictisch' rendered 'apodictically'.","The final example is as transmitted and is obscure (a line 'perpendicular upon a given point'); translated literally, with no emendation."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-084":["5. Space and time are indeed objective with regard to the objects, i.e. they represent the objects; but only as they are in the appearance, not however as they are in themselves.",["Gegenstände = objects; the OCR break 'Gegen -stände' joined silently. Erscheinung = appearance; an sich = in itself (here plural 'in themselves').","'Gegenstand' and 'Object' are both rendered 'object' (this passage Gegenstände; the preceding passage Objecten)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-085":["If one represents the thing in itself, so one considers it as a whole out of many parts; considers all parts in themselves and in their relation among one another; this happens through the understanding, which thereby forms the concept of the whole, under which the parts are comprehended. Now if space and time rested on concepts, one would absolutely have to be needing first to represent parts of space and time (and be able to represent this to oneself), before one could think space and time as a whole; the parts of them, namely individual spaces and times, are however contained as standing alongside one another in space and time, and not under it. No space, no time can be thought without thinking at the same time a far greater space or time, and thereby the representation raises itself to a single object, and this can only be grounded in intuition. Indeed, if space and time were constitutions of things in themselves, they would have to be properties of God. For space is unbounded, the duration of time is also without boundaries. Space and time are unitary; both are necessary with regard to the existence of all things. They are all-embracing in respect of all objects, which they all contain in themselves. They are eternal. Consequently, since the reality of things rests in God as the ground, both could also only be attributed to God, and in fact the hypothesis of Spinozism consisted in this, that one assumed space and time for the things in themselves and for properties of God.",["Sache = thing here (not Ding); Ding also 'thing'. Beschaffenheit = constitution; Eigenschaft = property; Grund = ground; Realität = reality; Dasein = existence.","begreifen (worunter die Theile begriffen werden) = comprehend (comprehendere is the glossary's Latin); 'enthalten' = contain; 'faßen' = contain (rendered 'contain' for 'in sich faßen').","'schlechthin nöthig haben' rendered literally; 'must absolutely be needing' is stiff but kept close to the German.","'unter ihm': singular pronoun after 'Raum und Zeit' preserved as 'it'.","'einig' rendered 'unitary' (cf. Einheit = unity); 'Gränzen/unbegränzt' = boundaries/unbounded (Grenze = boundary).","'weit großem' (OCR) read as 'weit größeren'; 'wircklich', 'Daseyns' modernized silently. 'Einzelnen Object' = 'single object'.","'so außer uns' and 'Sie sind ewig' etc. left as in transcript; 'Dauer' = duration."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-086":["19. If one now passes over to the cognition of humans from the things themselves, the faculty of humans to cognize things shows itself as very restricted. Only the understanding of God is called intuition: as inexplicable as this kind of understanding is to us humans; so it is nevertheless supposed to indicate that God has the faculty to cognize the things as they are in themselves, which is entirely lacking to humans. The human can cognize only through concepts, i.e. through marks, which he abstracts for himself from the objects sensed through intuition, but through the abstraction-procedure must set aside very many of the things, in order to have insight into only something of the things, consequently the marks made thereby and the thinking of the things through them give only a restricted cognition, which can never go further than sensibility offers him the material for it, hence he is also capable only of cognizing in the things, in the way as they appear to him, but nothing of the things themselves, and as they are in themselves. Thus just as sensibility is the faculty of intuition, so the understanding of the human is the faculty of concepts. Concepts can also be thought *a priori*, if they contain nothing but the concept of *synthesis*, i.e. composition of the manifold in the representation, in order to make up a cognition, and this *synthesis* has unity, i.e. the consciousness of myself of the combination of the manifold in my representation, whether this combination may otherwise *involve* negative or affirmative parts of the representation. Under these conditions there arises a concept that is thought *a priori* of the object; for the combination of the manifold in the representation and the consciousness of it, or the unity, are not empirical concepts, but *a priori*. E.g. that a body is composed, I cannot know from experience, I cannot see the composition. Here there is a mere *actus* of thinking, notwithstanding that at first appearance the concept of the composite seems to be made *a posteriori*. The understanding alone has combined the manifold in the representation, and through the consciousness of the combination the concept arose. On this rests the pure concept of the understanding: or concept *a priori*. The consciousness itself of the combination of the manifold the subject or the understanding obtains through the pure intuition of space and time. One calls the pure concepts of the understanding therefore those which contain synthetic unity or the consciousness of the combination of the manifold in the representation, or concepts of the unity of the manifold in the synthetic representation. The pure concept of the understanding one predicates as category — consequently this is the consciousness of the synthetic unity of the given manifold in the representation. The category in and for itself leaves unsettled, and undetermined, whether the concept, i.e. it itself, has objective reality. It is in this like a representation which contains nothing contradicting, can thus be thought, but can be empty. Whether it thus rests on, or has, sensible synthetic unity — empirical unity — remains undetermined with it. Meanwhile the pure concept of the understanding is understood only at the beginning so that it is uncertain whether it has reality or not, afterward, however, through the condition of space and time it is added that I thereby become aware that it signifies something objective.",["OCR corrected silently: Bingen/Binge → Dingen/Dinge; Benken → Denken; Ber → Der; Bas → Das. The stray \"6s\" before \"ist\" is treated as a page-number intrusion and omitted.","Erkenntniß = cognition, erkennen = cognize; Vermögen = faculty; Merkmal = mark; Stoff = material; Verbindung = combination; Zusammensetzung = composition (not in glossary); eingeschränkt = restricted.","Gegenstande (\"object\") and Objecte (\"objects\") are both rendered \"object\"; Latinate Object(e) vs. Gegenstand.","\"Den reinen Verstandes Begriff praedicirt man Categorie\": the German is elliptical; \"as\" is added in English to carry the predicate construction; \"Categorie\" is German-form here, rendered \"category\".","\"ob sie ... beruhe, oder habe\": the verb \"habe\" has no stated object beyond the shared \"auf ... Einheit\"; left as in the German (\"rests on, or has\").","\"sie selbst\" in \"d. i. sie selbst\" and the shifting \"er/sie\" referents (Begriff/Categorie) are preserved without resolution.","*involviren* kept in Latinate form as *involve*; *actus* retained in Latin."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-087":["E.g. the category of magnitude, as a many of the same kind, which together makes up one: this cannot be comprehended without space and time. Here, moreover, it does not depend on empirical actuality of bodies, but only on the possibility, according to our understanding, of finding what magnitude is, and this possibility is, as said, possible through pure intuition *a priori*.",["Größe = magnitude; begreifen = comprehend; Wirklichkeit = actuality; Möglichkeit = possibility.","\"Z. E.\" (zum Exempel) rendered \"E.g.\""]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-088":["So it is also with the concept of cause, insofar as it consists in the ground of the existence of another according to a constant rule. The category, that B is the effect of A, and the necessity of the cause, to be an effect, that a *causatum* has a *causam*, is however so little to be comprehended if one does not take the pure intuition of space and time to one's aid.",["Ursach = cause; Daseyn = existence; Wirkung = effect; Nothwendigkeit = necessity.","Latin \"caußatum ... caußam\" normalized to *causatum*, *causam*.","The sentence is syntactically awkward (\"ist ... so wenig zu begreifen, wenn ...\"); kept as is, with no completion of the comparison."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-089":["18) Upon the presupposition now that we are only in a position to represent to ourselves the objects in appearance, and not in themselves, Herr Kant builds",["Passage breaks off; continued in the next passages. \"an sich\" = \"in itself/themselves\"; Erscheinung = appearance; Object = object.","The numbering \"18)\" follows the transcript as printed, after \"19.\" in the preceding passage."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-090":["1.) the theory of the ideality of space and time, and rejects",["Continues the sentence of the preceding passage; ends without its object."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-091":["2.) the theory of the opposing reality of space and time.",["Realität = reality; \"entgegenstehenden\" = opposing."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-092":["The theory of ideality thus assumes that space and time are nothing other than something subjective, or = merely concern the form of sensible intuition. It thus rests on the principle that space and time are not real determinations of the things in themselves, but mere forms, so as conditions lying merely in the subject are given for the sake of intuition. The possibility of having such sensible forms is already clear e.g. from this, that we in the concept of God can ascribe to him not the predicate of thinking, on account of the restrictedness of this concept, but a faculty of intuition of the things in themselves, as that which rests on this principle.",["The sign \"=\" is in the text. \"oder = blos die Form ... angehe\" has a subjunctive in a clause co-ordinated with an indicative; kept.","\"so als blos im subject liegende Bedingungen\" is obscure; kept literally. \"als welches\" could refer to \"Anschauungs Vermögen\" or to the whole attribution; left open.","\"erhelle\" and \"beruhe\" are subjunctive (reported speech), rendered with the indicative.","Bestimmung = determination; Bedingung = condition; Eingeschränktheit = restrictedness; Behuf = sake (not in glossary)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-093":["The defenders of the theory of the reality of space and time assume on the contrary that space and time would be a constitution of the things in themselves, and we would have space and time not *a priori* but from experience. Herr Kant refutes this through the existence of the cognitions *a priori* of space and time, and that it would be impossible to cognize synthetically *a priori* the constitution of the same, e.g. the 3 dimensions of space, or to judge about it *a priori*.",["Beschaffenheit = constitution; Abmeßungen rendered \"dimensions\" (not in glossary).","\"a priori synthetisch zu erkennen\": \"a priori\" could attach to \"synthetisch\" or to \"zu erkennen\"; the English order preserves this.","\"derselben\" and \"darüber\" are left as pronouns (\"the same\", \"it\")."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-094":["Herr Kant determines however",["Breaks off; continued in the next passage. bestimmen = determine."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-095":["3. the theory of the ideality of space and time as something merely subjective, in this more precisely, that we thereby would become capable not merely of cognizing our own subject, but would actually cognize objects; for, if we once represented to ourselves objects as *phaenomena*, then all predicates too, which we by virtue of the subjective form had represented to ourselves and intuited, would actually belong to the objects, but only merely as appearance, and insofar the objects would have reality for us. Since it would thus be impossible according to this to represent to oneself the object in itself, *noumena* could concern only objects of non-sensible intuition.",["The subjunctives (reported speech, with the conditional \"kämen\", \"hätten\") are rendered with \"would\"/past forms.","\"blos unser eignes subject zu erkennen fähig würden, sondern wirklich Objecte erkennten\": the grammar of the \"nicht blos ... sondern\" pair is uneven in the German; kept.","\"Da es also hiernach unmöglich sey\" kept as a causal clause; \"so könnten noumena nur Objecte ... betreffen\": *noumena* could be subject or object of \"betreffen\"; English leaves it likewise.","Gegenstand/Object: here only Object(e), \"object\"; Realität = reality."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-096":["Herr Kant grounds his theory of the ideality of space and time more closely through the properties of both.",["Eigenschaften rendered \"properties\" (not in glossary; Beschaffenheit is \"constitution; property\" in the glossary). begründen = ground (cf. Grund)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-097":["If it is namely necessary that space and time belong to the things themselves as properties, and we do not intuit the things as appearance through the subjective form of our senses, then we must cognize space and time from the things through the understanding; whose form rests in the formation of concepts, but essentially on this, that it thinks to itself a many, i.e., parts, and combines these into a unity, i.e., into a whole, i.e., thinks the things to itself through one concept. The object therefore essentially has parts: now what is a part of another must also let itself be considered as subsisting for itself, and can be called a part only in relation to the whole. Now, however, the singular space can be thought only as a part, i.e., a section of a larger one, and this larger one again as part of a still larger one, and this on to infinity, hence the infinity of space and time must also be predicated. It can therefore have no boundary like objects, it has no parts under itself that let themselves be combined into unity, hence it also cannot be thought as a whole. It therefore has no determination at all that presents something objective, is therefore no object, and therefore cannot be thought through concepts. From this it follows that it is only something singular, hence that the representation of it rests on intuition. From this it follows that this intuition must be not empirical but *a priori*, since space can be thought sooner than the object in space. The representation therefore lies in the subject alone. Now, since space and time are thought *a priori*, it is necessary to lay all objects of sensible intuition into space and time, but they could not be ordered if sensibility were not provided with a form ordained for this, and this form must be sought in space and time. Hence they are the only sensible forms of our intuition, which are merely subjective. From this it also follows again that, since we intuit the objects themselves only in a certain form, this form lies in our subject and not in the object, and that we can therefore cognize the objects only as they appear to us.",["OCR corrected silently: 'I orm' = Form, 'Iheil' = Theil, 'großem' = größern, 'hegt/hege' = liegt/liege.","Object and Gegenstand are both 'object'; here the German is Object, Ding is 'thing'.","'Eigenschaften' has no glossary entry; rendered 'properties' (cf. Beschaffenheit = constitution; property).","'Er' (space, then time) is rendered 'it'; the reference of 'er' in the middle sentences is left as in the German, as is 'dessen' ('whose'), which could refer to the understanding or to the cognizing.","'Einzelne/einzelnes' = 'singular'; 'zugleich' (later passages) = 'at the same time'; 'ordinirten' rendered 'ordained'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-098":["*Consequentiae*. There now follow from the concept of space and time some synthetic propositions *a priori*.",["'Es folgern sich' is reflexive-passive in the German ('follow themselves'); rendered 'there follow'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-099":["1.) There are not two times at the same time, but one after another, just as two spaces are not one after another, but at the same time.",["OCR 'zw ey' corrected to 'zwey'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-100":["2.) In time all objects are thought and represented by me as they are at the same time or one after another, in space, however, the objects are represented as they are outside me, hence that which exists in me and not outside me cannot be intuited in space.",["Gegenstände = 'objects' (Gegenstand); not Object."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-101":["3.) The representation of time is namely in me, and time therefore belongs to inner sense, the representation of space, however, is outside me, and belongs to outer sense. The determinations of the representations with regard to time therefore all occur in me, with regard to space, however, all outside me. If Newton now says in a *scholio* of the *principiorum philosophiae naturalis*: time is everywhere and space at all times, this cannot be understood to the effect that time has a place. Time has no place, to which I can posit something in time, and what I think to myself in time is not at all in space. But it can quite certainly mean: the concept of time takes into itself all representations, but space only those representations insofar as the objects are outside me.",["'faßt ... in sich' rendered 'takes ... into itself' (not 'comprehends', which is reserved for begreifen).","Latin titles left as printed, in italics; 'scholio' as in the transcript.","'Rücksicht' rendered 'regard'; 'setzen' = 'posit'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-102":["4.) All things outside me I can think to myself, insofar as I take regard of the relation with other things, in no other way than in space. I.e., determinations of the relation of things outside me let themselves be given in no other way than under the presupposition of their existence in space.",["'existenz' (lower-case in the transcript) rendered 'existence' (Existenz)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s03-103":["If therefore Wolf thinks the things in space, and posits space in the *ordine simultaneorum*, then space is cognized through a concept of the understanding, through the relation of things. Likewise when he determines time through *ordinem succeßivorum, quatenus sunt invicem connexa (quae post invicem essent)*: But one must already have thought space and time in advance, before one thinks things at the same time or one after another.",["Latin quoted as in the transcript (including the spelling 'succeßivorum'); the case forms (ordine/ordinem) are as printed and are not emended.","The sentence beginning 'Ebenso wenn' has no finite main clause in the German; left so."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-001":["Remark",["Anmerkung; heading."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-002":["In the Critique of Pure Reason it was concluded, through the presentation of the concept of sensibility (*perceptivitaet*) as a faculty of obtaining representations according to the way in which the subject is affected, and of the concept of the understanding (faculty of thinking — *spontaneitaet*) as the faculty of cognizing in accordance with these representations, that the mind appears to itself, or that the soul could be an object of appearance of itself. It is correct in itself that the subject can be affected through itself, and thus through the inner sense of the soul can obtain representations and cognize from them, after the mind has previously been affected by the objects, but in order to avoid ambiguities about this, Herr Kant proposes instead of this the definition of sensibility to the effect: the merely subjective in the representation of things, insofar as it contains the formal ground of presenting the objects to oneself according to it, or also = sensible form of our intuition. For something objective itself cannot lie in it. It determines, according to the form lying in it, the appearance, i.e., it determines in advance the way in which the object will appear. It lies at the ground of all kinds of sensible intuition, and is thus present *a priori* in order to determine these intuitions in space and time, but has no combination at all with the object itself, still less is it a constitution of the object itself, but merely gives the appearance of it to be cognized.",["The word order of the opening clause is kept; the German could also be read as attaching 'zu erhalten' only to Vermögens, and the construction of 'geschloßen worden' is left as awkward as in the German.","'Gegenstand der Erscheinung von sich selbst': 'of itself' could attach to 'appearance' or to 'object'; left ambiguous.","'Art' = 'way' in 'nach der Art' and 'kinds' in 'Arten sinnlicher Anschauung'. 'Definition' (Latinate) rendered 'definition'; 'Beschaffenheit' = 'constitution'.","'dahin' ('to the effect') introduces the definition; 'präsentiren' = 'present'. 'Maaßgabe' = 'accordance'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-003":["Here is the place to indicate the distinction of a representation that is *sensitiv*, from the *sensibili*. With regard to the objects that are represented, the representations are either:",["'sensitiv' and 'sensibili' left in Latin as printed; 'sensibili' stands in the dative/ablative form as in the transcript."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-004":["*sensitive*, i.e., sensible, which rest on the form of sensibility alone, without the existence of matter concurring in them. Thus space and time are forms of sensibility without an object of the senses concurring in them and being combined with them.",["'sensitive' (Latin) and 'sinnlich' ('sensible') are both kept visible."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-005":["These can, however, at the same time be combined with the matter or the object of the senses, and then the *sensitive* become *sensibilia* or: sensable, i.e., those that at the same time contain reality. Thus there can be something in space and time that is an object of the senses, and this is the sensable, which gives the reality, hence the sensable is equal to the matter, to sensibility. — Thus all empirical cognitions have not merely the *sensitive rationem* of the form, but at the same time something sensable, whereas in sensible cognition in general nothing empirical, hence also nothing sensable, is contained.",["'empfindbar' (from Empfindung = sensation) rendered 'sensable' to keep it distinct from 'sinnlich' = 'sensible'; not in the glossary.","'der Materie, der Sinnlichkeit gleich': could be 'equal to the matter of sensibility' or 'equal to the matter, to sensibility'; left ambiguous as far as English allows.","'das sensitive rationem der Form': the grammar (neuter article, accusative 'rationem') is as in the transcript and possibly corrupt; not emended."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-006":["Opposed to sensible cognition *in opposito* are, however, intellectual representations, which are thought merely through the understanding. Here too the form of thinking and of the matter or reality is similar the concept of the understanding as the product.",["The last sentence is obscure or possibly corrupt in the transcript; 'ähnlich' could govern 'der Verstandes Begriff' as dative ('similar to the concept of the understanding') or the sentence could be broken; kept as it stands.","'Verstandes Begriff' = 'concept of the understanding'; 'intellectuelle' = 'intellectual'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-007":["ad § 19. Everything that has been said up to now about the possibility of obtaining cognitions *a priori* through the subjective form of sensible intuition belongs to the transcendental aesthetic, i.e., doctrine of the senses, on the other hand transcendental logic, i.e., doctrine of the understanding, supplies a second source of principles, in order possibly to obtain pure cognitions *a priori*. This differs from general pure logic in that this occupies itself with the mere form of the use of our understanding, but that goes to the determination of the pure cognition of objects through the mere understanding.",["*ad* § 19: Latin kept. Erkenntniß = cognition; Anschauung = intuition; Verstand = understanding; Gebrauch = use; Bestimmung = determination; Objecte = objects (Latinate form; Gegenstand is also \"object\").","\"möglicher Weise\" could be \"possibly\" or \"in a possible way\"; \"possibly\" chosen, and it may attach to \"obtain\" or to the whole clause.","\"diese ... jene\": the German pronouns are left as \"this ... that\" without saying which logic is which. The antecedents are ambiguous."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-008":["A pure concept of the understanding is a pure cognition of the object through the mere understanding, and the complete *oppositum* of the empirical concept, in that it is thought completely pure *a priori*, consequently one can also call it a concept of pure thinking, in order to distinguish it from sensibility.",["OCR \"V erstand\" corrected to Verstand. *Oppositum* is Latin in the source.","Sinnlichkeit = sensibility."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-009":["The concept *a priori* of the synthetic unity of the manifold in intuition is category. The combination of the manifold and the consciousness of the same constitutes the constitutive of the category.",["\"das constitutive\" is a substantivized adjective, kept as \"the constitutive\". \"derselben\" (\"the same\") may refer to the combination or to the manifold.","Verbindung = combination; Bewußtseyn = consciousness; das Mannigfaltige = the manifold."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-010":["The consciousness of the unity of the manifold according to concepts is logical function.",["No article before \"logical function\" in the German, and none in the English."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-011":["All objects (whether they occur in appearance or through concepts) can certainly be perceived, but never their composition. The understanding must add this to the representation, and it is thus alone an *actus* of the understanding, namely the composition, in order to represent to itself the composite there so that it becomes one. — Mr. Kant calls this *synthesis*, e.g., the parts that together make up a room.",["Gegenstände = objects; wahrnehmen = perceive (Wahrnehmung = perception); Erscheinung = appearance; Vorstellung = representation; Theile = parts.","\"sich ... dahin vorzustellen\": \"dahin\" rendered \"there\"; the German is awkward here and the awkwardness is kept. \"sich\" could be \"itself\" (the understanding) or \"oneself\".","Stube = room."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-012":["The consciousness of the synthetic unity of the manifold in intuition in general or (which =) the consciousness of the concept that contains the synthetic unity, is = category.",["\"überhaupt\" = \"in general\"; it may go with \"intuition\" or with the whole phrase."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-013":["Thus Aristotle named the pure concepts of the understanding; the Scholastics named them *praedicamenta* and they were equal in number; there are namely 12 in general, and they contain the *elementa* of the cognitions *a priori* through the understanding. Besides these there are also *praedicabilia*, which are partly composed of the first, e.g., cause combined with magnitude, partly the *praedicamenta* combined with the concepts of space and time. These too can be enumerated, and presumably quite completely. Mr. Kant has not enumerated them.",["Printed \"praedicabilien\" and \"praedicamente\" in German form; given here as Latin *praedicabilia*, *praedicamenta*.","\"aus erstem\" (\"of the first\") is left unresolved. The antecedent is unclear.","\"der Zahl nach gleich ;\" may be defective in the OCR; translated as it stands.","Ursach = cause; Größe = magnitude; Raum = space; Zeit = time."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-014":["Taken together, categories and *praedicabilia* make up the entirety of the elementary principles, in order to cognize the domain of all cognitions *a priori*.",["OCR \"Unfang\" read as Umfang (= domain). \"sämmtliche\" rendered \"the entirety of\".","erkennen = cognize; principien = principles (Latinate form; Grundsatz is also \"principle\")."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-015":["20. It is very remarkable that it lets itself be made possible to bring all possible concepts of the understanding into classes, and to derive them from the faculty of the understanding in such a way that it is thereby exhausted according to its domain, just as the principles of all sensible cognitions result from the form of space and time. The understanding is the faculty of concepts. Now in order to bring something under a concept, a judgment is each time necessary, consequently all actions of the understanding run out into a judging. For thinking is a discursive cognition; i.e., the cognition through certain marks of things. These latter make up the predicate, which is attributed to the representation of the thing as subject, but in this combination lies the judgment. Now just as logic contains functions of the understanding, i.e., rules of thinking in general, and without regard to an object, so transcendental logic, which is that general logic applied to objective cognition *a priori*, contains these functions of thinking or forms of judgments, consequently here too the 4 logical functions can be laid as the ground, and all possible elementary principles of pure thinking reduced to quantity, quality, relation and modality. Accordingly the table of the pure concepts of the understanding can thus be traced back to the table of the 4 logical functions, thus:",["OCR slips corrected: \"\\ erstandes\" → Verstandes; \"Dei\" → Der; \"mithm\" → mithin; \"Unfang\" is not in this passage.","\"um etwas uns unter einen Begriff zu bringen\": \"uns\" is odd or garbled; left untranslated, and the sentence is rendered as it can be made out.","\"es sich möglich machen laße\": literal and awkward; \"it\" has no clear antecedent.","Vermögen = faculty; Umfang = domain; Merkmal = mark; Verbindung = combination; Urtheil = judgment; Grund = ground; Object = object (Latinate form)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-016":["1) According to quantity our logical judgments are *judicia* a *singularia* singular b *particularia* particular which one, however, since here several objects are always presupposed, but it is undecided whether the several is not totality, this however must be different from it, would better call *pluralia*.",["\"die man aber ... beßer pluralia nennen würde\": the German sentence is tangled; kept as one sentence in the German order, including the \"dies ... davon\" clause.","Allheit = totality; Gegenstände = objects; besondere = particular."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-017":["c *universalia* — universal",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-018":["II) according to quality a *affirmativa* b *negativa* c *infinita*, i.e., affirmative judgments with regard to the *copula*, or negative with regard to the predicate, e.g., *anima est nonmortalis*. III) according to relation (relations)",["\"nonmortalis\" is as printed. \"Verhältniße\" in parentheses = relations; Relation and Verhältniß are both \"relation\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-019":["a Categorical in the relation of the predicate to the subject b hypothetical in the relation of the sequel to the ground c disjunctive in the relation of a member of the division to the whole sphere of the concept. IV) according to modality (relations)",["Folge = sequel; Eintheilung = division; \"sphaere\" given as \"sphere\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-020":["a problematic b assertoric c apodictic These contain the relations of the predicate to the subject, according to the difference *ad* a) of logical possibility *ad* b) of logical actuality *ad* c) of logical necessity Namely the judgment contains a predicate, which *ratione subjecti* is thought only under the condition of possibility, so it is thought merely problematically; but it becomes a proposition or assertoric judgment if it is actually attributed to the subject, and if this proposition is combined with necessity, the judgment becomes apodictic.",["OCR \"V erschiedenheit\", \"Noth Wendigkeit\" corrected.","Satz = proposition; Möglichkeit = possibility; Wirklichkeit = actuality; Bedingung = condition; beylegen = attribute."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-021":["Upon this now rest just as many categories only with the difference that these are directed at objects, here everything is predicated of objects that was said of concepts in the case of the logical functions. Thus: I) according to quantity",["The syntax is kept as in the German (comma-joined clauses)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-022":["1 a Unity",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-023":["2 b Plurality",["Vielheit = plurality (not in the glossary; Menge = multitude is kept for another word)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-024":["3 c Totality II) according to quality",["Allheit = totality."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-025":["4 a Reality",["Realität = reality."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-026":["5 b Negation",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-027":["6 c Limitation i.e., according to quality the category rests on the presupposition either of something that contains the concept of being, e.g., light, or the concept of non-being, e.g., rest, or the concept of being, at the same time combined with non-being as limitation. III) according to relation, where always two corresponding concepts concur",["\"Limitation\" (Latinate) and \"Einschränkung\" are both rendered \"limitation\"; Schranke is also \"limitation\" in the glossary.","Seyn = being (Wesen is also \"being\"); Nichtseyn = non-being."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-028":["7 a. Substance — *accidens*",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-029":["8b. Cause — effect",["Ursach = cause; Wirkung = effect."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-030":["9 c. *Agens* — *patiens*, or = the community, or = *influxus mutuus* or = *commercium*",["Gemeinschaft = community."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-031":["NB ad 9 c. The relation of community is different from the sequel to the ground in that here there is reciprocally cause and effect, i.e., there is something in the *caußato* that is ground of the *causa*, and something in the *causa* that is ground of the *caußato* = both at the same time. IV) according to modality",["\"von der Folge zum Grunde verschieden\": elliptical; kept (\"different from the sequel to the ground\"). *caußato* is as printed.","*NB*, *ad* kept as in the source."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-032":["10a. Possibility — (Impossibility)",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-033":["*potentialitas*",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-034":["11b. Actuality *actualitas*",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-035":["(Existence) (Non-being)",["Daseyn = existence (Existenz is also \"existence\" in the next passage)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-036":["12 c. *Necessitas* (Contingency) Here one cannot equate the concept of actuality with the concept of existence; for the existence of a thing comprehends within itself the possibility as well as actuality as well as necessity of an object, whereby of all 3 existence, but of the *actualitas* however actuality alone is predicated.",["OCR \"aetualitas\" read as *actualitas*; \"Neceßitas\" as *Necessitas*.","Existenz = existence (also Daseyn above); begreifen = comprehend; Zufälligkeit = contingency."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-037":["Comparison of the 4 logical functions with the 12 categories ad A. 1 Quantity *singularia* *particularia* *universalia* Unity Plurality Totality",["Table layout flattened by the OCR; translated as it stands."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-038":["2 Quality *affirmativa* *negativa* *infinita*",["The category row of the table is not in this passage."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-040":["categorical, hypothetical, disjunctive",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-042":["problematic assertoric apodictic Possibility, Actuality, Necessity",["\"N othwendigkeit\" in the OCR corrected."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-044":["I Remarks",["Bemerkungen = remarks."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-045":["1) One can reduce these 12 categories to 2 classes, of which each again comprises 2 classes under itself and",["The sentence breaks off with \"und\" and continues in the next passages."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-046":["A. ) call the first 6 categories, of quantity and quality, the mathematical class.",["Continues the sentence of 045 (\"call\" depends on \"one can\")."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-047":["B. ) call the last 6 categories, of relation and modality, from 7 — 12, the dynamical class.",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-048":["2) Aristotle brought out only 10 categories: but a classification according to the table of the logical functions was entirely lacking. They were therefore found contingently, deficient, and even the forms of space and time among them, notwithstanding that these belong in a particular class.",["\"zufällig\" = contingently; \"besondere\" = particular; \"sogar die formen ... darunter\" is elliptical and kept so."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-049":["3) The appearance that we are in a position to find the functions of the understanding completely, and that there are just as many pure concepts of the understanding *a priori* as there are logical functions to be found, that the former correspond to the latter, and so that we are thereby in a position to determine all elements of pure thinking about all objects, namely before all possible experience, is always remarkable: yet, as goes without saying, within the boundaries of transcendental philosophy, i.e. that the pure cognitions of reason, whose elements are set forth here, are immanent, i.e. have objective reality, and hence that class of cognitions of reason that have no object of possible experience is excluded here.",["Erscheinung is rendered 'appearance' (glossary); here it is used of a remarkable phenomenon or fact, and the English keeps the one word.","Verstandes Begriffe = 'concepts of the understanding'; Vernunft-Erkenntniße = 'cognitions of reason'; Gränzen = 'boundaries' (Grenze).","'Philosophie' is rendered 'philosophy'; the glossary's Weltweisheit does not occur here. 'a priori', 'transcendental', 'immanent' and 'objective' are kept as in the German.","'hie durch' read as 'hierdurch'; 'Gegenstände' = 'objects'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-050":["4) From the *praedicamenta*, i.e. these categories so called by the Scholastics, arise the *praedicabilia*, i.e. those pure concepts of the understanding that are either composed of 2 or more categories, or that arise from the combination of a *praedicamentum* with a form of sensibility, of space and time.",["Verbindung = 'combination'; Form der Sinnlichkeit = 'form of sensibility'.","Latin technical terms *praedicamenta*, *praedicabilia* are left in Latin and italicized; 'praedicaments' in the German is the Germanized form (singular *praedicamentum* below)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-051":["E.g. the concept of duration rests, with regard to quality, on reality or existence, and duration also has magnitude of an existence, with respect to existence in time. For insofar the duration of the grain of sand is equal to the duration of the mountain of sand, and this magnitude is different from material quantity. Hence a *praedicamentum* concurs with the form of sensibility. The concept of motion presupposes existence or quality, and is thought with alteration of existence with opposed determination of time and space, thus existence in successive form one after another in time and space.",["Daseyn (Dasein) and existenz (Existenz) are both rendered 'existence'; the German word is Dasein in 'Größe eines Dasejms' and 'Daseyn', Existenz in 'realität oder existenz' and the other occurrences.","'Dasejms' is an OCR slip for 'Daseyns'; 'Z. E.' = zum Exempel, rendered 'E.g.'.","Realität = 'reality'; Bestimmung = 'determination'; Veränderung = 'alteration' (the glossary has no entry; 'alteration' is chosen and used consistently).","'successiver Form' kept as 'successive form'; 'nach einander' = 'one after another'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-052":["5) In every function the categories are so ordered that the 3rd is composed of the two preceding, e.g. the community of concepts presupposes 2 substances, with cause and effect, and combines them into a third, namely the alternation — cause and effect. Thus reality, combined with negation, gives limitation.",["Gemeinschaft = 'community'; Verbindung/verbindet = 'combination'/'combines'; Wirkung = 'effect'; Realität = 'reality'.","'Wechsel' rendered 'alternation' (not in the glossary).","'Gemeinschaft der Begriffe' is as the German reads; 'Begriffe' may be a slip for 'Substanzen', but I leave it as it stands.","'limitation' renders limitation (Latinate form in the German); Schranke would also be 'limitation' in the glossary."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-053":["Thus totality = a multiplicity that is at the same time unity.",["Allheit = 'totality'; Vielheit = 'multiplicity' (glossary has Menge = 'multitude; multiplicity'; Vielheit is a different German word, also rendered 'multiplicity' here, and Menge would be marked if it occurred); Einheit = 'unity'.","'so zugleich Einheit ist': 'so' is a relative here, 'that is'; the German is kept compressed."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-054":["Thus necessity is a possibility from which actuality can be concluded.",["Wirklichkeit = 'actuality' (glossary); gefolgert = 'concluded' (folgern); Möglichkeit = 'possibility'; Nothwendigkeit = 'necessity'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-055":["6) In accordance with the premised classification of the categories, ontology or transcendental philosophy is now also to be treated, in that, by the categories being set up against one another, the development of these elements of our concepts of the understanding *a priori* is facilitated, through whose unification the arbitrary, as is connected with Baumgarten's method, is avoided, and these principles *a priori* are better comprehended as a whole. Accordingly the 6 categories of the mathematical class will also be treated, and among these there follows first:",["'prämittirten' = 'premised' (Latin praemittere); Willkührliche = 'the arbitrary'; Methode = 'method'.","'befaßt' rendered 'comprehended' (not begreifen in the German; the English word is the glossary's choice for begreifen, so the German is marked here: befassen).","'durch deren Vereinigung': the antecedent of 'deren' (elements, or concepts) is ambiguous; the English 'whose' preserves this. 'Diesen zufolge' = 'accordingly'.","'Ontologie' and 'transcendental Philosophie' kept as in the German; 'principien' = 'principles' (Grundsatz would be 'principle' also)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-056":["The category of magnitude or the class of quantity.",["Größe = 'magnitude'; Quantität = 'quantity'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-057":["21. Quantity — magnitude is a pure concept of the understanding, whose development rests on this:",["'21.' is the paragraph number as printed; 'darauf' is rendered 'on this'.","Entwickelung = 'development'; reiner Verstandes Begriff = 'pure concept of the understanding'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-058":["1) In every magnitude the concept of many is contained, which, considered as combined, presents itself as a one. A many of the same kind, combined, constitutes quantity in itself. Thus there lies in the concept of magnitude the representation of one and many.",["Verbunden = 'combined'; Betrachtung = 'consideration' (betrachtet = 'considered'); an sich = 'in itself'; Vorstellung = 'representation'.","'ein Vieles gleichartige' is kept as 'a many of the same kind'; 'Eins' = 'one' and 'Vieles' = 'many', with the German nominalization kept."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-059":["2) One and many are conceivable only as *correlata* and can only be thought *in opposito*, but neither of the two can be defined, rather they can only be thought through one another. Hence the *definitio autoris* S4 § 74 runs around *in circulo*: he thinks to himself a one in the presupposition of a many.",["'S4' is as in the transcript (the 'Autor' is Baumgarten, section 4); the OCR may be uncertain.","definiren = 'define'; Erklärung (not occurring here) is the glossary's 'definition'.","'Daher läuft ... herum' kept literal as 'runs around'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-060":["3) Unity can be thought both *formaliter* and *materialiter*.",["*formaliter* and *materialiter* kept in Latin."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-061":["Formal unity rests on the combination of the manifold under a principle. Material unity is unity of the object itself. Formally, therefore, only a unity of a thing is possible (*in singulari*), for every thing has only one concept, under which the manifold is brought by the understanding. Materially, however, there are unities of one and the same object, i.e. as many as can be called one in each thing. Multiplicity therefore in the object is at all times material. The manifold in the representation of the object in itself, however, is formal multiplicity.",["Mannigfaltige = 'manifold' (n.); Vielheit = 'multiplicity'; Object = 'object'.","'giebt s Einheiten' has an OCR slip; read as 'giebt es Einheiten'.","'Das Mannigfaltige in der Vorstellung des Objects aber an sich': 'an sich' may attach to 'Objects' ('of the object in itself') or to the whole clause; the English is left ambiguous in the same way.","'formelle' and 'formale' are both 'formal'; 'materiell' = 'material'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-062":["Hence a play has multiplicity of objects, but unity in the representation according to the laws of time, of place, etc., for without these it would combine to no end.",["Gesetze = 'laws'; Orts = 'place' (Ort); Zweck = 'end'; 'Objecte' = 'objects'.","'zu keinem Zweck verbinden' is kept literal; the subject 'es' (the play) is as in the German."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-063":["4) The Aristotelian school considers formal unity as transcendental, and assumes, as the author also does in Sect. 4, 6 and 7, in the metaphysical sense the principle: *quodlibet ens est transcendentaliter unum, verum bonum*, and predicated of the object a, *unum*, i.e. that One thing is not many things — unity b, *verum*, i.e. to each thing there really belongs what is its own, or — certain predicates that belong to the concept of the thing really belong to the thing, hence the predicates of the thing could be attributed to it according to actuality, not possibility, e.g. Every A has angles. This is called the proposition: every thing has truth in itself or in every thing there is truth, every thing has in itself nothing that does not agree with itself.",["Satz = 'proposition' (also 'principle' in the glossary); 'princip' = 'principle'; Wircklichkeit = 'actuality'; Möglichkeit = 'possibility'.","'Sect. 4, 6 u. 7' is as printed; the reading may be uncertain.","'unum, verum bonum' is printed without a comma in the German (the usual scholastic *unum, verum, bonum*); kept as it stands.","'iibereinstimmt' is an OCR slip for 'übereinstimmt'; 'Winckel' = 'angles' (the singular could also be read 'angle'). The syntax of 'praedicirten vom Object a, unum' is obscure and is left so.","'ihm eigen ist': 'what is its own'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-064":["c) *bonum*, i.e. every thing has everything in itself to be that which it is, or — every thing is transcendentally perfect.",["vollkommen = 'perfect' (Vollkommenheit = 'perfection')."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-065":["It clearly results that here every thing was considered only in relation to its own essence, and insofar these criteria of the thing are correct, since every thing after all is that which it is: but in the metaphysical sense, i.e. in the relation of the thing outside itself, hence considered against all other possible things, no perfection can be attributed to any thing, *exclusive* of the *ens realissimum*, rather each has a lack of reality, hence negative perfection, or is imperfect.",["Wesen = 'essence' (glossary: 'being; essence'); Criteria = 'criteria' (Kriterium); Realität = 'reality'; Vollkommenheit = 'perfection'.","'exclusive' is in Latin script in the German and is italicized; 'ens realißimo' is given as *ens realissimum* (dative in the German)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-066":["*In sensu metaphysico* these criteria are therefore unusable, but as logical precepts in the consideration of an object it is important that one must see from its determination whether it has unity, truth or perfection, e.g. formal unity of a book.",["Betrachtung = 'consideration'; Bestimmung = 'determination'; 'unnutzbar' = 'unusable'; 'Vorschriften' = 'precepts'.","'z. E.' = 'e.g.'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-067":["5) On the other hand, transcendental unity is in every thing insofar as much must be thought as combined in order to make, according to a principle, a concept of a thing. From this arises formal unity, but *formaliter* there cannot be unities of one and the same thing. Formal unity belongs essentially to the understanding, as that which is the faculty of bringing the manifold of the representation under a concept.",["Vermögen = 'faculty'; Verstände = 'understanding'; the OCR '\\ ermögen' is read as 'Vermögen'.","'Dagegen' = 'on the other hand' (could also be 'in contrast'); 'es' in 'formaliter kann es nicht Einheiten' is an expletive."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-068":["The author calls, § 74, *unitatem categoricam* the unity of the object, and in this he is right. Unity, considered objectively, is a thing that is not many, that with regard to what it contains is opposed to difference and to the many, hence a material unity. But *formaliter* it is hypothetical. A many, thought according to form, can be unity, even if the objects have a multiplicity materialiter.",["'Objective' = 'objectively'; 'Verschiedenheit' = 'difference'; 'Form' = 'form'.","'materialiter' is kept in Latin; in the last clause it is italicized in the German script as Latin and rendered as 'materialiter'; 'eine Vielheit' = 'a multiplicity'.","'so in Ansehung deßen' – the antecedent of 'so' (the thing) is kept ambiguous."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-069":["0) To magnitude belongs combination of multiplicity, and this is for the concept of the *quantum* not sufficient for the reason that according to it an aggregate of heterogeneous parts is admitted: by contrast the concept of the *compositum* is determined thereby.",["'0)' is as in the transcript (probably an OCR misreading of a numeral such as '6)'); doubtful. 'B<‘g> iff' is garbled OCR, read as 'Begriff'.","'hinlänglich' = 'sufficient' (zureichend in glossary is a different word; hinlänglich is rendered 'sufficient' as well); Theile = 'parts'.","*quantum* and *compositum* kept in Latin."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-070":["*Compositum est unum ex conjunctione plurium*, e.g. a garment, however diverse in kind its parts may be.",["'z.E.' = 'e.g.'; 'verschiedenartige' = 'diverse in kind'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-071":["If the combination is to involve the concept of magnitude, then the concept of homogeneity lies at the basis, and the concept of magnitude itself is determined through the word *quantum*: *est unum ex pluribus homogeneis conjunctum*. It therefore essentially contains",["Grunde = 'ground' in 'zum Grunde'; rendered 'lies at the basis'. The passage breaks off before the enumeration, which continues in the next passage.","'homogeneität' is rendered 'homogeneity'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-072":["1.) *multitudinem*: thus, what is considered as unity cannot be called *quantum*.",["*multitudinem* is Latin; the German 'Menge' would be 'multitude'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-073":["2.) *homogeneitatem*, i.e. things of one and the same genus (*genus*) *inde differt compositum* from the *quantum* and the many could then be *varietaet*, *omne quantum continet multitudinem, sed non omnis multitudo est quantum*, but only if the parts are of the same kind.",["Gattung = 'genus'; the German has 'Gattung (genus)', both rendered 'genus'.","'inde differt compositum vom quanto' mixes Latin and German; kept as it stands. *varietaet* is the transcript's spelling of *varietas*.","'gleichartig' = 'of the same kind'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-074":["That determination of a thing now through which one cognizes a matter as a *quantum* is quantity or magnitude.",["erkennt = 'cognizes' (cognoscere); Bestimmung = 'determination'; Sache = 'matter' (not Materie; Sache is rendered 'matter' here, and 'thing' stands for Ding); Quantitaet = 'quantity'; Größe = 'magnitude'.","'Sache' rendered 'matter' could be 'thing'; the glossary offers no equivalent."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-075":["NB Quantity is therefore nothing further than determination of how large something is: something can therefore be thought as a *quantum* without letting itself be determined as *quantitas*, or being determined thereby.",["Quantitaet = quantity (Latin *quantitas*); *quantum* kept in Latin as in the source.","Bestimmung = determination; 'sich ... bestimmen zu laßen' rendered literally; the reflexive 'sich' is left as in the German.","'dadurch' could refer to *quantitas* or to the determining; left ambiguous as in the German."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-076":["*Formaliter*, *quantum* is a manifold in the representation that is homogeneous, and the determination of the manifold as *quantum* is quantity.",["Mannigfaltiges = manifold (n.); Vorstellung = representation; gleichartig = homogeneous (literally 'of the same kind').","'so gleichartig ist' is awkward in the German ('so' = 'which'); the relative construction is kept awkward.","'Quantitaet' here rendered 'quantity'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-077":["From this it follows",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-078":["1) there are now several *quanta*, but not several quantities, since the *quantitas* as determination can only be one. *Quantitas*, like *qualitas*, are in general made from *quantum qualis* and in a barbarous way: one could just as well say *quaeditas*, in order to express through all 3 the familiar question: *quae, qualis, quanta*? *quidditas*, if one is to say so, would differ from quality as the determination of the genus and of the specific difference; e.g. *quidditas* of the essence of the genus: but whether it is hard or soft belongs to quality, hence with regard to the species comprehended under the genus.",["Qualitaet = quality, alongside *qualitas*; Weßen = essence (Wesen); 'das generis weßen' is as in the OCR, perhaps garbled; kept literal.","'Quantitas sowie Qualitas sind' has a plural verb after two subjects; kept.","'begriffenen' = comprehended (begreifen)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-079":["The *quaeditas* would express the relation; at least it signifies such in the interrogative word *quae*? where it is supposed to indicate the different relation of the concept: categorical, hypothetical, or disjunctive.",["OCR 'ausdriicken' corrected to 'ausdrücken'.","bedeuten = signify; Verhältnis = relation (also Latin *relation* in the text); Begriff = concept.","'es' and 'solches' are left unresolved as in the German."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-080":["2) *Qualitas* differs from quantity in that and insofar as the latter, given the same object, indicates something heterogeneous, which is found in it with regard to other determinations. Hence *qualitas* is that determination of a thing according to which that which is specifically different is found under the same genus, and can be distinguished from it. — This is heterogeneous *in opposito* to that which is not specifically different, or the homogeneous.",["Gattung = genus (cf. Latin *generis* in the preceding passage); 'findt' corrected to 'findet'.","ungleichartig = heterogeneous (literally 'of unlike kind'); the 'letztere' ('latter') refers to quantity; 'darin' ('in it') is ambiguous as to antecedent, and is left so.","'davon' ('from it') is ambiguous (the genus or the same thing)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-081":["3) By comparison of the thing with itself and its parts one can well cognize that it is a *quantum*, but never can one, without comparison of a thing with other things, determine what magnitude it actually has or how large it is. E.g. the earth has 5400 miles — the mile is [illegible fraction] of a degree — degree [illegible fraction] of the greatest meridian. All this can also be applied to a pea, and it is in vain to find the magnitude of the earth without comparison of the measure. Hence the definition of Wolff and of the author § 69 p. 3 *quantitas est interna entis determinatio, quae dari quidem sine alio assumto autem intelligi nequit* can never count as a definition, however correct the proposition is, and can be proven; but it is no concept equivalent to magnitude, nor can it be brought out in that way. It is meanwhile",["The fractions after 'Meile ist' and 'Grad' are garbled in the OCR ('JL', '~'); not reconstructed. Also '_' before 'wie groß' is a stray mark, omitted.","Erkennen = cognize; Größe = magnitude (but 'wie groß' = 'how large'); Maaß = measure; Satz = proposition; Begriff = concept.","'Autoris' = the author (i.e. Baumgarten); 'Wolf' rendered Wolff. 'aßumto' kept as in the source for Latin *assumto*; 'nie ... gelten' kept literal; the passage breaks off after 'Es ist indeß' (continued in the next passage)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-082":["4) striking: the concept of magnitude or the determination of a thing that it has magnitude is given, but magnitude as magnitude, i.e. how large the object is, is impossible to cognize from the thing itself. Indeed, even more. The concept of magnitude is quite proper to the understanding, since it occupies itself with combination of the homogeneous manifold. Magnitude is applied in mathematics with the help of a pure intuition in sensibility, i.e. through the form of space and time in the determination of every figure or number. But in philosophy it cannot be determined from the concept alone whether the category of magnitude has objective reality. I.e. it cannot be cognized that many together make up one.",["OCR 'd. 1.' read as 'd. i.'; 'lorm' as 'Form'; 'B.-griff' as 'Begriff'; 'Mathematic' kept in the German spelling.","Verstand = understanding; Verbindung = combination; Anschauung = intuition; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Realitaet = reality (*objective realitaet* in the source).","'dem Verstände gerade zu eigen' kept literal ('quite proper to', 'just proper to'); 'Vieles zusammen Eins' = 'many together one'.","'Philosophie' rendered 'philosophy' (the glossary's Weltweisheit does not appear here)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-083":["It is absolutely necessary to take the intuition *a priori*, as in mathematics, to help. All categories, with that of magnitude, are of the kind that through their concept itself it cannot be proven to what extent it is possible that the concept also has objective reality. This proof one must seek from the sensible intuition of space and time. Only thereby does extensive magnitude also obtain reality.",["'Biesen' in the OCR read as 'Diesen'; 'Hülfe' rendered 'help'.","'Anschauung a priori' could attach 'a priori' to the noun or to the verb 'nehmen'; the English keeps the adjacent order as ambiguous.","'von der Art' = 'of the kind'; 'mit derjenigen von der Größe' ('with that of magnitude') could mean 'including' or 'like'; kept ambiguous.","möglich = possible; Beweis = proof; sinnlich = sensible; Raum = space; Zeit = time."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-084":["*Extensio* differs from magnitude of time, or it is a manifold outside one another, e.g. as present is opposed to future time.",["The sentence is obscure in the German ('wie gegenwärtige zukünftiger Zeit opponirt'); kept obscure. The case of 'gegenwärtige' (nominative or other) is uncertain; 'Zeit' could be understood with it.","Extensio = *Ausdehnung* in the glossary but here the Latin stands in the text; kept in Latin."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-085":["5) If one takes particular account of magnitude in the case of numbers, one can determine *Quantum* by *aliquoties facta positio ejusdem*.",["besonders rendered 'particular' (glossary: besonder = particular); Zahl = number; bestimmen = determine.","Latin left as in the text."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-086":["*Succeßive unum uni addendo multitudinem cognoscimus*, i.e. through counting we cognize a multitude, with every number there is therefore a multitude present: if this goes on to infinity, there arises *multitudo omni numero major*: this one can well call an infinite multitude, but not an infinite number. For precisely on account of its infinity it cannot be expressed in numbers, and the expression therefore involves a *contradictio in adjecto*. Such an infinite multitude, however, is also *multitudo omni cognitione major*, for we can have a concept only of a determinate magnitude.",["Menge = multitude; Zahl = number; Ausdruck = expression; Begriff = concept; erkennen = cognize.","OCR 'unendliehe' read as 'unendliche'; 'Contradictio' written *contradictio*.","'Succeßive' kept as written; 'bestimmten' = determinate (bestimmen = determine)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-087":["*Numerus* is indeed *multitudo numerando (unum uni addendo) cognita*, to count on to infinity is impossible, then something ceases to lie within the bounds of a number, as soon as it cannot be counted further. &",["Grenze = bound(ary), here 'bounds'. The final '&' stands in the source (or OCR) and is kept.","'alsdann' rendered 'then'; 'ja' rendered 'indeed'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-088":["6) In order now to cognize a magnitude, it is necessary that the concept of measure be connected with it at the same time: for that something is a *Quantum* expresses merely something that determines the quality of the thing; on the other hand, without measure it is impossible to represent to oneself what *Quantum* has been produced through the composition.",["verbunden = connected, in the verb form of Verbindung (combination), kept as 'connected' here since the German verb is 'verbunden sey'; Maaß = measure; Vorstellung = representation (verb 'sich vorzustellen' = 'to represent to oneself').","'Composition' kept in the German spelling; 'qualitaet' = quality."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-089":["The category of magnitude cannot be cognized with certainty to the effect that it also has objective reality without presenting to it an object through which significance is given to it.",["Bedeutung = significance; darstellen = present; Object = object.","'nicht dahin ... erkennen' kept literal; the negation could attach to 'sich läßt' or to the clause 'ohne ... darzustellen'; left as in the German."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-090":["Hence: *mensura* is the *unum, quod numerando quantitatem reddit cognoscibilem*. Just as little would one see that there were threefold specifically different ways of measuring a magnitude, if one did not have an example in space according to its length, height, and thickness.",["Art = way; Raum = space; einsehen rendered 'see'; the Latin phrase is kept as in the text."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-091":["Time, by contrast, has only one dimension (*dimensio*) and this one represents to oneself in length and forms figuratively through the sign of a line. The line itself presents all points simultaneously, but the sign of the line expresses the successive sequence or the changes in time.",["Abmeßung = dimension (with *dimensio* in the text); Folge = sequence; 'successive' left as in the German ('successive Folge').","'stellt man sich vor' = 'one represents to oneself'; 'diese' is the antecedent 'Abmeßung' (one dimension)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-092":["7) A magnitude is now either",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-093":["a) a *quantum assignabile*, which one also calls *quantum dabile*. This is that magnitude which can be presented intuitively as a whole (in its totality); every measure must be so constituted, hence such a magnitude must also be able to be determined by the measure according to its relation, or",["anschaulich = intuitive, rendered 'intuitively'; totalitaet = totality (Allheit in the glossary; here Latin-derived *totalitaet*); beschaffen = constituted (cf. Beschaffenheit = constitution).","'aßignabile' read as *assignabile*; 'Verhältnis' = relation."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-094":["b) a *quantum omni assignatione majus*, or",["'assignatione' normalized from the OCR 'assignatione'; kept Latin."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-095":["c) *minus*, i.e. an infinitely large or infinitely small magnitude: hence they also signify *quantum infinitum* or *infinito-parvum*, and thus, since both are respectively larger, respectively smaller than every magnitude that can be given, both are not *quanta assignabilia*, and cannot be given.",["bedeuten = signify; 'anzugebende' rendered 'that can be given' (angeben, as against 'gegeben' = 'given'); both rendered 'given' in English.","'minus' stands as in the text, presumably *quantum minus omni assignatione*, not completed."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-096":["8.) The ground why a magnitude is not finite is called a negation, that negation on account of which a *Quantum* is not the *maximum* can be called either limitation or boundary. Only an object of reason is called limited, but an object of intuition bounded: just so also unlimited and unbounded, according as it is an object of mere reason or of the senses.",["Einschränkung / Beschränkung = limitation (limited, 'beschränkt'); Begränzung / Gränze = boundary (bounded, 'begränzt'); Schranke = limitation. Both Einschränkung and Beschränkung render as 'limitation'; the glossary's Grenze = bound(ary) is distinguished only by 'boundary'.","Gegenstand and Object both = 'object'; here Gegenstand (twice) and Object (once).","Grund = ground; Vernunft = reason; Anschauung = intuition; Sinne = senses."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-097":["Limitations therefore differ from boundaries. A being of the understanding (*noumenon*) has only limitations (*limites*), i.e. the negation is thought merely according to the understanding; hence here is limitation: thus a *Quantum noumenon* is magnitude thought through the understanding, and limited insofar as it is not the *maximum*, and limitation, *limes*, is called the negation on account of which it is limited. E.g. boundaries or limitations of human understanding, of the will. —",["Schranken = limitations; Gränzen = boundaries; Wesen = being; Verstand = understanding; Einschränkung and 'eingeschränkt' = limitation, limited.","'Verstandes Wesen' kept as 'being of the understanding'; OCR 'menschlichenVerstandes' read as 'menschlichen Verstandes'.","In the final example the German has 'Gränzen oder Schranken' ('boundaries or limitations'); kept in that order."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-098":["A *Quantum phaenomenon*, on the other hand, is bounded when the determination of its magnitude, or the negation, belongs at the same time to the *intuitus* of the same, or = when the ground of the limitation at the same time contains something positive in space.",["The OCR has an equals sign ('oder =') which is kept as '='. Begränzt = bounded; Beschränkung = limitation; Grund = ground; Raum = space.","'deßelben' (of the same) refers to the *Quantum phaenomenon*, or perhaps to magnitude; left ambiguous."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-099":["The positive, through which the limitations of intuition are determined, is called the boundary — The point in space has for itself no negation, is also not bounded, it is merely positive, and indicates the place whither something restricts itself.",["Schranke = limitation; Gränze = boundary (Begränzung = bounding); Einschränkung/einschränken = restriction/restrict (not in glossary). Anschauung = intuition; determinirt = determined; Ort = place."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-100":["The line receives its boundary through the point, the surface through the line and the body through the surface. These are also the three dimensions of space, if one thinks it completely as a part of a greater space for itself, and then corporeal content, surface and length each again have their space. Bounding and boundary are thus always something positive in the appearance, but not restriction.",["Erscheinung = appearance; Einschränckung = restriction (distinct from Schranke = limitation). OCR 'großem' read as 'größerem' (greater). Fläche = surface; Inhalt = content."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-101":["9) *Quantum est vel continuum vel discretum.* *Quantum continuum* is a continuous magnitude, of which all its parts are in turn *quanta*, however small or however large they may be.",["Größe = magnitude; Theile = parts."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-102":["It can therefore not contain simple parts. By contrast the composition is constituted thus: *quod sunt partes per se indeterminatae*, i.e. in itself, and from the magnitude itself it cannot be determined how many parts one is to assume, but according to our cognition it remains arbitrary.",["'d. i. an sich selbst' rendered 'i.e. in itself' (an sich); Erkenntniß = cognition; willkührlich = arbitrary. The antecedent of 'Sie' and of the Latin 'quod' is left as in the German."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-103":["To the *quanta continua* there now belong space and time. For every part of space and time cannot be thought otherwise than as again a space or time, and the division proceeds up to the point and the instant: only both of these latter are not parts of space and of time.",["Eintheilung = division; Augenblick = instant."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-104":["A point is only the positive, the boundary of a space, and the instant is just the same with regard to time.",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-105":["From this it follows that space and time do not consist of simple parts. A circumstance that, if the opposite were correct, would prove that space and time would be something objective in the next-to-one-another and after-one-another, but which, since one can come upon no simple part, proves that space and time lie only in the merely subjective of the form of intuition.",["objectives = objective; 'im neben einander und nach einander' kept as an awkward nominalized phrase; 'in dem bloß subjectiven der Form der Anschauung' kept ambiguous as in the German."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-106":["The point in space is indivisible and it is also not, as said, a part of space: hence a space of nothing but points would be a space that would consist of nothing but boundaries, consequently would be a nothing. The point is merely a spot in space, but up to there space, like time up to the instant, is divisible, consequently it is impossible to assume simple parts in space and time.",["Stelle = spot (distinct from Ort = place). 'bis dahin ... bis zum Augenblick' kept elliptical as in the German."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-107":["A point cannot be thought before the representation of space, from which it follows that space cannot consist of points. Space fills the matter as *extensum*, *extensum*, however, is *id, in quo est spatium*. The concepts are thus *correlata* with matter, space itself makes it that matter is extended: thus matter can consist of no other parts than space itself has, consequently it is impossible that matter could consist of simple parts.",["Vorstellung = representation; Begriffe = concepts; Materie = matter; ausgedehnt = extended (cf. Ausdehnung = extension). The sentence 'Der Raum füllt die Materie aus als extensum' is kept as it stands (the German is obscure)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-108":["A *quantum discretum*, by contrast, is a *quantum* that consists of units, i.e. of a determinate multitude of parts that I think as units. This is the concept of number, consequently magnitude expressed through numbers is *quantum discretum*. E.g. a *quantum* of florins: here it cannot be divided further without the unit = florin ceasing entirely, consequently the parts do not contain *quanta* again. As this would be thought of a *quantum* of silver in general in Schotten, ounces etc.; the latter can be divided into *quanta* and is thus *quantum continuum*.",["Gulden = florins; Menge = multitude; Zahl = number. 'Schotten' left untranslated; the reading is doubtful (possibly a misreading, perhaps of a unit of weight), and the sentence 'So wie dies ... gedacht seyn würde' is kept as obscure as it stands."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-109":["The *quantum discretum* can be regarded as *per se discretum*, because it consists of simple parts, i.e. because its units cannot again be considered as *quanta*, but they must be expressed through a number, in order to exhibit the quantity, i.e. in order to know how great the *quantum* is.",["Quantitaet = quantity; Größe appears as 'great' (wie groß)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-110":["10.) *Quantum* 1",["Fragmentary and garbled in the OCR (apparently a heading broken off); translated as it stands."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-111":["*Quantitas* is *vel rationale vel irrationale*. Rationale is *quantum computabile*, i.e. that magnitude which is to be calculated, or can be expressed in numbers; irrational, by contrast, is the *quantum finitum*, but *incommensurabile*, i.e. that which is not to be calculated, or whose relation to the measure cannot be expressed through numbers. It only approaches the determination that the measure gives, e.g. the relation of the root of 1 to √2, which obtains between the diagonal line and the two sides, is immeasurable.",["The OCR of the opening ('Quantitas j lst Vel rationale>') is garbled; read as 'Quantitas ist vel rationale vel irrationale'. 'j1 zu |/2' read as 'root of 1 to √2'; 'Lime' read as 'Linie'. Verhältniß = relation; Bestimmung = determination; unmeßbar = immeasurable."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-112":["These latter magnitudes in space and time are given to intuition only between the boundaries of space and time.",["The stray '&' at the end of the OCR line is omitted as an apparatus/OCR mark."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-113":["11.) A *quantum maximum*, like a *quantum minimum*, cannot be thought of an *ens phaenomenon*, and only to an *ens noumenon* — God — can a representation of it be attributed. For *quantum maximum* would be the totality, the magnitude thought without restriction. The like is just as little thinkable for human understanding as that magnitude whose still smaller measure would be as it were a nothing. E.g. totality of space and of time, which would be no part of a still greater space and time, is capable of no subjective intuition. The *phaenomenon* one can think only as a plurality, and under this concept a totality.",["Latin ablatives *ente phaenomeno*, *ente noumeno* in the original, given in nominative in the translation. Allheit = totality; Vielheit = plurality; Einschränkung = restriction; Verstand = understanding. OCR 'großem' read as 'größeren'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-114":["For *phaenomena* there is thus no *quantum absolute maximum*, but only ever *relative maximum*, e.g. *chorda maxima in circulo*, and just as little a *quantum absolute minimum*. For all parts, even the smallest representable, have *quanta* again, consequently here too the representation is directed only to a *relation*.",["OCR 'minimuni' read as 'minimum'. 'immer relative maximum' kept as in the German (Latin adjective form as printed)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-115":["On the concept of the relation of things, according to which we can cognize their magnitude only, rests the concept, different from that of the *quantum*, of *magnitudo*, *parvitas*. These involve the determination of the *quantum* or of quantity, according to which one calls something great (*magnum*) or small (*parvum*).",["Größe and *magnitudo* are both rendered 'magnitude'; the Latin is left in Latin. erkennen = cognize. 'die Größe derselben nur erkennen' kept ambiguous as to the placement of 'only'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-116":["If one assumes the representation that some things in the universe would become ever smaller, and all the remainder by contrast remained unchanged, one represents a relation in which the smaller remain to the greater, e.g. our earth, which one calls a *globus terraqueus*, were so flooded by water that only a magnitude of the pea remained, then the alteration would also with respect to magnitude be representable through comparison. But if one presupposes that the whole universe has altered, then no further comparison is possible, and thus also no alteration of magnitude thinkable; the smallest terrestrial globe would be equally great as the largest with regard to our subjective representation, because we could not compare it with a third object.",["Veränderung = alteration; Gegenstand = object. The subjunctives and the construction 'würde ... überschwemmt, daß ... bliebe, so wäre' are kept as in the German. OCR 'großem' read as 'größeren'; 'globum' given in nominative. Erdkugel = terrestrial globe."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-117":["The concept of magnitude would thus remain in itself the same, consequently this is absolute, but the *magnitudo* or *parvitas* can never be cognized absolutely, but only through *relation*.",["an sich = in itself. 'dieser' (consequently this is absolute) left without a noun, as in the German."]],"metaphysik-k3-s04-118":["From the fact now that we are unable to determine the magnitude of things in and before themselves, it now follows that we also cannot represent them to ourselves in and before themselves, but only as they appear to us. Space and the things that occupy space determine themselves thus merely according to the laws of sensibility, to which they are set in relation. Space and time are therefore also not thinkable through the understanding and therefore determinate concepts of the understanding, but are merely the subjective form under which things appear to us. They do not belong for intuition through sensibility and not for the understanding as concept of the understanding.",["'an und vor sich selbst' = 'in and before themselves' (literal). 'außer Stande' = 'unable'. Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Gesetze = laws; Beziehung = relation. The sentence 'nicht durch den Verstand und daher bestimmte Verstandes Begriffen denkbar' is syntactically irregular in the transcript and is kept so. The last sentence is kept obscure as in the German."]],"metaphysik-k3-s05-001":["Category of Quality",["Categorie = category; Qualitaet = quality. A heading."]],"metaphysik-k3-s05-002":["22.) Quality, also applied to objects, relates, like the corresponding logical function of quality, to judgments that contain the condition of the quality of a thing that is to be determined. Space and time contain no quality of a thing in themselves, i.e. they have no transcendental matter or an object in general in themselves; they are only the formal condition of our intuition. A Something, by contrast, that lies in the concept of being, existence, existence, belongs to the quality of the thing, which accordingly presupposes something material. Quality now has, as a function, a threefold object, i.e. the representation of a thing .... . Being involves",["Seyn = being; Daseyn and existenz both rendered 'existence' (Dasein / Existenz, glossary); the German has the two words side by side, hence 'existence, existence'.","Qualitaet is rendered 'quality' (Latin qualitas); Gegenstand = 'object' and Object = 'object' (Latinate member marked here); 'Etwas' capitalized as in the source.","'Function' kept as the cognate of Funktion; 'transcendentale Materie' = 'transcendental matter'.","The dots after 'eines Dinges' are in the source (OCR), apparently a gap or abbreviation; not filled. The passage breaks off at 'Seyn involvirt', continued in the next passage ('2.) Negation').","Ambiguity kept: 'so in dem Begriff von Seyn ... liegt' – 'so' is rendered 'that', as a relative; it could also be read as 'as it lies'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s05-003":["2.) Negation, i.e. the representation of a thing as something that contains a non-being.",["Nichtseyn = 'non-being'; Negation left as the cognate.","Item '1.)' (Realität) is missing here; the passage's 'Seyn involvirt' in the preceding fragment presumably leads in to it."]],"metaphysik-k3-s05-004":["3) Limitation, i.e. the representation of a thing whose being is affected by the non-being of the same, accordingly whose concept contains a being and non-being combined. E.g. light is reality; darkness is negation; shadow is limitation, for it is a darkness that is bounded by light. So too acquaintances — ignorance — restricted knowing.",["Kenntniße = 'acquaintances' (glossary: Kenntnis = acquaintance; information; knowledge); the sense in this series is doubtful, so the first glossary rendering is kept.","Wißen = 'knowing' (verbal noun, not Wissen = knowledge (scientia) as a term of the glossary); realitaet = 'reality'.","begränzt = 'bounded' (Grenze = bound(ary)); eingeschränkt = 'restricted' (cf. Schranke = limitation, which is not used here, since Limitation is Latin in the text).","verbunden = 'combined' (Verbindung = combination)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s05-005":["It is a positive Something, *in opposito* with *negatio*. Something or combined.",["The sentence is fragmentary in the transcript and probably corrupt ('Etwas oder Verbunden'); translated as it stands, nothing supplied.","The Latin in opposito, Negatio are kept in italics; the capital N of Negatio is in the source, here lowercased in the italic rendering."]],"metaphysik-k3-s05-006":["1) Reality of a thing is, according to the verbal sense, the thingness of the thing, accordingly something positive in itself.",["Wortverstande = 'verbal sense' (literally 'understanding of the word'); Verstand elsewhere = 'understanding', so this is a literal rendering of the compound.","Sachheit = 'thingness' (Sache/Ding not distinguished in English; Ding = 'thing' in the glossary); an sich = 'in itself'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s05-007":["2) In order to give the concept of reality significance, an object of sensibility must be placed under it, so that it can thereby be documented, and just as the category of magnitude corresponded to the concept of space and time as a something in intuition, in order to determine them to quantity, so too in the representation quantity is necessary, only however as that which comprises the sensation, or the real is something sensible in intuition, accordingly an object of sensation is required, in order to represent to oneself the magnitude of the real: not so in the case of quantity, where pure intuition, i.e. without object in space or in time, was present. Thus e.g. a thought has quantity as intuition in time, a bright head however reality, on account of the subjective sensations of its representations.",["Bedeutung = 'significance' (bedeuten/Bedeutung); Größe = 'magnitude', Quantitaet = 'quantity' (Latinate member marked: Größe and Quantität are distinct words and distinct in English here).","belegt = 'documented' is doubtful; belegen (to cover, to substantiate with evidence) – literal rendering chosen.","Empfindung = 'sensation'; empfindbar = 'sensible' avoided because of sinnlich = 'sensible'; rendered 'sensible' in the text, a collision with sinnlich which is flagged here: empfindbar and sinnlich are both 'sensible'.","The tense shift 'correspondirte' (past subjunctive/indicative) and 'war' are preserved; the clause 'nur aber als dasjenige was die Empfindung umfaßt, oder das reale ist ...' is run-on in the transcript and kept so.","'Realität' (here realitaet) = 'reality'; 'das reale' = 'the real'; 'heller Kopf' = 'bright head'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s05-008":["3) The real has a degree of sensation — or in the sensible of the sensible representation. The sensation is opposed to the formal in intuition, and the sensible then signifies the material in the transcendental sense, accordingly here the formal intuition in space and time is opposed to the real as sensible. Here, then, to mere intuition sensation is added: an intuition with sensation, however, is perception. This, however, requires an object of perception. Perception of an object is consciousness of the object through sensation — in the way, then, this intuition of the real differs entirely from the pure.",["empfindbar and sinnlich both appear and both come out as 'sensible' (Empfindung = sensation; sinnlich = sensible); the difference is marked here, not in the text.","formell/materiale: 'formal' and 'material', *in sensu transcendentali* kept in Latin and italics in the German; here rendered 'in the transcendental sense' (the Latin phrase is in the source, so it should stay Latin: read *in sensu transcendentali*).","Wahrnehmung = 'perception'; Bewußtseyn = 'consciousness'; 'Art' = 'way'.","'Empfindüng' and 'Wahrnehnehmung' are OCR slips, silently corrected.","'in dem empfindbaren der sinnlichen Vorstellung' preserved as obscure."]],"metaphysik-k3-s05-009":["One calls this degree of sensation intensive magnitude, in order to distinguish it from extensive magnitude in the case of quantity, and says: everything sensible has intensive magnitude, or = a degree of sensation: that is to say: it can be represented that from zero = null = which has no sensation at all, the sensation can rise, or from a certain measure decrease again to = 0 =. But a magnitude is understood in which the parts are not cognized beforehand in order to determine the magnitude, but it must be cognized as unity, and the parts drawn out of the unity. Thus e.g. a line, which must be put together, differs from a fading light: in the latter there is only a unity of sensation, but in every following state another degree of the same.",["Größe = 'magnitude', intensive/extensive Größe; Quantitaet = 'quantity'; Einheit = 'unity'; Grad = 'degree'; Zustand = 'state'; erkannt = 'cognized' (erkennen).","'Zero = Null' kept as in the source; 'Maaße' = 'measure'.","The sentence 'Es wird aber eine Größe verstanden ...' has an impersonal passive; 'verstanden' = 'understood' (verstehen)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s05-010":["The author calls the degree of sensation = the magnitude of quality: this determination does express that a something is thought as posited in the thing, but one determines the degree better through: magnitude of unity, i.e. the representation of an object, insofar as I think its magnitude (*quantitatem*) as unity, gives the magnitude the degree. Here, then, the magnitude is given not as multiplicity, but as unity, and then distinguishes itself exactly from extensive magnitude (*quantitas extensiva*, and therefore in the consequences different from *quantitas intensiva*). Hence e.g. a drop of boiling water is indeed less than a kettle full, but both are equally hot. The unity that is here perceived in the sensation of the object shows, then, that it rests on the equality of the ground, and the unity of the ground makes the manifold of the same kind quite dispensable in the consideration of reality. Thus the angle is equally large, however far the lines or legs may extend: it is made by the equality of the inclination, and only their difference also only a difference of intensive magnitude, since this reduces itself merely to the existence of the motion in large and small angles. An enlivening thought, which however gradually reverses its impression, has unity, which however is different in every step of the decrease.",["Größe = 'magnitude' throughout; Vielheit = 'multiplicity' (Menge also = 'multiplicity'/'multitude'); mannigfaltige = 'manifold'; wahrgenommen = 'perceived' (Wahrnehmung).","Folgen = 'consequences' (Folge = sequel; sequence; Folgerung = consequence): 'in den Folgen' rendered 'consequences' as a choice between these.","'Eindruck' = 'impression'; 'Grund' = 'ground'; 'Betrachtung' = 'consideration'; 'existenz' = 'existence'.","'Autor' = 'author' (Baumgarten, presumably; not specified in the text).","'GrößederEinheit' (OCR run-together) corrected to 'Größe der Einheit'; 'wenngleich'/'es macht die Gleichheit ... und nur deren Verschiedenheit auch nur ...' is syntactically broken in the transcript and kept awkward.","'verkehrt' = 'reverses' is doubtful (could be 'perverts', 'turns about'); 'unterscheidt' = 'distinguishes itself' (reflexive in German 'sich unterscheidt')."]],"metaphysik-k3-s05-011":["4.) it follows from this now that the real, since it has its ground in sensation, accordingly in the object of the senses, cannot take place in the merely intellectual, accordingly the degree of the real cannot be thought either as maximum or as minimum. On the other hand it is certain that the alteration of the degree of intensive magnitude of the real quality must be infinite, even if it can be imperceptible. Hence between the determinate degree A and 0 = zero there must be found an infinite multitude of qualities of the real, in a degree, even if imperceptible; e.g. knowing, representations, indeed even the consciousness of the human being have many degrees, without one being able to determine the smallest.",["reelle / reale rendered 'real'; Realität = 'reality'; Menge = 'multitude'; bestimmt = 'determinate' / 'determine' (bestimmen).","'ün blos' is an OCR slip for 'im blos' (read 'in the merely intellectual'); 'müßcn' corrected to 'müssen'.","'Wißen' = 'knowing'; 'Bewußtseyn' = 'consciousness'; Grad = 'degree'; Abänderung = 'alteration'.","'maximum', 'minimum', 'zero' are Latin/foreign in the source and given as in the text."]],"metaphysik-k3-s05-012":["5.) One uses the word reality in a double sense",["Verstände here = 'sense' (not Verstand = understanding); literal sense of 'in doppeltem Verstände'.","Passage ends without a period, continues with '1.) adjective ...'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s05-013":["1.) adjectively, and then it signifies only the form of the object, is accordingly applied *formaliter*, and indeed it can then be used only *in singulari*. E.g. representations, concepts have objective reality. Magnitude is here reality and belongs to the form of the concept, insofar as it has an object,",["'adjective' (adverbial use in Latin-derived German) rendered 'adjectively'; *formaliter* and *in singulari* kept in Latin.","Bedeutet = 'signifies' (bedeuten); Begriff = 'concept'; Vorstellung = 'representation'; Form = 'form'; Object = 'object' (Latinate member)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s05-014":["2.) or substantively, and then the reality relates to the material of the object and is usable only *in plurali*, because the realities of the thing are considered in itself.",["'substantive' = 'substantively'; *in plurali* kept; 'an sich selbst' = 'in itself'.","Materielle = 'material' (n.), Materie = matter in the glossary; here the substantivized adjective is kept as 'the material'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s05-015":["Thus differently is the word unity also used in the German, Greek or Latin language.",["Einheit = 'unity'; 'Thus differently' follows the German 'So verschieden', word order kept."]],"metaphysik-k3-s05-016":["Namely adjectively, e.g. one says: unity of the play, of the presentation, truth of the proposition, of the perfection of the thing. It signifies the point of view which one places under it in regard to the object. Substantively, by contrast, or unities of the things themselves, i.e. *unitates quantitative spectatae*, are named only *in plurali*. Thus *quantitas* is usable only formally and *in singulari*, *quanta* by contrast *in plurali*, for these go to the quality of the object itself, but those only to the form of an object in general.",["Satz = 'proposition'; Vollkommenheit = 'perfection'; 'Object' = 'object'; Qualitaet = 'quality'; Vortrag = 'presentation' (doubtful: 'delivery', 'lecture').","'der Vollkommenheit des Dinges' is in the genitive after 'Wahrheit des Satzes' as in the transcript (possibly a slip for 'Vollkommenheit'); kept.","'Gesichtspunkt' = 'point of view' (literally 'point of sight'); 'unterlegt' = 'places under'.","'unitates quantitative spectatae' kept as in the transcript (one would expect 'quantitate'); the Latin is in italics here.","'jene' / 'diese' = 'those' / 'these' kept as in German; referents not resolved."]],"metaphysik-k3-s05-017":["6.) One also calls all realities perfections, i.e. every single perfection in the object itself, (accordingly this expression also differs from the perfection of things as a genus-concept in general). Objectively, finally, reality consists in thingness or something positive — and that which is positive is perfect — and an object must have something positive, and can have much positive or perfections. An *ens mere negativum*, i.e. one that would have nothing positive at all, is a straight contradiction, for even the being of the thing already involves something positive, otherwise it would be, even if this were negative, no thing, since here the object is considered only materially; for formally an *ens mere negativum* can at least be thought without contradiction. Every thing must therefore have reality, and an *ens realissimum*, which one also calls *ens perfectissimum*, *summum*, is that whose sum total of determinations are realities, accordingly in which there is nothing negative.",["Gattungs Begriff = 'genus-concept' (Gattung = genus); Widerspruch = 'contradiction'; Vollkommenheit = 'perfection'; Ausdruck = 'expression'; Sachheit = 'thingness'.","'sämtliche determinationes' = 'sum total of determinations' (Inbegriff is not in the German; 'sämtlich' = 'all'; the choice 'sum total' is a gloss, 'all' is the literal reading).","'und muß ein Object etwas positives' lacks a verb in the transcript ('haben' supplied from the following clause by the German construction); kept.","'ens mere negativum', 'ens realissimum', 'ens perfectissimum', 'summum' kept in Latin; 'Objective' = 'objectively'; 'formaliter' rendered 'formally'.","'Ding' = 'thing'; 'Object' = 'object' (Latinate member)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s05-018":["*In sensu transcendentali.* Metaphysics sees, then, that here merely the concept of non-being, hence an analytic cognition of the thing, is laid at the basis, yet in such a way that the thing still remains thinkable, hence is not subject to the *principium contradictionis*; *Realiter opposita* are, however, *contrario opposita* and must be investigated according to synthetic *principiis* of the ground to the sequel, *sive rationis ponentis non tollentis*.",["Metaphysic = \"Metaphysics\"; Erkenntniß = \"cognition\"; Grund = \"ground\"; Folge = \"sequel\" (glossary).","\"sieht also\": \"also\" rendered \"then\"; it could also be read as \"thus\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s05-019":["The Wolffian philosophy called, because reality makes up the material of the thing, negation the formal of things. Negation would also in the case of the things themselves make up nothing more than the formal, whether it may arise from a *contrarie* or *contradictorie oppositum*, if one could reduce the *oppositi realitates* (namely not in a merely logical sense) to a negation. E.g. the Wolffians say, sin consists merely in the omission of the good, or absence of the good, hence it is *formale* the not good, they consider therefore the *contrarie oppositum* of the latter as negation. It involves therefore the idea of the *mali metaphysici*. Since in objects of appearance *realia* and *negativa* are found mixed, a negation, *quatenus ens non est ens realissimum*.",["Materiale (\"the material\") is not Stoff (\"material\") or Materie (\"matter\"); Formale rendered \"the formal\".","Realität/realitaet = \"reality\"; Erscheinung = \"appearance\"; Object = \"object\".","The last sentence has no main clause in the German (\"Da ... eine Negation, quatenus ...\"); left as it stands, possibly broken off or corrupt.","\"Philosophie\" (not Weltweisheit) rendered \"philosophy\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s06-001":["Category of the dynamical class",["Singular \"Categorie\" kept, although the OCR heading may have lost a plural ending."]],"metaphysik-k3-s06-002":["23.) There shows itself in the logical relation of the category of this class to that of the mathematical class a difference therein: The categories of relation and modality carry along with them nothing but *correlata*, which are placed next to one another in such a way that *posito uno ponitur* [*et*?] *alter*, e.g. substances refer to *accidentia et v. v.*, cause to effect, just as interaction directly on one another: in the case of the category of quantity and quality they were, however, single, subordinated to one another, and could not be placed next to one another. E.g. unity takes no reference to plurality, but in plurality lies unity, and together they again make up totality. Just as little are reality and negation compatible next to one another, whereas the modalities in their *correlatis* cancel one another: possible — not possible, actual — not actual, necessary — contingent, *contrarie opposita*.",["\"est\" in \"posito uno ponitur est alter\" is doubtful in the OCR (perhaps \"et\"); left as read, with a bracketed query inserted by me instead of a reading.","\"Unter -schied\" and the stray line numbers 5, 9/10 treated as OCR/apparatus.","Verhältniß and Relation both \"relation\"; Beziehung/sich beziehen = \"reference\"/\"refer\"; wirklich = \"actual\"; Vielheit = \"plurality\"; Allheit = \"totality\"; zufällig = \"contingent\".","\"Es zeigt sich ... darin ein Unterschied\" kept in German order; \"darin\" could also be \"in this\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s06-003":["The categories of relation in particular have the cognition of the things themselves in their relation to the object, the categories of modality by contrast go only to the cognition of the concept of the thing to the whole faculty of cognition.",["Verhältniß = \"relation\" (also Relation); Gegenstand = \"object\"; Erkenntniß Vermögen = \"faculty of cognition\".","\"zu dem ganzen Erkenntniß Vermögen\": construction left as in the German."]],"metaphysik-k3-s06-004":["24.) In the categories of relation in particular, then, the realities are considered against one another as relations, i.e. the relations of things with respect to their existence, in that a *reale* of the thing nevertheless always involves the concept of being, and indeed according to the threefold *modi* of existence in the series of logical judgments, namely: assertoric hypothetical disjunctive, is grounded on relation of the *antecedens* to the relation",["The end (\"gründet sich auf Relation des antecedens zum Verhältniß\") is syntactically unconnected and looks garbled or cut off; translated as it stands.","Existenz/existenz = \"existence\"; Ansehung = \"respect\"; Urtheile = \"judgments\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s06-006":["Hereby they are therefore distinguished from the categories of the -lity above, where this was indeed compared in various ways, without taking regard of the relation of several.",["\"lität\" is a fragment in the OCR (probably Qualität or Quantität); not completed.","\"dieser\"/\"diese\" and \"this\" are as uncertain as the OCR reading; the antecedent is unclear."]],"metaphysik-k3-s06-007":["25.) The first category is the relation of substance to *accidens*.",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s06-008":["One calls this relation, with respect to substance, *subsistentia*, with respect to the *accidens*, *inhaerentia*. *Inhaerentia* is an *interna determinatio substantive*, thus a determination that pertains to substance by inhering, hence substance is one and the same with that, *quod ei inhaeret*. E.g. the human being (substance) thinks, *est id quod homini tamquam substantiae ejus inhaerendo competit*. The expression dependence, which one, because there is no better, uses of the *caussatis ratione causa* in the relation of causality, cannot be confused with inherence. The latter also depends on substance, but now as necessary inner determination of the substance, causality by contrast does not have dependence necessarily in itself. E.g. the thought in the human being is an essential determination, but the warmth of the stone from sunlight does not inhere in the latter, is also not a necessary connection with the stone, but exists as something distinguished from the substance. The sound does not inhere in the speaker, nor in the listener *substantialiter*, but subsists in a way subsisting by itself. So it is also with interaction.",["\"substantive\" and \"caussatis ratione causa\" are kept as printed (probably for substantiae, causae); possibly OCR or the transcriber's Latin.","Bestimmung = \"determination\"; Ausdruck = \"expression\"; Verknüpfung = \"connection\"; Wechselwirkung = \"interaction\"; Mensch = \"human being\".","\"auf eine für sich bestehende Art\": bestehen rendered \"subsist\" to echo subsistiren in the same clause."]],"metaphysik-k3-s06-009":["There are actually 3 categories of relation:",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s06-010":["1.) the relation of inherence to subsistence",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s06-011":["2.) the relation of *causalitas* to dependence",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s06-012":["3.) of community, i.e. that two substances have in themselves at the same time the determination of the *causae* and *caußati*.",["Gemeinschaft = \"community\"; \"caußati\" kept as printed (causati)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s06-013":["Hence one lays at the basis in the case of relation the consideration of a thing, partly as subject, and insofar as it cannot be a predicate of another, partly as ground of another thing, partly insofar as it is reciprocally determined with another thing.",["Betrachtung = \"consideration\"; Grund = \"ground\"; \"zum Grund legen\" rendered \"lay at the basis\"; \"wechselsweise\" = \"reciprocally\" (also \"alternately\")."]],"metaphysik-k3-s06-014":["The concept of substance requires a preeminent exposition, since in it lies a source of cognition of many metaphysical truths.",["Erörterung = \"exposition\"; \"Erkenntniß Quelle\" = \"source of cognition\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s06-015":["Substance is that which can exist without being a predicate of another thing (*quod potest esse, etiam si non existit tamquam determinatio alterius*).",["The Latin is kept as the transcript has it (\"eße\", \"tan quam\" silently normalized); the sense of \"esse ... non existit\" is left as it stands."]],"metaphysik-k3-s06-016":["*Accidens* by contrast is that which cannot exist without being a predicate of another thing. Hence",["Breaks off at \"Daher\"; not completed."]],"metaphysik-k3-s06-017":["1.) Substance and *accidens* are each something real, since their concept contains a being and the existence of the thing, be it substance or *accidens*, is necessary thereto. A thing, therefore, whose concept contains a non-being (negation) can be neither substance nor *accidens*, precisely because reality and existence is lacking to it.",["\"Seyn\" = \"being\"; \"Nicht-seyn\" = \"non-being\"; the singular verb \"mangelt\" after two subjects is kept (\"is lacking\")."]],"metaphysik-k3-s06-018":["E.g. body is real",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s06-019":["Motion is real",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s06-020":["Rest by contrast is negation, hence it can be no *accidens* of a body. One therefore calls the existence of the substance the subsistence, and the existence of the *accidens* of the same inherence, i.e. the *accidens* belongs to the substance as positive real predicate in such a way that it inheres in it.",["\"derselben\" (\"of the same\") could refer to the substance or to the accidens's substance; left ambiguous.","\"accidentis\" in the German is a Latin genitive; rendered \"*accidens*\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-001":["Scholion.",["Spaced OCR letters normalized."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-002":["The Scholastics divided the *accidens sive inhaerens* into 1.) *praedicabile*, and understood by that the *accidens* of the essence; hence *accidens praedicabile* = the contingent. For essence of a thing is that which necessarily belongs to the concept of a thing: hence, since the *accidens* was the *oppositum* insofar, it was = the extra-essential *extraessentiale*.",["Wesen = \"essence\" (glossary also \"being\"); zufällig/Zufällige = \"contingent\"; \"außerwesentlich\" = \"extra-essential\".","\"das accidens des Wesens\": \"of the essence\" kept as the German."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-003":["2.) *praedicamentale*, by which they understood *accidens* of the substance, hence it was something real positive, inhering in the substance; both are also distinguished by this, that *praedicabile accidens* lies only in the concept and was there, the *praedicamentale* however exists, and is predicated of a real. E.g. the figure of a square was an *accidens praedicabile*, it contains no existence, but rests only on mere form or way of representation of the square, in the case of which it was wholly indifferent with respect to matter.",["\"Plazzes\" (Platz) rendered \"square\"; Platz is not Ort (\"place\"); it could also be read as \"place\" or a geometrical square.","\"esetwas\" (OCR) read as \"es etwas\"; \"und da war\" kept as is (obscure).","Vorstellungs Art = \"way of representation\"; Materie = \"matter\"; Form = \"form\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-004":["Heaviness of the body by contrast is *accidens praedicamentale*, for it exists inhering in the body. So also warmth, cold are also *res existentes inhaerendo* as *accidentia* of the same, the body itself however exists with regard to them *subsistendo*.",["Schwere = \"heaviness\"; \"deßelben\" = \"of the same\" (the body); \"accidentien\" rendered as the Latin plural *accidentia*."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-005":["2.) The substance, thought with the omission of all inhering accidents (i.e. determinations of the same), is called *substantiale*. This remainder is a mere concept that has no determination. It is a something, which is merely thought, or representable, for the *substantiale* cannot be cognized. One can cognize nothing if one does not have predicates of the object, by which one cognizes something, since all cognitions occur only through judgments. Here, however, there remains only the subject *absque praedicato*, hence no relation between the two. Thus there remains only a representation of a something, of which one does not cognize, however, what it is. E.g. if one abstracts from body all *accidentia* such as divisibility, impenetrability, extensibility, there remains a figure, which is a shape in space, but which does not exist, because a space, such as it nonetheless is, cannot exist for itself (*subsistendo*), and since space is thought only through bodies as *inhaerens*, nothing substantial that exists remains.",["Bestimmung = determination (\"Bestimmung derselben\" is singular in German); Erkenntnis/erkennen = cognition/cognize; Urteil = judgment; Verhältniß = relation; Vorstellung = representation.","\"Es ist ein Etwas, so blos gedacht, oder vorstellbar ist\": the relative \"so\" is rendered \"which\"; the German construction is loose and the English follows it as closely as it can.","\"ein Raum, dergleichen sie doch ist\": \"sie\" could refer to Figur/Gestalt; \"dergleichen\" is kept vague (\"such as it nonetheless is\"). Reference of \"it\" left open as in the German.","Dehnbarkeit rendered \"extensibility\" to keep it apart from Ausdehnung = extension."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-006":["Thus thinking, willing, feeling of pleasure and displeasure are *praedicata* of the human soul. If one leaves these out, and thinks the soul to oneself without these *inhaerentia*, there remains a something of which one has no concept, a thought without thinking subjects, and this is *substantiale*. One also calls it the *substratum* of all accidents. To cognize objects in this is, as said, impossible for the human understanding, and one makes a useless complaint about its limitedness, by virtue of which the understanding can cognize only through the effects, but not the objects in themselves and in their substance. It lies in the quality of the faculty of understanding that we can cognize only through predicates, which however are here left out, and thus all cognition without combination of the accidents with the substance is impossible",["The Academy's editorial note that the text breaks off at the end has been left out as apparatus; the passage ends without a full stop.","Eingeschränktheit rendered \"limitedness\" (cf. Schranke = limitation); Verbindung = combination; Wirkung = effect; Gefühl = feeling.","\"Objecte\"/\"Objeckte\" (OCR/spelling) = object; \"der Verstand ... erkennen können\": the singular subject with plural verb is as printed.","\"Hieran Objecte zu erkennen\": \"Hieran\" rendered \"in this\"; the reference is left open."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-007":["*phaenomena substantiata*, an expression of Leibniz (*ad autorem* P. 193), means in general nothing more than the substance considered as phenomenon, or reality as determination in space and in time. All substances are cognized and considered by us insofar as they let themselves be determined in space and time; we cannot cognize their predicates in themselves, but only insofar as they stand in relation with the form of our sensibility. Hence *substantiae noumenon* cannot be cognized, because the object corresponding to the concept is lacking in intuition. Hence, since the *substantialia* do not exist in themselves, we cannot cognize the substances in themselves, but only through their inhering accidents; e.g. through the representation of I nothing can be cognized of the subject without attaching a predicate to it. It serves only as a designation of the representation of a being that makes itself into object. Through observation of myself only, when I direct my attention to inner sense, which just as much as outer sense can be made to present itself as *phaenomenon*. If one therefore cognizes the substances according to their determinations in space and in time, and holds these determinations for the thing itself, then in this illusion one mixes the concept of the substances with that of the *phaenomenis substantiatis*.",["\"Gegenstand\" (\"der korrespondierende Gegenstand\") and \"Objekt\" are both rendered \"object\".","\"Bestimmung\" = determination; \"Schein\" = illusion; \"Sinnlichkeit\" = sensibility; \"Beobachtung\" = observation; \"Anschauung\" = intuition; \"Ding\" = thing.","OCR \"innem\" read as \"innern\" (inner). \"phaenomenis substantiatis\" is kept as printed (the case is not that of the German construction).","\"Durch Beobachtung meiner selbst nur, wenn ...\" is a fragment with no finite verb in the main clause; kept as such. The Academy's \"bricht ab\" is omitted as apparatus."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-008":["Since the world is an aggregate of many substances connected among themselves, i.e. a real whole, as soon as one considers it in relation to the senses in space and time, there can be of worlds, as *mundus phaenomenon*, because of the unity of the unbounded space, only One world. According to form the world is therefore a *totum substantiale quod non est pars alterius* . . .",["Verknüpft = connected (Verknüpfung = connection); \"unbegrenzt\" = unbounded (Grenze = bound(ary)); \"Einheit\" = unity.","\"so kann es der Welten ... nur Eine Welt geben\": the genitive plural \"der Welten\" is kept as a separate phrase; \"Eine\" capitalized as in the source.","The passage ends in an ellipsis as printed."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-009":[". . . another world than the present one cannot *actu* be assumed as possible . . . If one now thinks the world as noumenon, it is nothing further than an absolute whole of substances; but one is also not further in a position to determine *a priori* what properties or determinations it has. But if one thinks the world as phenomenon, hence the things in space and time as their real relations, in which they must stand to one another, then the following four principles can be established, under which one must think the determinations of the world: *In mundo non datur* 1. *abyssus*, 2. *saltus*, 3. *casus* (blind chance), *fatum* (blind necessity).",["\"Eigenschaften\" rendered \"properties\" (cf. Beschaffenheit = constitution; property); \"Determinationen\" (Latinate) rendered \"determinations\", as is Bestimmungen.","\"a priori\" (\"a priori zu bestimmen\") could attach to \"determine\" or to what is determined; left as in the German.","\"mithin die Dinge im Raum und Zeit als ihren reellen Verhältnissen\": the case of \"Verhältnissen\" is awkward in the German and kept awkward (\"as their real relations\").","The enumeration runs \"1., 2., 3.\" with \"casus\" and \"fatum\" both after \"3.\"; \"four principles\" is as printed. \"Grundsatz\"/\"Prinzip\": \"Prinzipien\" = principles. \"Ungefähr\" = chance."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-010":["This was also the opinion of Wolff and Leibniz. One sees well that this idea, thought through the mere understanding, can count in itself as correct, and insofar can be thought: *compositum substantiale consistit ex simplicibus*. As phenomenon, however, here is an extended substance, a persistent thing in space, that cannot be thought without parts and without division to infinity. The monads, however, cannot make up parts of the body in space, but must be merely points, because otherwise they would be parts of a space . . .",["\"ein Beharrliches\" = \"a persistent thing\" (beharren/Beharrlichkeit = persist/persistence); \"Zerteilung\" rendered \"division\" (as Teilung/Einteilung; Teil = part).","\"Idee\" = idea; \"Meinung\" = opinion; \"an sich\" = in itself.","\"Als Phänomen aber ist hier eine ausgedehnte Substanz\": the grammar is kept as printed. \"Wolf\" rendered \"Wolff\". The Academy's \"bricht ab\" is omitted; the ellipsis is as printed."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-011":["The substances in the world must have a reciprocal influence on one another, i.e. stand in *nexu reali*, which can take place only through interaction on one another. This *nexus realis per commercium* would not be possible to assume among the things if one thinks them through the understanding as existing in themselves. The substances would each exist for itself without any relation and combination among one another. Hence a *totum reale* of necessary substances cannot be thought at all. For then none is dependent on another with regard to its existence, each exists for itself, because each has its necessarily sufficient ground of its existence in itself: many necessary substances would thus have among themselves no combination, each can for itself be only world and the fundamental cause of a world, but with another world and the things in it they could stand in not the slightest combination, e.g. many gods. All such substances would thus be unconditioned and determined through themselves, but each isolated through its absolute necessity. Since, therefore, according to this, straightaway, and without being a hindrance to their necessity, their connection among one another cannot be assumed, one cannot, in order to think this to oneself, do otherwise than derive their existence from a universal common original source, which is the universal force for the universal effect of all things. Through this, however, the latter become dependent on it, and in themselves contingent, are they, through this universal cause, combined among one another, and there thus arises a reciprocal connection and community among one another through the common cause, since an action of a single being was necessary in order to produce them all, and in this way the *nexus realis* arises.",["Verbindung = combination; Verknüpfung = connection; Gemeinschaft = community; Wechselwirkung = interaction; Dasein and Existenz both = existence; Wirkung = effect; Kraft = force.","\"nothwendig hinreichenden\": hinreichend rendered \"sufficient\" (as zureichend); \"Grundursache\" rendered \"fundamental cause\"; \"Urquelle\" = original source.","\"Hierdurch aber werden letztere von ihr abhängig, und an sich zufällig, werden sie ...\": the construction is loose (second \"werden\" with inverted subject); kept as close to the German as possible. \"ihr\" and \"diese\" referents left unresolved.","\"hinderlich zu sein\": its subject (\"ihre Verknüpfung\" or the argument) is not explicit in the German; left unresolved."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-012":["Newton calls space the *organon* of divine omnipresence. This idea, however, is incorrect, since space in itself is nothing, and as something in itself actually existing cannot be thought through the connection of things. On the other hand, if one thinks space as *symbolum*, i.e. in the place of all relations and interaction itself, then one thinks under it the sum total of all phaenomena, and indeed as *compraesentia*, i.e. as present to one another, and reciprocally acting on one another, and the being that contains them, as symbol.",["OCR \"wiikend\" read as \"wirkend\" (acting). \"Phaenomena\" and \"compraesentia\" kept in Latin in italics (the first as printed in roman type, rendered in italics only as a Latin word in the source's second occurrence; here left in plain form as it appears in the German).","\"Inbegriff\" = sum total; \"Wirklich Existierendes\" = \"actually existing\" (Wirklichkeit = actuality); \"Verknüpfung\" = connection; \"Wesen\" = being.","\"als Symbol\" at the end of the passage is ambiguous as to whether it qualifies \"das Wesen\" or the way it is thought; kept as in the German."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-013":["The *influxus physicus* is a) *originarius*, i.e., through their existence the substances are already in *commercio* without assuming a ground, b) *derivativus* (*rationalis*). All *influxus physicus* presupposes a *derivativum*; for this alone is the true one. It does not go without saying that substances are in *commercio*; for substances are precisely that which exists for itself alone, without depending on another. In the *mundo phaenomeno* (which is in space and time) it is precisely space that combines the substances, whereby they are in *commercio*. But how are the substances in *mundo noumeno* in *commercio*? — The harmony of the substances is supposed to consist in this, that their state agrees with one another, i.e., according to universal laws. The world is considered either as *totum ideale* and here there is then a *harmonia absque commercio*; or the world is *totum reale* and here there is a *harmonia substantiarum in commercio*. This latter *systema* is called the *systema influxus physici*. But the *systema harmoniae substantiarum absque commercio* is the *systema influxus hyperphysici*, i.e., the world derives from a *causa extramundana*; i.e., from God.",["The initial 'I' before 'Der Influxus' was treated as an OCR or numbering slip and left out.","Dasein and existiert/Existenz are both rendered with 'exist/existence'; Dasein = existence, Existenz = existence (one English word for two German words).","'daß ihr Zustand auf einander, d. i. nach allgemeinen Gesetzen übereinstimmt': 'according to universal laws' may attach to 'agrees' or be an appositive gloss on 'with one another'; left ambiguous as in the German.","'ohne einen Grund anzunehmen': 'without assuming a ground' (or 'a ground' as the object of assuming); kept literal. The final 'd. h von Gott' is as printed."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-014":["This latter can now be: 1. *Systema adsistentiae* — 2. *Systema harmoniae praestabilitae*. Leibnitz wanted to explain thereby not the *commercium* of the substances, but only the *commercium* between soul and body, because these are a pair of such heterogeneous substances. Cause and effect, however, can be *realiter* different; thus I can assume that something depends on something entirely dissimilar, e.g., motion of the body on the representation of the soul, as effect on the cause, on one another. This, however, cannot be explained further, rather we assume such propositions because through them experience becomes possible. The *systema influxus physici* has again a twofold way of representation, of thinking the same as possible: 1. *commercii originarii*, if substances are in *commercio* by their existing; 2. *derivativi*, if something more must be added in order to bring about this *commercium*. The *commercium originarium* is a *qualitas occulta*; it is assumed as its own ground of explanation. Thus nothing remains but the *systema influxus physici*, and indeed in *commercio derivativo*, where I assume that all substances exist through one causality, whereby they are all in *commercio*. This idea has something sublime. If I assume all substances as absolutely necessary, they cannot stand in the slightest community. But if I assume the substances as existing in community, I assume that they all exist through one causality, for only thereby can their community be explained. — Space itself is the form of divine omnipresence, i.e., the omnipresence of God is expressed in the form of a *phaenomenon*, and through this omnipresence of God all substances are in harmony. But here our reason can have insight into nothing further.",["'wie Wirkung von der Ursache von einander abhänge': the construction is awkward in the German and is kept awkward ('depend ... as effect on the cause, on one another').","Gemeinschaft = community; Latin *commercium* kept in Latin. Vorstellungsart = 'way of representation' (Art = way).","A stray underscore at the end of the OCR text was left out.","The German writes 'Leibnitz'; the spelling is kept."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-015":["Those who assume space as a thing in itself or as a constitution of things in themselves are compelled to be Spinozists, i.e., they assume the world as a sum total of the determinations of a single necessary substance, thus only One substance. Space as something necessary would then also be a property of God, and all things exist in space, thus in God.",["Beschaffenheit = 'constitution'; Eigenschaft = 'property' (two German words, two English words, kept distinct).","'Sache an sich selbst' / 'Dinge an sich': 'thing in itself' / 'things in themselves'.","'einigen' (in 'einigen notwendigen Substanz') rendered 'single'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-016":["it displeases. If the ground of liking or disliking is to be sought merely in the sensation through the sense, or sense-sensation (not sensible, because to that belongs also the imagination, not *convenirende* here), then it is not the object that pleases, but it is a state that pleases me, by virtue of the sensation aroused in me through the sense. This mere subjectively related constitution of the influence of the object, which expresses the state of the subject aroused through inner or outer sense, is, however, that of which one must say: it gratifies, it pains.",["The passage begins mid-sentence ('es misfällt'); translated as it stands.","Gefallen = 'liking' (gefallen = 'please'); Misfallen = 'disliking' (misfallen = 'displease'); Vergnügen/vergnügen = 'gratification'/'gratify'.","'convenirende' is the German's Latinate participle (from *convenire*); left as printed because the sense ('suitable', 'agreeing') is not certain. 'mere subjective bezogne' is as printed (Latinate 'mere'); 'subjects' read as 'subject'.","Object and Gegenstand are both 'object'; here Object.","'Sinnen=Empfindung' rendered 'sense-sensation'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-017":["Hence gratification and pain arises merely through the immediate sense-sensation of the subject: the characteristic of it lies in the interest in the existence of the object, or non-existence of it; and gratification is therefore the satisfaction and interest in the existence of the thing. What gratifies us is also agreeable to us, and in its existence we take simply each time an interest, in that this takes place only insofar, and with it the gratification only in the degree, as and insofar as the existence of the object pleases us, or (properly) is agreeable; because it depends on it to maintain for us longer the subjective state into which we are placed. Likewise it is *in casu contrario* with pain. There is, however, also a state of liking and not liking, which depends not on the immediate influence of sense-sensation, but on reflection about the object and arises from it. Here there is present neither gratification, nor pain, nor an interest in the existence of the object and maintenance of our subjective state: rather in regard to the existence of the object there is present indifference, it is indifferent whether the state of my sensation is to be gratification or pain, from that side no preferential decisive influence shows itself. From this arises that which is called liking, i.e., that satisfaction in the object through the mere intuition of it, with complete indifference (without regard) to the existence of it. — Pure or sensible intuition can lie at the ground. E.g., A beautifully built house pleases me according to its form: it is possible that its existence pains me for entirely other grounds, which the understanding offers, which can co-operate contingently with the gratification and pain.",["Daseyn and existenz are both 'existence'; Gegenstand and Object both 'object' (here mostly Object, once Gegenstand in 'Reflexion über den Gegenstand').","Wohlgefallen = 'satisfaction'; Gefallen = 'liking'; angenehm = 'agreeable'; reflexion (Latinate spelling) = 'reflection'.","'Zum Grunde liegen' rendered literally 'lie at the ground'.","The antecedents of 'indem dieses' and 'es' ('it depends on it to maintain...') are left as ambiguous as in the German; 'mitwirken zufällig' = 'co-operate contingently'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-018":["That which pleases through the mere intuition is beautiful, that which in intuition leaves me indifferent whether it could please or displease is not-beautiful; that which in intuition displeases is ugly. On this liking rests now the concept of taste. Namely, much can please in the representation through the senses, with which the understanding can combine in its operation, and gratification arises; but insofar as the power of judgment combines with the feeling of pleasure and displeasure, and it accompanies and determines the former in the intuition of an object of the senses, or in general its impression of the same, there arises thence taste, which rests accordingly on aesthetic power of judgment, aesthetic (from [atulla] to taste *per organa sensuum*, sense as source of sensible representations) representation determined according to the laws of sensibility. Aesthetic power of judgment is therefore that power of judgment which is directed to the satisfaction or disliking in objects, insofar as these objects are of my sensible intuition, just as the logical power of judgment assesses the object not as such in intuition, but through reason alone.",["'atulla' is a garbled OCR reading (presumably a Greek word); left in brackets and not corrected. The parenthesis is itself unclosed/odd in the source.","'UAtheilskraft' read as 'Urtheilskraft'; 'Vernunftallein' read as 'Vernunft allein'.","Lust/Unlust = 'pleasure'/'displeasure'; misfallen/Misfallen = 'displease'/'disliking'; beurtheilt = 'assesses'.","'ihren Eindruck von demselben': the referent of 'ihren' (the power of judgment or the feeling) is left ambiguous; rendered 'its'. 'sie das erstere' likewise literal.","'Gegenstände der Sinne' / 'Gegenstände meiner sinnlichen Anschauung': 'of my sensible intuition' kept elliptical as in the German."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-019":["All objects of sense-intuition or of the imagination are also objects of the aesthetic power of judgment — and that is entirely the same as the concept of taste. It is thus occupied with the satisfaction in the beautiful, which it, whether it be beautiful or not beautiful, should determine and consequently build its operation on a principle of the beautiful.",["'princip' (Latinate spelling) rendered 'principle'; Gegenstand = 'object'.","'das sie, ob es schön oder nicht schön sey, bestimmen': 'which' may refer to the satisfaction or to the beautiful; kept ambiguous as in the German."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-020":["Were now the matter of the object, i.e., that which through immediate sensation of it affects the mind, the object of the estimation, then it would be one and the same with gratification and pain. These sensations of the senses, which would here be aesthetic sensations, cannot, however, since they are merely subjective, be determined and assessed according to rules. The satisfaction or disliking therefore in the object, in which the aesthetic power of judgment considers the feeling of pleasure or displeasure, concerns merely the form of the object, which is subjected to sensible or pure intuition, and of which it determines only whether it is beautiful or not beautiful.",["Beurtheilung = 'estimation'; beurtheilen = 'assess'; Materie = 'matter'; Gemüth = 'mind'; afficirt = 'affects'.","'Gegenstand der Beurtheilung' and 'Object': both 'object'.","'der ... unterworfen ist' and 'von dem sie nur bestimmt' are kept as literal as English allows; the referent of 'sie' (the power of judgment) is as in the German."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-021":["A particular phenomenon manifests itself in the judging of the beautiful. One demands and presupposes that what we find beautiful as an object of taste, every other too, who has taste, will find beautiful like us; and just as particular is it that the one judging cannot at all determine himself as to whether something is beautiful otherwise than through his own judgment, unless he wanted to ape a foreign judgment without himself judging, whence one can then also well assume that that which, according to the judgment of all other equally cultivated persons, does not receive approval, may well in the end also not be beautiful: and there arises therefore a peculiar antinomy, which for the time being cannot be distinctly developed according to its ground. One assumes that the other must find something beautiful, at the same time one fears his displeasure, because one has no grounds by which to compel him to approval.",["*Phaenomen*: Latin-script word in the German; kept as \"phenomenon\".","*Beurtheilung* = \"judging\" (glossary: \"estimation; judgment\"); *Urtheil* = \"judgment\", *urtheilen* = \"judge\"; *Urtheilende* = \"the one judging\".","*Gegenstand* = \"object\".","*Grund* = \"ground\" (*Gründe* = \"grounds\"); *deutlich* = \"distinct\".","OCR \"mtiße\" read as \"müße\" (= müsse); \"Misfallen\" rendered \"displeasure\" (not in glossary).","\"woher\" rendered \"whence\"; the reference of \"sein\" (his) in the last sentence is left as in the German."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-022":["For what is supposed to please the other absolutely must be able to be cognized by him *a priori*, there must therefore be a rule at hand that is prescribed to him, by virtue of which he is determined to approval or displeasure: hence the necessity and universal validity of the beautiful can be demonstrated only if a *principium* of the beautiful exists. Now, however, a *demonstration* of the beautiful is impossible; it is abstracted only from the sensible according to laws of the understanding, without one being able to specify these laws, which are found thereby, as universally valid, precisely because of the subjective in feeling. — Models even the Greeks now had, e.g. their Doryphoros of Polyclitus, which they called the rule of a beautifully formed young man in the art of sculpture. The judging of the beautiful therefore remains, like the ground and source of taste in general, which the soul so much lays at the basis, an object of the most difficult investigation.",["*a priori*, *princip* (written \"princip\", rendered *principium*, a conjecture of form), *demonstration*: Latin-script words, italicized.","OCR \"umnöglich\" read as \"unmöglich\"; \"Gültigkeit\" = \"validity\", \"gültig\" = \"valid\".","\"es wird nur allein vom Sinnlichen ... abstrahirt\": impersonal passive kept; \"es\" has no stated referent.","\"die die Seele so sehr zum Grunde legt\": the relative clause could attach to \"Geschmacks\" or to \"Quelle\"/\"Grund\"; ambiguity kept. \"Gegenstand\" = \"object\".","\"Doriphoron\" is the transcript's spelling; rendered \"Doryphoros\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-023":["Beautiful in aesthetic judging is only that which, without any interest in the existence of the object itself, pleases merely in the intuition of it, and indeed in the form of it, and pleases because here a free play of the imagination in agreement with the lawfulness of the understanding is at work.",["*Anschauung* = \"intuition\"; *Einbildungskraft* = \"imagination\"; *Verstand* = \"understanding\"; *Form* = \"form\"; *existenz* = \"existence\"; *Gegenstand* = \"object\".","*Beurtheilung* = \"judging\"; \"wirckt\" read as \"wirkt\"; *Gesetzmäßigkeit* = \"lawfulness\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-024":["From this it follows therefore:",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-025":["a) that everything that is supposed to be beautiful must have at least similarity with concepts, or with the laws of the understanding. Yet the consciousness of the rules or of these laws of taste is by no means necessary, but only their existence in the subject so far as required as they could serve for the support and direction of the imagination: hence these concepts of the understanding also may not be at hand in its determination;",["*Begriff* = \"concept\"; *Bewußtseyn* = \"consciousness\"; *Bestimmung* = \"determination\"; *Einbildungskraft* = \"imagination\".","\"soweit erforderlich, als sie ... dienen könne\": subjunctive and construction kept; \"ihrer/ihre\" and \"ihrer Bestimmung\": the referent of \"its\" is ambiguous in the German (the German reads \"ihrer\"). *nöthig* = \"necessary\" (*notwendig* is also \"necessary\")."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-026":["b) that the imagination occupy itself only with the form of the object, and not with its corporeal existence, consequently that it not be referred back into the limitations of the deficiencies of the same, but retain in itself an unbounded flight to form the form for itself; but that, so that it not follow its laws alone, and extravagate over the immeasurability of the object, but only create for the understanding a manifold in the object into a whole.",["The sentence is syntactically incomplete in the German (\"daß aber, damit ...\"); kept as it stands. Subjunctives in the \"daß\"-clauses kept.","*Gegen-Standes*/*Gegenstande* = \"object\"; *Schranken* = \"limitations\" (*Schranke*), *unbegränzten* = \"unbounded\"; *Mannigfaltigkeit* = \"manifold\" (glossary gives *Mannigfaltige*); *Verstände* = \"understanding\".","\"derselben\" (\"of the same\") has no clear antecedent (corporeal existence or object)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-027":["c.) the understanding refers it back each time to order, holds it within limits, whereupon then",["Continues the \"daß\" construction of b); the passage breaks off into d). *Schranken* = \"limits\" (glossary \"limitation\"); \"sie\" = \"it\" (feminine; antecedent *Einbildungskraft* in b)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-028":["d.) both powers mutually support each other, drive a free play, and so occupy themselves with pleasure. An object of this kind is found tasteful.",["*Kräfte* = \"powers\" (*Kraft*: \"force; power\"); *Wohlgefallen* = \"pleasure\" (\"mit Wohlgefallen\"); *Gegenstand* = \"object\".","\"geschmackvoll\" rendered \"tasteful\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-029":["From that which gratifies and pleases there is further distinguished that which is approved. This is the good, i.e. what pleases in the representation of reason.",["*Vorstellung* = \"representation\"; *Vernunft* = \"reason\"; *vergnügt* = \"gratifies\"; *gebilligt* = \"approved\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s07-030":["Remark. One can compare the 3-fold kinds of the faculties of the soul with the 3 operations of the understanding, according to which the faculty of cognition sets up the concepts, that in which pleasure and displeasure is subsumed under the concepts through the power of judgment, and the faculty of desire is determined through reason, consequently concludes, insofar as the pleasing or displeasing takes place in the representation.",["*Vermögen* = \"faculty\"; *Erkenntniß* = \"cognition\"; *Urtheilskraft* = \"power of judgment\"; *Begehrungs Vermögen* = \"faculty of desire\"; *Art* = \"kind\".","\"Seele\" = \"soul\" (not in glossary). \"concludirt\" rendered \"concludes\" (*Schluß* = \"inference; conclusion\").","The clause \"das, worin die Lust und Unlust ... subsumirt wird\" is syntactically obscure; kept as it stands."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-001":["§ 68 Faculty of desire",["*Begehrungs-Vermögen*; \"§•\" read as \"§\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-002":["All representations relate to the object as object of cognition, but they can also be considered as actions, and then there lies in the representation the ground of the production of the object of the same. With this there then corresponds the faculty of the soul, through the representation of an object itself, to become cause of the actuality of the object, = and this is the faculty of desire, which one can also determine just as validly as *causalitas* of the representation with regard to its object. That action of the soul in general whereby the representation seeks to attain the actuality of its object is in general desire and desiring, which are sensible and intellectual. One expresses the feeling of pleasure and displeasure through *voluptas et taedium*. Both are however merely sensible: it lets itself be determined more generally through *complacentia et displicentia*,",["*Erkenntniß* = \"cognition\"; *Object* and *Gegenstand* both \"object\"; *Wirklichkeit* = \"actuality\" (glossary also \"reality\"); *Vorstellung* = \"representation\"; *Grund* = \"ground\"; *Ursache* = \"cause\".","OCR \"Vei mögen\" read as \"Vermögen\"; \"caußalitaet\" given as *causalitas*; \"=\" kept as in the text.","*Begierde* and *Begehren* both \"desire\" (\"desire and desiring\"); \"es\" after \"Beydes ... sinnlich\" is left unresolved; the sentence continues into the next passage."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-003":["pleasure and displeasure according to Herr Kant, and to ground thereupon the concept of desire, namely as *complacentia respective actualitatis objecti*, i.e. the",["*Wohlgefallen* = \"pleasure\" (Latin *complacentia*), *Mißfallen* = \"displeasure\"; *Begriff* = \"concept\"; *Wirklichkeit* rendered in Latin *actualitas*.","The passage breaks off mid-sentence (\"d. i. die\") and continues in the next."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-004":["representation of the object, which is connected with the pleasure in the actuality of the same, and which is the ground for the production of the same.",["*verknüpft* = \"connected\" (*Verknüpfung* = \"connection\"); *Wohlgefallen* = \"pleasure\"; *Wirklichkeit* = \"actuality\"; *Grund* = \"ground\".","Begins mid-sentence, continuing the previous passage."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-005":["The pleasure in the intuition of the object therefore differs from the desire for it in that this concerns that relation of the representation to the object insofar as it can be cause of the actuality of the same.",["*Anschauung* = \"intuition\"; *Verhältniß* = \"relation\"; *Object* = \"object\"; *Begehren* = \"desire\"; *Ursache* = \"cause\"; *Wirklichkeit* = \"actuality\".","Stray apostrophe before \"dasjenige\" in the OCR omitted. \"dieses\" (\"this\") refers to *Begehren* by the German's grammar; \"sie\" = the representation."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-006":["The doubt seems to conflict with this concept, that one very often desires something whose actuality it is impossible to bring forth. For there are two kinds of desires: an *appetitio practica*, i.e. the representation of the possibility of making it actual, hence a desire according to which the representation is so qualified that the object can become actual, and an *appetitio minus practica*, which one calls wish, *optare*, a desire combined with the consciousness that it does not stand in our power to be able to bring forth the object actually. Yet both kinds of desires nevertheless unite in this, that they go out to a representation of the object, by virtue of which it posits in itself the ground of the possibility of being able to bring forth the object, only with the difference that in the first case the determining ground is sufficient, but in the latter case the causality is insufficient. The latter one calls *conatus* or wishing with regard to desire, which then degenerates into longing when this wishing passes over into a violent agitation of the mind, to the point of exhaustion, in order to make actual often quite unnatural or physically impossible representations, to which belongs e.g. the: *o mihi prateritos referat si Jupiter annos*. The cause of this characteristic of human nature, in that one often harbors wishes and seeks to realize them, whose possible causality one does not cognize in oneself as sufficient, lies in this. But if the human being, instead of venturing attempts, wanted first to examine whether his forces sufficed with certainty to reach his end, he would omit much, and seek to spare himself the effort. But as it is, there lies in him the summons of his nature to apply his striving to this, that he try his forces; he is satisfied with the possibility of being able to reach the end, although for now he feels himself insufficient, and it is wholly uncertain whether he could succeed; and with great exertion there often develop in the human being forces that were previously unknown to him, but that now ever more convince him of the possibility, that he has no cause to posit mistrust in himself any longer, and that he will have adequate forces to attain his end. If the wishes are indeed contrary to nature, then there arises from this the useless sinking back, which manifests itself through weeping as weakness in the female sex, and through merely futile attempts in the male sex. The desires now with regard to such objects, of which we are conscious that we have sufficient forces for them to bring them forth, is an *actus* of choice, and the faculty that corresponds to this desire is choice, *arbitrium*, which therefore involves not only determination to causality but also sufficient force to make the object actual: but where the faculty of choice is with regard to the bringing forth of the object, there there must also absolutely be accompanied the faculty of disregarding the bringing forth, or of omitting it. *Arbitrium* or choice is therefore in the proper sense *facultas appetendi vel aversandi objectum, i. e. omittendo vel committendo*.",["Willkühr = \"choice\" (Latin arbitrium, kept in italics); Begierde = \"desire\"; Wunsch = \"wish\"; these are not in the glossary, so the plain cognate or standard equivalent is used throughout.","Wirklichkeit/wirklich = \"actuality\"/\"actual\"; Möglichkeit = \"possibility\"; Vorstellung = \"representation\"; Bestimmungsgrund = \"determining ground\"; zureichend/unzureichend = \"sufficient\"/\"insufficient\"; erkennt = \"cognize\"; Kräfte/Kraft = \"forces\"/\"force\" (Vermögen = \"faculty\"); Object and Gegenstand both = \"object\".","\"vermöge deren sie ... setzt\": the antecedent of \"sie\" (the representation or the desire; the verb is singular, the preceding subject plural) is ambiguous in the German; kept as \"it\".","\"Die Begierden ... ist ein actus\": plural subject with singular verb, as in the German. \"da\" in \"da man oft Wünsche hegt\" rendered \"in that\"; the clause's grammatical attachment is loose in the German.","\"Eigenschaft\" = \"characteristic\" (to keep \"property\" for Beschaffenheit); \"im eigentlichen Verstände\" = \"in the proper sense\" (Verstand here is not the faculty).","OCR: \"Bewußtsejm\", \"W illkühr\", \"aucli\" silently corrected; \"prateritos\" kept as printed (the Virgil line, normally *praeteritos*); \"Caußalitaet\"/\"caußalitaet\" = causality."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-007":["In itself, with regard to the determining ground, the *arbitrium* has a double side. For there lie *elateres animi* or determining grounds, sources of the possibility of bringing forth what is represented, in the human being, *causae determinantes sive impulsivae*, and these lie either in the understanding, as the law of the action, or in sensibility, namely the feeling of pleasure and displeasure, and are therefore *vel sensitivae causae sive elateres vel intellectuales causae sive elateres* — the former one calls *stimuli*, the latter *motiva*. Hence arises the division of the concept into *arbitrium intellectuale sive superius*, the higher or rational choice, i. e. *facultas appetendi per motiva*, or *voluntas sive arbitrium ex causa impulsiva intellectuali* and *arbitrium sensitivum, facultas appetendi per stimulos*.",["OCR \"Arbiti ium\" corrected to \"Arbitrium\".","Willkühr = \"choice\" (arbitrium); Verstand = \"understanding\"; Sinnlichkeit = \"sensibility\"; Einteilung = \"division\"; Begriff = \"concept\"; Bestimmungs Gründe = \"determining grounds\".","\"vernünftige\" rendered \"rational\"; Gesetz = \"law\"; Gefühl = \"feeling\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-008":["Rational choice is in turn *vel purum vel affectum*, according as in choice no *stimuli* at all concur as cooperating causes, or such sensible impulses at the same time affect the mind and determine it to action,",["Willkühr = \"choice\"; Gemüth = \"mind\"; Antriebe = \"impulses\"; \"afficiren\" = \"affect\".","The passage ends with a comma in the source; it is presumably continued in the next passage."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-009":["In appearance, however, human choice can never be assumed in such a way that it would be determined without being affected by *stimuli*, and just as little that it could be determined *per stimulos* alone.",["Erscheinung = \"appearance\"; \"determinirt\" = \"determined\" (Latinate verb, same English as bestimmen); Willkühr = \"choice\".","The subjunctives \"werde\" and \"könne\" are rendered with \"would\"/\"could\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-010":["Animals have an *arbitrium mere sensitivum*, e.g. in choosing food — they are thus determined *per stimulos* alone, hence one can also call an *arbitrium sensitivum* *brutum*: the *arbitrium humanum*, by contrast, is never *purum*, but *semper affectum*. The cooperating *stimuli* can never determine it, however, but only affect it sensibly, and the concurrence of the understanding remains necessary in order to determine him: but one can never say that *stimuli* should not have affected him, e.g. in giving alms there would already be *stimuli*, if he gives the alms at the cost of his comfort, from which he withdraws, or from love of honor, in order not to be harshly scolded in the eyes of his fellow human beings, or from compassion toward the ragged needy one, or his entreaties, which strike the weakness of the giver, or also an expected reward from God: often the *stimuli* are so concealed that one must examine oneself precisely, whether merely the love for compliance with the law brings me to the action, and I thus act from *arbitrio puro*: but it is just as true also that it is never *stimuli* alone that determine me to the action: a representation of the law of duty, even if not a clear one, always concurs therein, and one must assume this for this reason alone, because otherwise one would make the human being equal to the cattle or to the devil, if one could presuppose that he could do everything from self-interest, etc.; otherwise it is indeed possible that *stimuli* can incite to the good just as well as to the evil.",["\"es\" ... \"ihn\" in the third sentence: the pronouns are as in the German (\"it\" for Willkühr in the first clause, \"him\" in the second); the shift of referent is preserved.","\"hart geschölten\" read as *gescholten* (OCR/orthography), \"genauselbst\" read as \"genau selbst\", \"Allmosen\" = alms; the syntax of the alms-examples (\"seine ... Bitten\") is loose in the German and kept so.","Vorstellung = \"representation\"; Pflicht Gesetz = \"law of duty\"; Vieh = \"cattle\"; Eigennutz = \"self-interest\"; Verstand = \"understanding\"; Willkühr = \"choice\" (arbitrium)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-011":["On this now rests the concept of freedom: namely the faculty of the human being to determine himself to action through motives, independently of the sensible impulses affecting him, choice is therefore free only insofar as it is not determined *per stimulos*: which in human choice comes about through the overcoming of the *stimulorem*. Hence free choice, or freedom, is the independence of choice *a determinatione per stimulos*. — Thus in human choice the *stimuli* are not, and must not be, excluded, for otherwise it would be an *arbitrium purum*, the pure self-subsistent being, which can determine itself only in accordance with the laws, not against them. The human being has *facultates inferiores*, which work for him as sensible impulses, and indeed become *causae impulsivae*, but can only affect him, never determine him, neither to the good nor to the evil: his choice is thus *partim sensualis, partim intellectualis*, of which the acting *stimuli* are indeed also to be regarded as ground for action, but only as an insufficient ground, the motives by contrast containing that determining ground which makes the action necessary.",["\"stimulorem\" is printed (OCR or original) for *stimulorum*; kept as printed.","Willkühr = \"choice\"; Vermögen = \"faculty\"; Motive/motiven = \"motives\" (Latin *motiva* kept in 007); Freiheit = \"freedom\"; unzureichend = \"insufficient\"; Bestimmungs Grund = \"determining ground\"; nothwendig = \"necessary\".","\"die Willkühr ist daher nur frei\" follows a comma after \"zu bestimmen\" in the source; the sentence is kept as one, with the comma splice.","\"Wesen\" = \"being\"; \"selbständige\" = \"self-subsistent\"; \"Gesetzen\" = \"laws\" (plural, with article)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-012":["§69. All objectively necessary rules and determinations of choice, insofar as they are subjectively contingent, are called imperatives.",["\"imperative\" (adjective-form plural in the German) rendered \"imperatives\"; Willkühr = \"choice\"; zufällig = \"contingent\"; Bestimmung = \"determination\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-013":["a) They are objective in general because they express what must be done.",["\"Objectiv\" = \"objective\"; \"ausdrücken\" = \"express\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-014":["b) Every imperative is objectively necessary, i. e. it contains with regard to the action a determining ground that makes the action necessary according to a rule; but at the same time is",["The passage breaks off mid-sentence in the source (\"zugleich aber ist\"); not completed.","Bestimmungs Grund = \"determining ground\"; imperativ = \"imperative\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-015":["c) every imperative is subjectively contingent, i.e. it is connected in its determination with the possibility for the subject of deviating from the rule and doing the opposite. Hence in a being set up according to both conditions of the imperative, which acts according to imperatives, there arises the necessitation to the action: for it is subject to such commands, in which there is present an objectively necessary rule as well as a possibility of the subject's deviating from the rule, and consequently for it an imperative is an objective rule insofar as it is necessitating with regard to the subject, and both conditions are connected in it, if it is to act according to imperatives. It is also only such a being of which one can predicate an ought, i.e. a necessity of acting according to a rule, the transgression of which remains subjectively possible. Hence neither a God nor an animal can act according to imperatives. God is not capable of a deviation from the law, he determines himself only through the law, i.e. through himself, in him no necessitation, no ought takes place. He therefore also has no duties, since these actions rest on an ought or necessitation. He has only rights against human beings, just as these can have only duties toward him, but never rights. Animals, on the other hand, do not act according to rules, because on account of lack of understanding they do not know the like, but have only sensible impulses; they therefore also cannot follow imperatives, and thereby determine themselves to action, i.e. let themselves be necessitated.",["OCR 'lüi ihn' read as 'für ihn'; 'handlen' = handeln; 'Noth wendigkeit' rejoined.","'Bestimmung' rendered 'determination' (glossary); 'bestimmen' = 'determine'; 'Wesen' = 'being'.","'neceßitirend' / 'Nöthigung' both rendered with 'necessitat-' / the German 'Nöthigung' = 'necessitation'; 'nöthigen' = 'necessitate'.","'kennen' = 'know' (noscere sense), 'Verstand' = 'understanding'.","'imperativ' is lowercase Latin-derived in the source and is kept as 'imperative'; 'subject' (Latin-form noun in German text) rendered 'subject'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-016":["Imperatives therefore have actions as end, and are therefore called practical imperatives, i.e. practical rules, which indicate what ought to be done. — The *oppositum* is physical rules according to which something ought to happen; practical imperatives, however, can be either technically practical imperatives, i.e. they can, for the attainment of an end, command the means hypothetically, they are therefore rules that demand conditioned compliance. To these belong all mechanical rules for an art or skill, by which one, in order to attain the end, follows the rules of the same. — Rules of prudence with regard to the action of other human beings toward one's end, which latter one, however, calls pragmatic. In both cases it depends first of all on the will of the one acting, whether he wills the end. If he has no interest in it, then no one can compel him to follow the rule; they therefore command on both sides only conditionally, for the sake of the end, or else as morally practical rules.",["OCR 'piagmatisch' read as 'pragmatisch'.","'Zweck' = 'end'; 'Mittel' = 'means'; 'Intereße' = 'interest'.","The sentence 'Regeln der Klugheit … welche letztere man indeß piagmatisch nennt' is grammatically loose in the German and is kept so; 'welche letztere' could refer to the rules or to the actions of others.","The final clause 'oder aber als moralisch practische Regeln' hangs on loosely after the semicolon; kept as it stands.","'Oppositum' kept in Latin, italicized."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-017":["Remark. One could also very well assume a disjunctive imperative, which would consist in this, that the choice of the subject with regard to several determinations commanding disjunctively would indeed be permitted, but transgression and contravention against every condition of the law would be impermissible, e.g. in a civil war it would be impermissible to remain neutral, but one would have to attack one party or the other.",["'innern Kriege' rendered literally as 'civil war' (lit. 'inner war'); 'Bestimmungen' = 'determinations'.","'Bedingung' = 'condition'; the phrase 'alle Bedingung des Gesetzes' is kept in the singular as in the German.","'contravention' stands as in the source (a Latinate word)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-018":["Morally practical rules are by their nature categorical, because they command absolutely, immediately through reason, and without any regard to the existence of other determining or motive grounds: they are unconditioned and immediate determining grounds of actions; every other assumed motive ground other than the one that reason gives itself would be a condition and its command only hypothetical. E.g. if a witness wanted to speak the truth only because he fears worldly or divine punishment, because his friend however has an advantage in it, then these and other grounds could easily be eluded, and the duty to truth would no longer take place at all, if it depended only on conditioned circumstances, since the opposite of these is just as possible. Reason alone is the legislatress, and it ought also to follow the law through the subject. If the subject is to be able to do this, regardless of its inclination of sensible impulses, then it must be capable of being able to do this, it must be possible for it to exercise that which reason commands. The categorical imperative therefore presupposes in the human being a subjective possibility for following the law as well as for transgressing the same, or, which is one and the same, an *independentia a determinationibus per stimulos*, consequently freedom; the human being must therefore be free, if he is to follow absolute commands solely and alone because reason commands them.",["'independenz a determinationibus per stimulos' kept in Latin, italicized, with the German 'independenz' normalized to *independentia* only as a reading of the OCR-printed form; the source form is 'independenz'.","'Bestimmungs- oder Bewegungs-Gründe' = 'determining or motive grounds' (Bewegungsgrund = motive; Grund = ground).","'eludirt' rendered 'eluded'; 'Gesetzgeberin' = 'legislatress' (feminine kept).","'Soll dies das subject … können so muß es fähig seyn, dies zu können' is awkward in the German and is kept so; 'fähig' = 'capable'.","'Neigung sinnlicher Antriebe' = 'inclination of sensible impulses', genitive kept."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-019":["For if this were not the case, all actions of the human being would have to be determined merely according to laws of nature, or according to natural necessity, and consequently all imputability would fall away. For freedom is contradistinguished from natural necessity. Actions that happen according to laws of freedom must be exercised in such a way that they are not determined by the preceding state of the world, *in contrario* actions according to natural necessity cannot be considered otherwise than that they are necessarily predetermined in the series of effects and causes. If they are this, however, then they are determined in the previous (past) time, and actions, considered as determined in the previous time, are not in my power, to omit the action as well. The opposite therefore cannot happen, because they are determined according to unalterable laws of causality in the previous time, and thus appear merely as effects, of which the cause is present in the previous time, and consequently are not in my power, because in general the previous time is not in my power.",["OCR 'bestimmtwerden, undmithinwürde' rejoined as 'bestimmt werden, und mithin würde'; 'Wirckungen' = 'Wirkungen'.","'in contrario' kept in Latin, italicized.","'Zurechnungsfähigkeit' rendered 'imputability'; 'Gewalt' rendered 'power'.","'contradistinguirt' rendered 'contradistinguished'; 'determinirt' rendered 'determined'.","'die Handlung auch zu unterlaßen' is kept in its loose syntactic position; 'auch' ('as well') may modify 'unterlaßen' or the whole clause.","'Zustand' = 'state'; 'Zeit' = 'time'; 'vorige' rendered 'previous'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-020":["An *arbitrium* therefore that was determined by laws of nature as predetermining causes would be an *arbitrium servum*, and the opposite of the predetermined effect could not possibly be done by it. This is however contrary to the nature of a command of reason, which permits and presupposes possibility of fulfillment, not necessity, consequently also possibility for the opposite. The determination of compliance therefore lies in the free choice of the subject. Hence free choice is accompanied by a will that takes its own and indeed sufficient determining grounds (intellectual) not from states and conditions of the previous time, but rather through self-determination (spontaneity) and without being necessitated by any cause of the previous time, exercises the action.",["'arbitrium' and 'arbitrium servum' kept in Latin, italicized; 'Willkühr' rendered 'choice' and 'Wille' 'will'.","OCR 'fiir’s' read 'für's'; 'Wiilkühr' read 'Willkühr'; 'spontaneit-aet' read 'spontaneität'.","'hinreichende' rendered 'sufficient' (cf. zureichend, glossary).","'Bestimmung der Befolgung' = 'determination of compliance'; genitive left ambiguous as in German.","The last sentence's relative clause ('der … hernimmt, vielmehr … die Handlung ausübt') has its verb at the end; word order kept."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-021":["It becomes clear from this that the system, or principle, which one has hitherto called determinism, must properly be called predeterminism. For, since it is absolutely necessary that every action must be determined by a ground, even the one that a deity exercises, determinism expresses nothing of what matters here, namely the principle according to which every action, even of a free being, is thought as determined by its determining grounds in the previous time, and thus as not given in the power of the one acting. This one should call predeterminism according to Herr Kant.",["'Grundsatz' = 'principle' (glossary), 'princip' also 'principle': one English word for two German words.","'determinismus' and 'praedeterminismus' kept as 'determinism' and 'predeterminism'.","'Gottheit' rendered 'deity'; 'Wesen' = 'being'.","'als nicht in der Gewalt des Handelnden gegeben' kept literally."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-022":["Now, however, the striking phenomenon in the human being is that freedom is found united in him with natural necessity. Both take place in him, so that one can say that everything happens in the human being according to laws of natural necessity, and also that everything happens according to the principle of freedom.",["'Phaenomen' rendered 'phenomenon'; 'Natur Nothwendigkeit' rendered 'natural necessity'.","'princip' = 'principle'.","The stray hyphen after 'Nothwendigkeit' in the OCR is dropped."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-023":["As a natural being (*phaenomenon*) every new action can be explained as determined according to laws of the necessity of nature. One also often does this in criminal cases. E.g., one takes into account in the case of the criminal his upbringing, external circumstances, inclinations or other motives, which are merely subjective, in order to derive from them the *determination* to the crime. As an *intelligence* (rational being), which however must not be thought as determined as pure through reason itself (or as *noumenon*). The human being is self-determining, independent of all laws of nature, takes from himself the ground to omit his action, which he can do, or ought to do, which, determined according to laws of nature, he would not be capable of. We are meanwhile compelled to assume in his selfhood a union of both beings present in him, apparently entirely contradictory, by virtue of which he is the very same person who is affected by the lower forces, but determines himself through the independence of reason, and thus he appears ordered not in succession, but at the same time under reason and nature. With regard to his actions one can therefore also name freedom (nominal definition):",["OCR: 'neue Handlung' kept literally as 'new action'; the reading may be a slip (e.g. for 'jede Handlung'), doubtful. 'Natur Wesen' = 'natural being'; 'Vernunft Wesen' = 'rational being'; 'Wesen' = 'being' throughout (glossary), including 'beyder ... Wesen'.","OCR 'pure' left as in the text ('as pure'); possibly 'als rein' / 'blos' intended; sentence 'Als intelligenz ... (oder als noumenon)' has no finite verb and is kept as it stands.","'Nothwendigkeit' = necessity; 'Bewegungs Gründe' = motives (Bewegungsgrund); 'Grund' = ground; 'bestimmen' = determine, 'determination'/'determinirt' left as Latinate in the text and rendered 'determination'/'determines'; 'Bestimmung' also 'determination'.","'sich j selbst bestimmend': stray 'j' silently dropped. 'seine Handlung, die er thun kann, oder soll, zu unterlaßen': the construction is kept ambiguous (the action to be omitted, which he can or ought to do).","'Vereinigung' = union; 'Selbst heit' read as 'Selbstheit' = selfhood; 'independenz' = independence; 'succeßiver' = succession (adjective form rendered as noun 'in succession'); 'zu gleicher Zeit' = at the same time; 'Kräfte' = forces. The final colon breaks off; the passage continues in the next one."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-024":["the accountability of the human being.",["'Zurechnungsfähigkeit' = accountability (Zurechnung = imputation; Fähigkeit = capacity); a compound, rendered by one word; lit. 'capacity for imputation'. Continues the preceding passage's sentence."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-025":["Merely considered as intelligence, the human being is an accountable subject, in whom, to be sure, a natural being is at the same time united, which subjects him to the laws of nature, which however, insofar as the possibility to act is left to him, where the determination depends on the intelligence, therefore it is only to be derived, how far the human being can be called cause of his actions and they can be imputed to him, and just as certain, that if he were guided merely according to laws of nature, it would be impossible to impute any action to him, since the ground of the action would then never lie in his power, but would be determined in the previous time.",["OCR 'Bios' read as 'Blos' = 'merely'. The syntax (welches ... das jedoch, ... daher ist es nur abzuleiten) is anacoluthic in the German and is kept as it stands.","'zurechnen' = impute; 'Zurechnungsfähigkeit' = accountable (zurechnungsfähig); 'Ursach' = cause; 'Gewalt' = power (not 'Kraft' or 'Vermögen'); 'Möglichkeit' = possibility; 'Intelligenz' = intelligence.","'in der vorigen Zeit' = 'in the previous time' (literal)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-026":["The human being thus has as *noumenon* the faculty to determine himself to action in spite of his necessity of nature. From this it follows that freedom is thinkable without contradiction of the concept and therefore logically possible.",["'Vermögen' = faculty; 'Widerspruch' = contradiction; 'Begriff' = concept; 'ohnerachtet' = 'in spite of'. 'Natur Nothwendigkeit' = 'necessity of nature'. 'seiner Natur Nothwendigkeit' could be read as 'his natural necessity'; rendered literally."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-027":["But we cannot cognize things in themselves, what and how they are in themselves, but only in appearance, hence we also know only the human being as he presents himself to us in the form of sensibility as *phaenomenon*, hence his actions, but not his grounds of determination: it is therefore impossible to prove the real possibility of the absolute self-determination of the human being, or how the human being can determine himself freely and yet at the same time be subject to the laws of nature, for to that it would necessarily belong that freedom be an object of possible intuition. It is so little this that rather all actions of the human being appear as determined according to the law of causality or in the previous time, according to their ground, so that the human being therefore does not have it in his power how he ought to act, so that he must therefore be subject to the necessity of nature. But precisely because the human being presents himself in the way according to the form of sensibility, a possible inference can be made to the supersensible, as something present, which could be counted among the grounds of determination of his actions. A supersensible source can perhaps be sought in him, where he no longer appears as object of the senses, although we cannot determine *a priori* how he is there. This source would be the choice [*Willkühr*] of the human being as *noumenon*, i.e. the independent, but to us unknown, ground of all appearances in the sensible human being. Insofar one can presuppose him as the self-own, independent ground of all his actions, and assume of him that he now, through the ground of determination present in him, begins a series of actions, and consider him thus as if he were not in time: in this respect one could determine freedom through the faculty of the human being to begin of itself a series of sequels.",["'erkennen' = cognize; 'kennen' = know; 'Erscheinung' = appearance; 'Gegenstand' = object; 'object' (Latinate, in 'object möglicher Anschauung') also 'object' — one English word for both.","'Bestimmungs Gründe' = grounds of determination; 'bestimmen' = determine throughout; 'Anschauung' = intuition; 'Sinnlichkeit' = sensibility; 'Schluß' = inference; 'Folgen' = sequels (Folge); 'Reihe' = series.","'in der Art nach der Form der Sinnlichkeit': kept as in the German ('Art' here 'way'). 'Noth Wendigkeit' = necessity. 'Willkühr' left in German in brackets after 'choice'; not in the glossary.","Ambiguity kept: 'durch den in ihm vorhandenen Grund der Bestimmung' and 'von selbst' (of itself) — the neuter 'itself' follows 'Vermögen'/the German 'von selbst' and is left as ambiguous as the German. 'selbsteignen' = 'self-own' (literal). 'a priori' may attach to 'bestimmen' or to 'wie er da ist'; left ambiguous.","OCR: 'absolutenSelbst Bestimmung' silently corrected to 'absoluten Selbst Bestimmung'; 'Betracht' rendered 'respect'; 'Gewalt' = power."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-028":["One says quite correctly in this respect of him that his actions are subject to *determinismus*, for, since he cannot act without being determined to the action through a ground, so here there is only the presupposition that he determines himself to the action, but not that he is predetermined to the action through a previously determining time, i.e. through the law of causality. This confusion of concepts made it that the concept of freedom became the *vera crux philosophorum*. How is one to unite it, that the human being is subject to the law of natural necessity and insofar is already predetermined by a previously determining time as cause to his actions as effects, and that the human being nevertheless is supposed to be able to act freely ? To be sure, as a natural being he stands alone under *praedeterminismo*, and if therefore the power of choice of the human being could be assumed as determined merely in the previous time, then no freedom at all would be possible: but if one may presuppose in the human being a *Noumenon*, if one may therefore assume that the actions flow from independent determinations of the forces of this being in him; then the concept of freedom is thinkable and possible without contradiction. The reality of this is to be sure impossible to prove, since it is a pure concept of reason *a priori*, which cannot be grounded through the moral actions that are exercised by virtue of the determination of reason and which are the only ones through which it reveals itself in appearance. Suppose, e.g., the witness represents to himself, in giving his testimony, the importance of his duty to speak the truth, and to arrange his actions in conformity with law accordingly, without any other side-motives, and he acted without the concurrence of any natural cause; then this would still prove in him nothing further than the existence of the consciousness of the moral law, which commands him, passing over all natural causes, to follow the law. From this example it follows at the same time that there is present in us a consciousness of a moral law, through which we are necessitated to action, in that it commands us absolutely, and by virtue of the existence of this law we can infer to the freedom of our actions, that we are obligated, or are necessitated, with every sensible sacrifice, to act in accordance with the law. Quite incorrect, however, is the idea of some philosophers (e.g. Rehberg) in this, as if one could become conscious straight out and immediately of the absolute spontaneity or of the influence of the freedom of law in our actions, i.e. we can cognize that the law alone determines us, and that no determining ground occurring in the sense is connected with it. But this is impossible, we can well become aware that sensible impulses concur in the determination to the action of duty, but from the fact that we are not conscious of their existence *in casu speciali*, we can by no means infer that they would also not be present in us at all, and show themselves efficacious, for how is one to become aware of the non-existence of them? Immediately this is impossible in itself; since there is no mark, and that it does not take place mediately is evident from this: the action of duty rests on necessitation, insofar as the sensible affection concurs, without necessitation no free power of choice is thinkable; how, now, can it be cognized whether the determination to the action has its ground in the sensible impulses or in spontaneity alone, or in both? E.g. one lets someone have the choice between 2 things that are in themselves entirely alike ? It is false that one assures, he could in this not choose otherwise than through free determination of his reason itself without the concurrence of all natural causes: one can assume that he certainly beforehand often and repeatedly looks at both things, whether from mistrust, or because he expects a preference in one, finally after fruitless effort impatience overpowers him and he grabs one : is the latter not a determining ground that flows into his reason, so that it finally decide to what it calls upon him? So it is undecided whether the witness determines himself to the deposition of the truth more from feeling of his moral conscience, or from rules of prudence. One cannot therefore become aware of freedom, and that it commands the determining grounds of our moral actions, but must infer to it only from the consciousness of the laws of reason to the existence of the same. From this it follows that we cognize the laws of morals indeed *a priori*, but as dogmatically practical laws, in distinction from the dogmatically theoretical propositions *a priori*, which we can have only of objects of possible experience. These moral laws are now also what properly pertains to the human being as *Noumenon*, and constitutes the character of the latter; as which presents itself as the subjective principle of legislation in the human being. This being in us unknown to us is the most sublime thought, which is very fruitful in consequences for our moral existence. One finds in this that all these laws are laid in us plain and clear, and that it is so little striking to see them followed that one must rather wonder that they are so often not followed; that every common human understanding, on their ground, immediately decides about the legitimacy of the actions, and that we alone ourselves are at fault for every case of transgression. If one now, in the examination of this being ruling in us, does indeed find that it commands over our whole sensible nature, hence must itself be something supersensible, if now its proper nature and constitution remain unknown to us, then this circumstance transports us into deep admiration : we do not know, is there in us a creating being, that represents these laws to us as effects, or do we possess this creating faculty? In short, what works in us we cannot explain. Meanwhile its effects over sensible nature are astonishing. Against its commands nothing is to be done. To suffer torment and bodily sufferings, indeed even death, it does not outweigh : *Phalaris licet compert*, says Juvenal, and other poets apportion to it the most sublime properties. But just this deep admiration over the sublime properties of a being present in us unknown to us is just what constitutes its excellence and should be used quite preeminently for religion and instruction.",["Willkühr = power of choice (kept consistently); Bestimmungs Grund = determining ground; Neben Bewegungs Gründe = side-motives (Bewegungsgrund = motive); Nöthigung/genöthigt = necessitation/necessitated; Antrieb = impulse; Gewißen = conscience.","Dasein/Daseyn and existenz/Existenz are both rendered 'existence'; Gegenstand is the German word behind 'objects'; princip (Latinate) and Grundsatz would both be 'principle'.","OCR corrections made silently: PTrsache = Ursache; Bewußtse}rn = Bewußtseyn; Ereiheit = Freiheit; ündt = findt; rnoralische = moralische; Gestze = Gesetze; letztem = letztern (kept as 'latter').","'Einwirkung der Gesetzes Freiheit' is ambiguous (the freedom of the law / law-freedom); the English 'the freedom of law' keeps the ambiguity.","'selbst freie Bestimmung seiner Vernunft': selbst could attach to the determination, to freie, or to reason; the English 'of his reason itself' keeps selbst loosely placed, and the attachment remains open.","'die existenz derselben' (in 'auf die existenz derselben schließen'): the antecedent may be the laws or reason; left as 'the same'. 'als welches sich ... darstellt' has an ambiguous antecedent (Noumenon / character / the being).","'Phalaris licet compert' is printed so in the source (Juvenal, Sat. 8, has 'imperet'); left as it stands and not emended. The subject and object of 'wiegt es nicht auf' are unexpressed in the German and left so.","Eigenschaft = property (Beschaffenheit = constitution); 'wissen' = know; schließen = infer; 'verschulden' rendered 'to be at fault for'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-029":["§70. The consideration of the sensibility of the human being shows a remarkable distinction with regard to the *arbitrio* and especially to the morality of the human being, namely between the representation of a sensibility that includes merely sensible feeling, or that offers intellectual feeling, whereby the difference in determining grounds for our moral actions arises.",["*arbitrio* (ablative) is left in Latin as printed; 'so' in 'so blos ... einschließt' and 'so ... darbiethet' is a relative pronoun, rendered 'that'.","'Verschiedenheit ... entstehen' has a singular subject with a plural verb in the German; rendered with the singular, 'arises'.","Vor-Stellung (hyphenated in the source) = representation; Bestimmungs Gründe = determining grounds."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-030":["Namely sensibility affects either our sensible feeling alone, or at the same time with it our faculty of desire. In the latter case it becomes at the same time a determining ground of our actions, in the former not, in that the power of choice cannot be brought to action without prior determination of the faculty of desire. Hence distinguishes itself",["The passage breaks off mid-sentence ('Daher distinguirt sich') and is translated as it stands.","Begehrungs Vermögen = faculty of desire; the 'es' in 'wird es' is ambiguous (sensibility / the faculty of desire / the feeling) and is left as 'it'.","OCR: VVillkühr = Willkühr (power of choice)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-031":["a) sensible pleasure and displeasure (or sensible feeling), i.e. when the representation produced or the action itself had its ground in a faculty of desire determined through pleasure or displeasure. Hence every circumstance located outside me, every gratification or pain, advantages or representations of happiness, if these become the ground of the action or representation in such a way that the feeling of pleasure or displeasure in the representation precedes the faculty of desire, and works on it as determining ground, then the pleasure and displeasure, which is at the same time the determining ground of the faculty of desire, is sense-high feeling; which is always empirical, and determines the faculty of desire in this way, or",["OCR 'Lnlust' read as 'Unlust'.","'sinn -hohes Gefühl' is garbled in the OCR (perhaps 'sinnliches Gefühl'); translated literally as 'sense-high feeling'; reading doubtful.","Begehrungsvermögen = 'faculty of desire' (Vermögen = faculty); Bestimmungsgrund = 'determining ground'.","'Vorstellung' = representation; 'Art' = 'way' (in 'in der Art', 'in this way').","The passage begins with 'a)' and ends in 'oder', so it is continued by the next passage."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-032":["b) intellectual feeling, i.e. insofar as the representation of the law precedes and only first arises from the determination of the feeling of pleasure and displeasure, worked on the faculty of desire. The determining ground lies here in the law and the consciousness of it, reason determines the power of choice in accordance with it, and from this follows the pleasure in my representation or action, in that the claim of reason is satisfied through my compliance with its prescription, and therefore inevitably gives birth to an inner pleasure of the soul. This pleasure is rational, which has its source always in the moral determining (breaks off)",["OCR 'dei' read as 'der'; \"’\\ ernunft\" read as 'Vernunft'.","Willkühr = 'power of choice' (not in the glossary; chosen here and to be kept consistent).","'Bestimmungs' at the end: the word breaks off, as the editors indicate ('bricht ab').","The syntax of 'aus der, auf das Begehrungs Vermögen gewirkten Bestimmung ... erst entspringt' is awkward and was kept as it stands; the construction of 'gewirkten' is ambiguous."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-033":["First, it must be asserted a) against the materialists that the soul can as little be material in and before itself, nor a simple part of matter. The soul is rather a simple being (in the negative concept); hence also: the soul is immaterial and incorporeal. For matter here means not only what is itself matter throughout, but also what can be only a part of a matter, thus an extended impenetrable being.",["Wesen = 'being'; Theil = 'part'; Begriff = 'concept'.","'an und vor sich' rendered 'in and before itself'; 'vor sich' is unusual and kept literal (cf. 'an sich' = 'in itself').","'Zuvörderst' = 'first'; the same word begins the next passage."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-034":["But first, it cannot be simple and nevertheless a part of matter. For as a (even simple) part of matter it must occupy a space with the latter; but space in turn consists of spaces, hence this simple thing would again have parts; but every part of matter is again matter, hence the soul must be either material (wholly) or immaterial (for itself). But to assume a *materia cogitans* is something impossible; rather the soul is simple and in no case composed. This simplicity rests on the unity of consciousness in thinking or the unity of the manifold in representation in general. All representations relate to an object, by virtue of the determination in the mind, by virtue of which we are capable of representing anything to ourselves at all. The object, however, must absolutely be a unity, with which the consciousness of the manifold is combined, for otherwise the sentence, e.g., a. *didicisse* b. *fideliter* c. *artes* d. *mollit* f. *mores* etc., would have to be able to be thought in its whole in such a way that different powers: a. b. c. d. e. f. etc., each would think a concept for itself, and yet could be conscious of the whole sentence in common. — Since this is impossible, no representation of an object can therefore arise without an absolute unity of the subject representing itself being present, and it is impossible to let the consciousness of the representation arise through a corporeal divisibility, as if different subjects were there that divided the representation among themselves, because then it would be impossible for these parts, which would be known only to each subject for itself, to be combined without an intermediary into a whole, i.e. into unity. If one now assumes that the *materia cogitans* is an aggregate of substances, then the representation attributed to it would also have to contain an aggregate of representations, namely of its parts, which would be separated from one another, whereby one part of these would be comprehended by one part, another thought by the other, and whereby a unity would not be attainable. In order, however, that it be brought to a whole, it always remains necessary to assume a unifying subject, which combines all these parts again among themselves and with one another. Thus a composite representation cannot be attributed to the thinking subject, but only a simple representation: i.e. it combines the representation into a simple principle or is simple.",["'vel materiell (ganz) oder immateriell (für sich)': Latin 'vel' left in roman as in source, rendered 'either ... or' only for the following 'oder'; 'vel' itself is not separately rendered.","Bewußtsein = consciousness; Mannigfaltige = manifold; Vorstellung = representation; Gegenstand and Object are both 'object' (Gegenstand in 'Der Gegenstand muß...'; Object in 'Vorstellungen beziehen sich auf ein Object').","Kräfte = 'powers' (Kraft = force; power); Satz = 'sentence' here (the Latin example), though Satz can also be 'proposition'.","'Bestimmung im Gemüth' = 'determination in the mind'; 'vermöge' = 'by virtue of'.","The example is a Latin line (Ovid, 'ingenuas didicisse fideliter artes emollit mores'); the OCR has 'd. mollit' and skips 'e.' before 'f. mores', yet the following 'a. b. c. d. e. f.' suggests a lost letter; left as it stands.","OCR 'dasBewußtseyn', 'seyn,wozu' separated; 'aggregat', 'substanzen', 'subjects' kept as in the source (lowercase Latin/foreign words).","Ambiguous: 'wobei von einem Theil dieser eine Theil begriffen, von dem andern ein anderer gedacht würde' kept as awkward as in the German; 'begriffen' = 'comprehended'.","'Gemüth' and 'Gemüt' = 'mind'; 'subject' kept in lower-case roman."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-035":["Through this it is now indeed well proved that the subject can think without its principle being corporeal, the materialists are refuted, but there follows from it",["Grundsatz = 'principle', Latin 'princip' in the next passages; here 'princip' rendered 'principle'.","Breaks off mid-sentence; continued by the next passage ('b) noch nicht, ...')."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-036":["b) not yet that the soul can think without being combined with the body, and this is what pneumatology teaches, the possibility of which, however, can never be proved.",["'deßen Möglichkeit' = 'the possibility of which'; the antecedent of 'deßen' is ambiguous between 'pneumatologie' and 'was' (the thing taught); both readings preserved in the English 'which'.","'verbunden' = 'combined' (Verbindung = combination)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-037":["We are unable to investigate the causality of such a principle subsisting for itself, to determine the effects and their constitution, which could arise from it as cause, and thus do not know a priori whether such a thinking principle, separated from the body, is present. We could not learn it otherwise than empirically, by considering a soul separated from the body. But this does not work in life on account of the combination with the body, and outside of this state we become aware of nothing of the soul, and what the soul will be after death is just as impossible for us to know on the same ground: hence, since through experience we can obtain no object of representation here, it is also impossible to arrive at pneumatology, and ever to cognize whether there is any spirit, even a God, in the *universo*.",["'caussalitaet' kept as 'causality'; 'Ursach' = 'cause'; Wirkungen = 'effects'; Beschaffenheit = 'constitution'.","'wißen' = 'know' (wissen); 'zu erkennen' = 'cognize'; 'a priori' kept in the same form as the source.","'empirisch ... erfahren': 'empirically ... learn' (erfahren, cognate of Erfahrung = experience).","'Geist' = 'spirit' (glossary: mind; spirit)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-038":["We know nothing further than that the soul is an immaterial substance, and that it cannot be cognized as the *praedicat* of another being, because I cannot think a *compositum* of many substances, but rather the unity of the subject is absolutely presupposed for this, hence materialism contradicts itself in the idea, and nothing can be explained from it; but whether the soul is a (*spiritus*) spirit, as a thinking being, as the author thinks in § 742, does not follow from the latter concept. The animals also have souls, which are not for that reason spirits. Spirits are *specifice* thinking immaterial substances, which can also think without combination with the material, in case only the body were an indispensable *adminiculum* and a condition for the thinking of the soul, it would then exist as soul, but not as spirit, since the former, but not the latter, stands in a necessary connection with the body, in order to effect *actus* of the substance.",["'contradicirt' = 'contradicts' (Widerspruch = contradiction); 'Idee' = 'idea'.","'dem letztem Begriff' = 'the latter concept' (OCR/orthography 'letztem' kept).","'Verbindüng' (OCR) read as 'Verbindung' = 'combination'; 'Verknüpfung' = 'connection'.","'§ 742' refers to the author, i.e. Baumgarten (Metaphysica); the number is as printed in the OCR.","The sentence 'im Fall nur der Körper ... wäre' is a conditional with no clear apodosis marker in the first clause; left as is, with the comma splice preserved.","'Geist' = 'spirit' glossed by Latin *spiritus*; 'praedicat' kept in roman form with italics per Latin technical terms."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-039":["§ 82. In case one now considers this immaterial being in the body, which we call soul, in relation to matter, one has taken pains to find the seat of the soul, or the place where the *sensorium commune* is located, i.e. partly the place where the soul receives all impressions of objects, i.e. the sensation of them, partly the place corresponding to this sensation, which can be called the *primum movens* of the animal, i.e. that from which the whole body can be set in motion.",["Eindruck = 'impression'; Empfindung = 'sensation'; Gegenstand = 'object'; Ort = 'place'.","'Thier' = 'animal'; 'Sitz' = 'seat'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-040":["One calls this capacity of the soul the *facultatem locomotivam*, in distinction from the *facultate cogitandi*; both faculties, namely to sense and to communicate motion, one posits in the *sensorio communi*, from which they are both supposed to proceed.",["Fähigkeit = 'capacity'; Vermögen = 'faculty'; the Latin 'facultatem'/'facultate' kept with the case endings as in the source (the first likely an accusative error for the nominative).","'setzt man' = 'one posits' (setzen = posit).","'beyderseits' rendered 'both'; literally 'on both sides'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-041":["Some have now assumed the *glandula pinealis*, the pineal gland, as the place of the *sensorium commune*. For there is only one isolated nerve in the human brain, the pineal gland, which lies in the middle of the brain, where the tips of the nerves going to the brain first abut, upon which the soul reacts back when the body is to be moved. In the whole body there is only one such gland, and since the simplicity of the soul does not permit granting it more than one place, this nerve has been assumed as the common seat; this was especially the opinion of Cartesius. It is, however, in itself still always possible that there could be still other living principles in other parts of the body, which are not soul or the supreme principle of all, and then in more recent times it has been discovered that the pineal gland is, most of the time, filled with sand.",["Ort = place; Meinung = opinion; Einfachheit = simplicity; möglich = possible.","gemeinschaftlichen (Sitz) rendered 'common' (cf. *sensorium commune*); the German is the adjective of Gemeinschaft, not gemein.","principien (Latinate, lower case in the source) = 'principles'; the same English word would also stand for Grundsatz.","'auf welche von der Seele zurückgewirkt wird': the antecedent of 'welche' is left as ambiguous as the German (the tips of the nerves, or the gland).","'die mehreste Male' rendered 'most of the time'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-042":["Others, especially Bonnet, have therefore given out the callosity of the brain, i.e. the *corpus callosum*, as the *sensorium commune*, where the sensation, or from there the movement, is supposed to come forth. But if one considers the question, whether the soul perceives itself and can determine the place of its abode in the body? metaphysically, then the resolution must be declared impossible.",["The syntax of 'Betrachtet man aber die Frage ... ? metaphysisch, so muß ...' is kept as it stands, including the question mark inside the sentence.","'wo dahin ... oder daraus' is awkward in the German and is kept awkward: 'where [to] there the sensation, or from there the movement ...'.","wahrnehmen = perceive (Wahrnehmung = perception); Auflösung = resolution; Seele = soul."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-043":["No place can be assigned anywhere in space to an immaterial being, because what is object in space must be simply matter, if all relations of a local presence are not to be lacking to it. One can therefore attribute to the soul only a dynamical relation, a virtuality, toward the body, and only by virtue of this *nexus dynamicus* does it stand in combination with it, and produces its alterations through and according to the aroused sensation, but how this happens is not to be determined. For one can never think first the place from which the soul acts on the body, but one must think first the sensation, and then with the place that which immediately produces the sensation. In general, what is object of outer sense can be thought in no relation with the object of inner sense; the soul is indeed conscious of the objects of outer sense, but as little as they are present in the soul, so little does their influence on the soul determine the place where they are produced. It is as much impossible to perceive the soul externally as it is not possible to perceive the place of another soul. Just as little can the soul itself determine its place immediately in the body, nor immediately in the world; for it cannot become aware of itself as object of an outer sense. For this it would have to determine outer things in their relation to the soul and would have to perceive its presence according to place, hence therefore become aware of itself through its outer sense. But to perceive itself belongs to inner sense, to perceive itself through place belongs to outer sense. Thus it would have to perceive itself as object of inner sense at the same time through outer sense: but both senses have entirely no relation that would be local. — The soul therefore cannot become conscious of all this, because an outer perception of itself is lacking to it, and it cannot become aware of itself, outside itself, and its relations to itself, thus the soul cannot determine the place. Now it is asked how one is then to understand it, if one asserts an actual perception of the soul? The community of the soul with the body cannot be thought at all as soon as in both that which is *phaenomenon* is taken: how also can, e.g., with regard to pleasure and displeasure, with regard to the imagination, anything bodily be made noticeable? If the human being is to have representations of outer objects, they are yet not depicted in him, as it were as enclosed in space. He cognizes the objects not in material figure, i.e. the outer matter does not flow over into the soul. But it is a something unknown, which is not appearance, that flows into the soul, and so we obtain a *homogeneitas* in us with the things. Therein lies the representation, which generates in him not the *phaenomenon* itself of the body, but the *substratum* of matter, the *noumenon* in us; it separates this from the object, the *noumenon* in the body stands in agreement with that *noumenon* of the soul, and this unity is the determining ground of both to represent the object to themselves, and on this rests the *commercium corporis et animae*. So too must one explain to oneself the assumed *influxus physicus*. First of all one must think it real, i.e. that the substances outside one another, what through their existence (hence outside space, for in space the *influxus* has no difficulty) can be in influence on one another; however, this *influxus* between soul and body thought materially from one another, and yet so that both would be outside themselves, and each for itself, is something in itself impossible: and if one assumes it ideally, then this would be nothing but the *harmonia praestabilita*, and would no longer be *influxus*. It must therefore be thought as immaterial effect of the *noumenon* of both, according to which this then means nothing further than that something flows into the soul, and then no *heterogeneitas* remains that could cause doubt here, in that nothing further can be said about the constitution of this action upon it. One can therefore only say that the soul is in combination with the body, and where the body is, there is the soul, but in the body one can assign no position to the soul.",["Object (Latinate) and Gegenstand both = 'object'; here the source has 'object', 'Object', and 'Gegenstände' (Vor-Stellungen von äußern Gegenständen).","Virtualitaet, homogeneitaet, heterogeneitaet are Latinate German forms; 'virtuality' is given in English, and the two others are kept in Latin italics as *homogeneitas*, *heterogeneitas* (normalized). Phaenomenon/phaenomen/noumenon/noumeno are given in Latin.","'Es ist sowohl, die Seele äußerlich wahrzunehmen unmöglich, als auch nicht möglich ...': the German is clumsy and the English follows it.","'sich, außer sich, und ihre Verhältniße zu sich selbst' is kept as it stands; the construction is obscure and may be corrupt or elliptical.","'ihm' / 'sie' in 'die ihm nicht das phaenomen ... erzeugt; sie sondert dies vom Object ab' are left ambiguous (him/it; the representation/the soul). 'Hierin liegt die Vorstellung' could also be 'Herein' — read as in the source.","'daß die Substanzen außer einander was durch ihre existenz ... auf einander in Einfluß seyn können': the 'was' is doubtful (a verb may have dropped out, or 'was' is a garbled reading); rendered literally and not repaired. Existenz = 'existence' (the same English as Dasein).","Einfluß = influence; Einwirkung = 'action upon'; Wirkung = effect; Platz = 'position' (distinct from Ort = place); Bestimmungs Grund = 'determining ground'; wirklich = actual; Gemeinschaft = community; Verbindung = combination; bedenken = 'difficulty'.","'Vor-Stellungen' (hyphenated in the source) = 'representations'; 'z. E.' = 'e.g.'; 'dem Orte nach' = 'according to place'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-044":["One further says against this: the brain feels the thinking. It is true that the nerves are that instrument where the soul, according to its *deportement*, through aroused stimuli exercises its action, and that conversely the feelings are propagated through the nerves to the soul. *Virtualiter* one can assume that the soul is in the brain, but *localiter* this cannot be concluded from this. *Anima est tota in toto corpore et tota in quavis parte*, the ancients said rather. But to assume that it could be whole and undivided but also in two particular places is not possible, because otherwise it would also be possible that it could also be outside myself, and I would then consider myself as an object outside me, which is impossible. We merely cognize the effect of the reciprocal influence of the soul on the body, e.g. we feel a pain whose action upon us comes from the finger, and finally reduce it to the finger. But how here *sensibilitas* and *locomotivitas* hang together in their effects, or to determine the way of the effect, is as little possible as to determine the place of the reacting soul. The soul has no determinate place or local relation to the body; for this would be a mathematical relation, which we simply do not comprehend, but can only make for ourselves a concept of the dynamical relation of the soul.",["'deportement' is a French word in the source and is kept as is, in italics.","'redueiren' read as 'reduciren' (OCR slip); 'Zusammenhängen' read as the verb 'zusammenhängen' = 'hang together' (not the noun Zusammenhang = interconnection).","'sensibilitaet' and 'locomotivitaet' are Latinate German forms, given as *sensibilitas*, *locomotivitas*; Wirckung (sic) = effect, Einwirkung = 'action upon'.","'Wie aber hier ... oder die Art der Wirckung zu bestimmen, ist so wenig möglich ...': the construction is elliptical and kept so ('how ... hang together ... or to determine the way ...').","'deßen Einwirkung': 'whose action upon us' — 'upon us' is supplied by the English idiom only; the German has no object.","'welches unmöglich' has no verb in the source; 'which is impossible' follows the German ellipsis.","'Reitze' (Reize) rendered 'stimuli'; Gefühl = feeling; begreifen = comprehend; Begriff = concept; Gegenstand = object."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-045":["§ 83. The object *ad b.* of *psychologia rationalis*: what, namely, the soul was before the birth of the human being, or the question *de origine animae*, so human beings divide themselves to this effect,",["OCR 'neralich' read as 'nemlich' (namely); the stray 'h' after 'Geburt' is omitted as an OCR/line-break artifact.","'Der Gegenstand ad b.' — Gegenstand = 'object'; the sentence breaks off with a comma and continues in the next passage.","'so theilen sich die Menschen dahin' is kept literal ('divide themselves to this effect')."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-046":["1.) that one lets it arise *per ortum physicum* either as educt or as product of an older soul, or",["'Educt' (Educt) and 'product' are kept as the cognate technical terms; the sentence breaks off ('oder') and continues in the next passage."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-047":["2.) that one represents it as created by God at birth, which one calls the *ortus hyperphysicus*. The latter is the system of creations, the system of creation, where the soul is assumed as a product of the parents; and a power of creation is attributed to the parents, to which, however, one should not take one's refuge as long as one could get by with the *ortus physicus*.",["- \"ortum hyperphysicum\" (accusative, after \"nennt\") and \"orto physico\" (ablative, after \"auslangen\") are given in the nominative/Latin base form in italics; the German text has the Latin case endings, which English cannot reproduce.","- \"Schöpfungskraft\" = \"power of creation\" (Kraft = power per glossary; Schöpfung = creation, rendered consistently with \"Creationen\" = \"creations\").","- \"Das letztere\" (\"the latter\") is kept as in the German; its referent is left to the reader. \"zu dem\" is rendered \"to which,\" preserving the unclear antecedent (it may refer to the power of creation or to the system).","- \"auslangen könnte\" is rendered \"could get by\"; the subjunctive \"könnte\" is kept as the German has it.","- The \"2.)\" and the opening \"daß\" show the passage begins mid-enumeration; nothing is supplied."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-048":["The *ortus physicus*, on the other hand, gives two systems to be cognized, namely",["- \"zu erkennen geben\" is rendered literally as \"gives ... to be cognized\" (erkennen = cognize); the idiom normally means \"to indicate/give to understand.\"","- The sentence breaks off after \"nemlich\"; it is translated as it stands, without completion."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-049":["a.) one assumes the soul as an *educt*, i.e. that the human soul or the animating principle, the animal, was already present in the body before its birth (perhaps as a raw substance that was unanimated), and was now brought to life through the forces of nature. This is the system *a praeexistentiae*, in that it must indeed already have existed beforehand in the body of the parents; or, insofar as it exists free in the body, and is transferred from this into the descendants, or if it lay as a germ of the soul in the soul of the parents, and, separated as a part of the same, goes out from it. This latter is the *systema per traducem*, which however also comprehends the former under itself, since it always comes from the body of the parents, into which the germs could also have come from the air etc. through nourishment.",["- \"Kräfte der Natur\" = \"forces of nature\" (Kraft = force per glossary, here in the plural); \"Kraft\" in -047 is \"power\" in \"power of creation.\"","- \"begreift ... unter sich\" is rendered \"comprehends ... under itself\" (begreifen = comprehend).","- \"Theil\" = \"part\"; \"Substanz\" = \"substance\"; \"princip\" = \"principle.\"","- \"a praeexistentiae\" is printed thus in the source; the form is possibly a slip for \"praeexistentiae\" or \"a praeexistentia,\" and is left as it stands.","- \"bzw.\" (\"or, insofar as\") is an abbreviation in the OCR/edition, rendered \"or\"; the grammar after it (\"existire,\" \"übertragen werde\") is subjunctive and is kept as such only loosely in English.","- \"ohnbelebt\" = \"unanimated\" (belebend = \"animating\"); \"Thier\" = \"animal,\" in apposition to \"Princip\" as in the German, so the apposition is ambiguous.","- The referent of \"sie\" and of \"das erstere/letztere\" is left unresolved, as in the German.","- Possible OCR doubts: \"vielleicht als rohe Substanz, die ohnbelebt war\" and \"bzw.\" are taken as read."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-050":["b.) or one assumes it as *educt*, yet in such a way that it is brought forth by the parents through the mixture with other matters and thus has proceeded out of the soul of the parents, where, then, parents' souls give birth to children's souls. This latter is now obviously opposed to the simplicity of the soul's substance, because here it would be presupposed that the parents' souls would have to be composed, in order to yield up their children's souls as parts.",["*educt* is left in Latin as in the source (Latin *eductum*); Materien rendered 'matters' (plural of Materie = matter).","'Eltern Seelen', 'Kinder Seelen', 'Seelen Substanz' are unhyphenated compounds/juxtapositions in the German; rendered as genitives ('parents' souls', 'children's souls', 'the soul's substance'), which fixes a reading the German leaves open (could be 'soul-substance').","'sie' at the start refers back to the soul in the preceding (untranslated) context; left as 'it' without resolving.","'wo also' is kept as 'where, then', following the German's loose syntax.","'Einfachheit' = simplicity (einfach = simple); 'zusammengesetzt' = composed."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-051":["At bottom these systems, insofar as they take account of the *ortum physicum*, are formed and thought according to the analogy of the animal generation of human beings, where likewise the different systems come down to",["*ortum physicum* is as printed in the source (accusative; a Latin form of *ortus physicus* would be expected); left as it stands.","The passage breaks off at 'hinausgehen'; the sentence is continued in the next passage, and is translated without completion.","'Im Grunde' rendered 'At bottom' (Grund otherwise = ground)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s08-052":["1.) to the *systema praeformationis*, according to which either, as Leeuwenhoek assumed according to his *theoria involutionis* (the system of encasement), in the whole animal and plant kingdom, from the creation, for each species in its seed the germ for all future generations was already so laid down that one germ was laid in the other as dead matter, and only individually was animated. Hence the *animalcula spermatica* of the animals: or, as others assume, that providence had created the individual germs beforehand, lying free and next to one another, e.g. when they float in the air, are free in the body, and are educed from this.",["'Leeuvenhoeck' in the source corrected to Leeuwenhoek (spelling of the name, silently).","'Einschachtelungssystem' rendered 'system of encasement'; 'Keim' = germ; 'Saamen' = seed; 'Materie' = matter.","'educirt' (from Latin *educere*) rendered 'educed', matching *educt* in the preceding passage.","The syntax is irregular as in the German: 'wäre ... gelegt' and 'würde' stand in the subjunctive after 'annahm', and 'daß die Vorsehung ... hätte' after 'annehmen' shifts construction; preserved. 'Vorsehung' = providence.","'diesem' (in 'aus diesem') could refer to the body ('Körper') or to the air/the free state; rendered 'this', preserving the ambiguity.","'1. )' in the OCR read as '1.)'; 'generationen' kept lower-case in the German but rendered 'generations'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s09-001":["The *systema praeexistentiae* of freedom",["Literal rendering of 'liberae' as genitive 'of freedom' is not possible; the Latin 'liberae' is an adjective (feminine) and may be read as agreeing with 'praeexistentiae' ('free preexistence'), so the rendering 'of freedom' is doubtful. Possibly an OCR slip for another word (e.g. 'generalis' or similar); the reading is uncertain."]],"metaphysik-k3-s09-002":["One has now fairly generally rejected this system, because, in order to preserve a species, an infinite sum of germs would have to be assumed in each individual, and with the consumption of one seed-kernel millions would at once have to be lost, but this useless sacrifice would not at all be arranged in conformity with the purpose of nature.",["Art = species here (glossary: way; species; kind; manner). Zweck = purpose (glossary: end; purpose).","'individuum', 'consumtion', 'Saamenkernes' kept to the sense of the German; 'Summe' rendered 'sum' (cf. Inbegriff = sum total, a different word).","Syntax preserved: 'diese ... Aufopferung ... veranstaltet seyn würde' is a dependent clause under 'weil'; the 'aber' is retained as 'but'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s09-003":["2.) Hence the *systema epigenesis* is now fairly generally accepted, according to which the parents are the producing cause of generation, and the young animal thus arises as a product from the mixing of both sexes. This is more probable, as the pairing of related species, e.g., ass and horse, black and white humans, the similarities of the varieties in mules and mulattoes etc. etc. already show, and likewise the bastard plants generated through related pollen.",["wahrscheinlicher = more probable (Wahrscheinlichkeit = probability).","Ursache = cause; Zeugung = generation; Art/Arten = species (see glossary on Art); Abarten = varieties.","'Maulesel' rendered 'mules'; 'Bastardtpflanzen' (sic) rendered 'bastard plants'; 'Blumenstaub' literally 'flower-dust', rendered 'pollen'.","The final clause 'und so auch die erzeugte Bastardtpflanzen durch verwandten Blumenstaub' has no verb of its own and is left as in the German, with 'show' carried over from the preceding clause; the grammatical case of 'erzeugte Bastardtpflanzen' is irregular, possibly an OCR or transcript slip.","'Systema epigenesis' kept in Latin as in the text."]],"metaphysik-k3-s09-004":["If one now wants to apply these theories to the origin of an infinite soul, it turns out that, assumed as *educt*, the *systema propagationis per traducem vel praeexistentiae* would have to be correct, yet one would also have to presuppose that the souls of the children and of their descendants were present in the body of the parents, but then the souls of the parents would be composite, which is impossible. And, as *product* after the similarity of *epigenesis*, one would have to concede to the parents a generative power of the soul; but then the soul could not previously have been in the body of the parents as *accidens*. For the soul is substance and thus, as simple substance, cannot have existed at the same time as *accidens*, hence the parents would have had to undertake a creation, and generation would consist in this, this would therefore be *productio ex nihilo*, to which a creative power belongs. Now, however, this cannot be thought in any being that is itself created, it can indeed alter, separate, combine the form and shape of what is created, but bring forth nothing new: for this the faculty can be only in the original being of all creatures.",["OCR corrections: 'unendliehen' read as 'unendlichen' (translated 'infinite'; AA may read 'endlichen' — doubtful, since 'infinite soul' is odd); 'mäßen' read as 'müssen'.","'Wesen' rendered 'being'; 'Urwesen' rendered 'original being'; 'Substanz' = 'substance'.","'Zeugungskraft' = 'generative power'; 'Schöpfungskraft' = 'creative power'; 'Kraft' = 'power' per glossary; 'Vermögen' = 'faculty'.","'educt' and 'product' are Latinate terms left as printed, italicized; 'Ähnlichkeit der epigenesis' kept literal ('after the similarity of epigenesis').","Sentence-joining by commas ('hence... , and generation... , this would therefore...') is preserved from the German; 'dazu kann das Vermögen nur allein...' is kept in its awkward order."]],"metaphysik-k3-s09-005":["One could therefore conclude, since the soul can be neither *educt* nor *product*, therefore neither the *systema per traducem* nor that of *creationis* takes place, the soul must be *praeformirt* as a simple being, but properly we have of this no proper representation of what the situation is with the origin of the soul.",["'praeformirt' is left in Latinate form as printed (English 'preformed'); 'Vorstellung' = 'representation'; 'einfaches Wesen' = 'simple being'.","'eigentlich ... eigentliche' repeated in the German and kept as 'properly ... proper'.","'was es mit ... für eine Bewandtniß habe' is idiomatic and obscure; rendered literally as 'what the situation is'; 'Bewandtniß' = 'situation'.","'systema ... creationis': 'systema' is carried over by ellipsis, as in the German."]],"metaphysik-k3-s09-006":["One could also assert as *hypothesis* that perhaps the *noumenon*, the *substratum materiae*, which lies at the ground of all appearances of materials, besides that it expresses itself as body, also at the same time brings forth another appearance, which we call soul, i.e. that this *substratum materiae* also at the same time takes the place of the soul, without being able, to be sure, to generate a soul.",["'Erscheinungen' = 'appearances'; 'zum Grunde liegt' = 'lies at the ground'; 'Materialien' = 'materials'.","'Stelle der Seele vertrete' = 'takes the place of the soul'; 'vertreten' could also be 'represent/act as a substitute for'.","'sich als Körper äußert' = 'expresses itself as body' (äußern could also be 'manifest')."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-001":["§ 84. The unity of the monads.",["Einheit = unity; Monade kept as 'monad'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-002":["Leibnitz, after him Wolf and others, assumed that all matter is an aggregate of monads, and these simple substances in themselves, which propositions have already above been denied to the effect that it is impossible that matter could consist of simple parts, rather it is divisible *in infinitum*, hence remains matter.",["Sätze = propositions; an sich = in itself; einfach = simple; Theil = part (spelling as in source); Materie = matter.","'an sich einfache Substanzen' is ambiguous as to whether 'an sich' modifies 'einfache' or the whole phrase; the English keeps 'in themselves' before 'simple' and so preserves this.","The relative clause 'welche Sätze schon oben dahin verneint worden sind, daß...' is awkward in the German (plural 'welche Sätze', 'dahin ... daß'); the English follows it literally.","'mithin' rendered 'hence'; the subject of 'bleibe' is left implicit, as in German."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-003":["Externally now these unities were supposed to have all the relations that pertain to things in space in their composition, hence at the same time *composita* and as such to have contact, motion, etc. of composed substances.",["Äußerlich = externally; Einheiten = unities; Verhältniße = relations; Raum = space; Zusammensetzung = composition; *composita* kept in Latin as in source; componirter = composed.","'sollten' rendered 'were supposed to'; 'als solche' could attach to *composita* or to the monads/unities; left ambiguous as in the German.","The final clause 'Berührung, Bewegung usw. componirter Substanzen haben' is syntactically loose; 'of composed substances' may attach to all three nouns or only the last, as in the German."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-004":["But Leibniz above all attributed to the monads a *vis repraesentativa*, and to each monad, as its only *substantiale*, the power of representation. In itself, since we are incapable of observing the inner in other things, and can observe only ourselves, but then become aware of nothing except that we have representations, it was to this extent correctly concluded that the inner forces of the monads consisted in the power of representation and the consequences of the representation of pleasure = desire; yet substance and force are not synonymous, rather substance only has forces, it is by no means a necessary consequence that the monad *quoad substantiam* could have no other forces than the power of representation, we just do not know them. Now Leibniz further assumed, on account of the *commercium* of things in the world, that all monads stood in connection with one another, and that each monad was conscious of all the remaining monads in the *universo*. He called this the *speculum universi*, but posited that although all monads live and have *vim repraesentativam*, nevertheless some are to be considered as *sopita*, i.e. slumbering, i.e. as having either no actual consciousness at all of their representations or at least not a consciousness of the manifold in things, for which reason they could make for themselves no cognition, because they would be incapable of separating, combining, comparing representations, and he separated these off as *bruta*. Herr Kant calls this division *specula bruta* in relation to the understanding mirrors, *rationalia*. In the latter he now posited degrees and a progression of *vires repraesentativae*, namely *obscurae*, *clarae*, *distinctae*, and so on in all degrees up to the highest perfection of God. This is a continuation of the *continuum formarum*, which Bonnet *ratione physicorum* especially aggregated, where he passed from the minerals to the sponges, mushrooms, lichens, from here to the plants, from here to the zoophytes, to the animals, humans and finally to God, as who comprised in himself the origin of all things, and from whence one afterwards assumed the emanation. Thus Leibniz too assumed that matter contains in itself the material for all souls, and now built everything up to the steps of the Godhead.",["'veribus repraesentativis' in the OCR read as 'viribus' (*vires repraesentativae*); 'Claris' read as *clarae*; the Latin case endings are normalized, though the German text has 'veribus ... obscuris, Claris, distinctis' (ablative/dative form).","Latin endings in the source ('vim repraesentativam', 'in universo', 'ratione physicorum') kept as printed; I have not normalized them.","Vorstellung = representation; Vorstellungskraft = 'power of representation' (Kraft = force in the glossary, but rendered 'power' in the compound, and 'Kräfte' as 'forces').","Verknüpfung = connection; Verbindung = combination; Erkenntniß = cognition; Bewußtsein = consciousness; Mannigfaltige = manifold; Vollkommenheit = perfection; Grade = degrees; Stoff = material.","'Herr Kant' is the transcriber's third-person reference to Kant.","'als der allen Ursprung der Dinge in sich faßte': 'faßte ... in sich' rendered literally 'comprised in himself'; 'der' could be read as relative ('who') or demonstrative; ambiguity left as in the German.","'Fortsetzung des Continuum formarum, so Bonnet ... aggregirte': 'so' = relative 'which'; 'aggregirte' kept as 'aggregated'.","'substantiale' is Latin in roman type in the source; kept in italics."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-005":["Nothing can be said toward a grounding of this hypothesis of Leibniz, any more than Bonnet has rather already been very well refuted by Blumenbach.",["'als Bonnet vielmehr von Blumenbach schon sehr gut widerlegt ist' is syntactically awkward; rendered literally, with the awkwardness retained."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-006":["§ 85. According to what has been adduced so far we therefore know nothing about the origin of the soul, and just so, even if we assume the life of the soul as immaterial substance (and not predicate of another matter) and as created (only not through the generative power of the human being and his soul), it is nevertheless impossible for us to experience the state of life, namely whether it could be *vita animalis vel spiritualis*. In life we know nothing other than that the soul in thinking is in combination with the body, and if we were to go out of the body with the soul, it is just as impossible to experience anything about the state of the thinking being that would then be, or how it is now constituted.",["wissen = know (*Wissen* sense); erfahren = experience (cognate of Erfahrung); Zustand = state; Verbindung = combination; Wesen = being; beschaffen = constituted.","'wißen' in 'Im Leben wißen wir' translated 'know'; 'wie es jetzt beschaffen ist' has no clear subject (it could refer to the thinking being or to the state); left ambiguous.","'gingen wir ... hinaus, so ist es' retains the German indicative in the apodosis; rendered with a subjunctive 'were to' for English grammar."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-007":["Yet it nevertheless seems to let itself be asserted that one can assume a spiritual life, i.e. a living principle without combination with the body. For",["'anehmen' read as 'annehmen' (OCR/typo). behaupten = assert; Verbindung = combination. The passage breaks off at 'Denn' ('For'), continued in the next passage."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-008":["a.) All matter is lifeless. A proposition that indeed contradicts the animated monads of Leibniz, but which one must assume for the reason that to be matter means to be composite, but to live, to be the cause of the representation of the actuality of the action. Representing requires a thinking principle; a *compositum qua tale*, however, absolutely cannot think, because this manifold of representation must be united in the unity of the subject: if therefore a living principle is to be on hand for thinking, then it must be simple.",["Satz = proposition; Wircklichkeit = actuality (glossary: Wirklichkeit = actuality; reality); Einheit = unity; einfach = simple; Mannigfaltige = manifold; Vorstellen = representing.","'zusammengesetzt' rendered 'composite' (cf. *compositum*)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-009":["b.) From this one can conclude that a separation of the living principle in the subject from matter is possible, that this separation hinders life so little that rather, since matter is lifeless, and thus can only make difficult and hinder the thinking of the living principle, thinking would be made easier and furthered through the separation, and an unhindered life be produced. According to this, therefore, there would",["folgern = conclude. The passage breaks off ('Es würde hiernach also'), continued in c.).","'mithin' rendered 'thus'; the clause structure 'daß ... daß vielmehr, da ...' is kept as in the German."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-010":["c.) the combination with matter not be the ground of life and necessary to it. Against this",["Continues the sentence of b.). Grund = ground; Verbindung = combination."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-011":["d.) it seems indeed to be opposed that the body is an *adminiculum* to thinking, as one must assume according to experience; but the question is whether it is absolutely necessary that the body remain in combination with the soul in order to be able to think? For us it is impossible to think the effect that each separated substance (body and soul) would have, we assume in the combination that the body is an *adminiculum* as well as a hindrance in regard to thinking; yet it could still be assumed that notwithstanding this there lies in matter",["Wirkung = effect; Erfahrung = experience; Verbindung = combination; Hinderniß = hindrance. The passage breaks off, continued in e.).","'scheint diesem zwar entgegen zu seyn' — 'diesem' has no clear antecedent in the passage (probably what precedes in c.)); left as is."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-012":["e.) a preponderant hindrance, which would fall away after the separation, and the soul would work so much the more freely. Necessary to it, however, the combination with the body might not be, because matter, as noted, is lifeless, and the *adminiculum* as well as the hindrance which the body effects with respect to the soul can be contingent. Yet with certainty nothing can be decided about this question either.",["zufällig = contingent; notwendig = necessary; Verbindung = combination; Gewißheit = certainty.","'ihr' in 'Nothwendig aber dürfte ihr die Verbindung' is dative ('to it/her'), referring to the soul; English 'it' keeps this."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-013":["§ 86. As to what now concerns *ad c*, the third section of the *Psychologia rationalis*, namely what the soul will be after death, everything rests on the question whether death (separation of soul and body) will consist in a dissolution of the whole life of the human being, namely of the soul and of the body, or whether the life of the human being could also be continued after the separation from the body? For the decision of this, the essential difference of the theories now deserves consideration:",["'betritt' read as 'betrifft' (OCR/typo). Trennung = separation (not in glossary); Rücksicht = consideration (glossary Betrachtung = consideration; here Rücksicht).","'ad c' refers to the lettered division of rational psychology; kept as printed."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-014":["a.) *spem vitae futurae concernens*",["Latin kept as printed, in italics."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-015":["b.) *de immortalitate animae.*",["Latin kept as printed."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-016":["Both presuppose a future life, only the *immortalitas* involves the assumption of this, that from the nature of the soul it is regarded as necessary that the human being cannot die at all, thus a future life after death is also necessary.",["'Immortalitaet' rendered *immortalitas*; 'involvirt' = 'involves'. notwendig = necessary. 'deßen' ('of this') has no clear antecedent beyond what follows 'daß'; rendered literally."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-017":["From immortality the *resurrection* is still by far distinguished, according to its source: for this consists in this, that the substance of the human being (not the combination of the accidents of the body with the appearance, like the spiritual breath) indeed remains even after death, but the animation of the same depends solely on divine arbitrary will, and thus only in consequence of its decree is the body with the soul reawakened.",["'resurrection' is a Latinate word in roman type in the source; here kept in italics as a foreign word. 'accidenzien' = accidents; Substanz = substance; Erscheinung = appearance; Folge = sequel/consequence (here 'in consequence').","'ihres Beschlußes': 'ihres' could refer to the divine will (Willkühr) or to the 'Belebung'; rendered 'its' to keep the ambiguity.","The parenthesis '(nicht die Verbindung ... geistige Hauch)' is obscure and possibly corrupt; kept as it stands. 'Willkühr' = arbitrary will."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-018":["In relation to the *spes vitae futurae*, immortality likewise distinguishes itself as something that follows according to the order = according to the laws of the nature of the soul. For from moral grounds one assumes the future life, for the sake of the general purposiveness, which then has its ground either in general natural ends or in divine ends, and in accordance with this the future life is considered in conformity with teleology or theology. Now from the purposiveness in nature one can say as little as, if it were in conformity with the divine aim, that a future life ought to be, that it is a natural law of the soul to be immortal. To that it would belong that the nature of the human soul made this necessary, since the impossibility of dying is immortality, and it can lie only in the nature of the soul as condition not to be able to die, if it is to be necessary to assume a future life. On the other hand the determination of the divine will to the future life can be regarded only as a supernatural cause of immortality. Hence immortality and resurrection also rest on the distinction, either",["*spei* is as in the transcript (OCR or original; the genitive of *spes*). *immortalitaet* is rendered \"immortality\"; *resurrection* is the Latin word as written.","\"so wenig, als wenn es ...\" is kept as awkward as the German; the comparison is left unresolved. Absicht = \"aim\" throughout.","\"eineübernatürliche\" silently corrected to \"eine übernatürliche\". Zweck = \"end\"; Zweckmäßigkeit = \"purposiveness\"; Bestimmung = \"determination\"; Beschaffenheit as in the glossary."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-019":["a.) that the life of the soul after death is thought and assumed according to natural causes, i.e. according to the constitution of its nature, or from physiological grounds, or",["Beschaffenheit = \"constitution\" (glossary)."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-020":["b.) that it is assumed from supernatural, i.e. hyperphysical causes. In the first case immortality, in the latter resurrection. If, on the other hand, one derives it from the purposiveness in nature, or from the moral end of the highest being, then one can infer no necessity of the future life, but only that we have cause from moral grounds to expect a future life, which is *spes vitae futurae quam maxime different* from the *immortalitate animae*.",["\"Noth wendigkeit\" silently corrected to \"Nothwendigkeit\".","\"quam maxime different\" is as printed (apparently for *differt*); the grammar of the Latin phrase with \"ist\" is left as it stands. *immortalitate animae* is ablative in the text."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-021":["§ 87. If one is now to be able to assume a life of the soul after death, this requires",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-022":["1.) Continuance of its substance after death.",["Fortdauer is not in the glossary; rendered \"continuance\" throughout."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-023":["2.) Continuance of its person, i.e. identity of its personality, i.e. that it be conscious in the state after death of being precisely the same subject that it was before.",["\"bewußt sey\" is subjunctive in the German; \"ebendaßelbe subject\" = \"precisely the same subject\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-024":["N.B. If the consciousness of itself is *memoria intellectualis* *in sensu psychologico*, another *in sensu morali*, where it consists in the ground of imputation, or of imputability.",["The sentence is syntactically broken in the transcript (\"Ist ... anderer\"); translated as it stands, with \"[is]\" not supplied. \"anderer\" is doubtful (perhaps \"anders\" or \"andere\").","Zurechnung = \"imputation\"; Zurechnungsfähigkeit = \"imputability\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-025":["Both must be proved from physiological grounds.",[]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-026":["a.) One now usually sets up the proof for it *ad 1.* thus: The soul is simple, therefore indivisible, or which = *anima est incorruptibilis*; it is subjected to no decay, i.e. to no dividing-up through dissolution of its parts, therefore it cannot perish. This means inferred according to the analogy of the body. Wood, e.g., decays, to be sure, yet it is nevertheless also true that various constituents of the wood remain, which themselves are not chemically dissolvable, but nevertheless make the dividing-up possible. The soul, however, as an incorporeal substance, consequently simple, is therefore, according to the presupposition, not subjected to corruption. Mendelssohn (in his Phaedo) quite rightly recognized this proof as insufficient, in that there lay in it no necessity to assume that, because the soul is not *corruptibilis*, it could not perish, since it could indeed also perish, without dividing-up, through vanishing. But on the other hand he nevertheless inferred too much by the assumption of this, that he assumed its substance to be imperishable: he concluded this thus: if it were not so, it would be present in the one point of time, but in the following no longer be. But since between two moments there absolutely must be a time, through which the one can pass over into the other, then, if it is no longer to be in the next moment, a being and a not-being would have to follow one another, without an intermediate time of a not-wholly being and of a not-completely not-being being determined; consequently the transition would be thought through a leap, but a simple substance could not be transformed into nothing, or annihilated, through any leap, consequently the soul is immortal.",["\"unterwarfen\" silently read as \"unterworfen\"; \"•viel\" read as \"viel\".","\"nach der Voraussetzung der corruption nicht unterworfen\" is ambiguous: \"according to the presupposition\" may stand alone, or \"presupposition of corruption\" may be meant; the English keeps the first reading, the German allows both.","schließen = \"infer\", folgern = \"conclude\"; erkennen here = \"recognize\". \"Dies heißt ... geschloßen\" rendered literally. Zertheilung = \"dividing-up\"; Zeitpunkt = \"point of time\"; Augenblick = \"moment\".","\"sey\" and \"könne\" (subjunctives in indirect speech) are rendered as indicative/\"could\" where English requires."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-027":["N.B. He presupposed that a simple being could not perish like a body through successive diminution of its parts, but must either be wholly or cease at once ever to have been at all.",["\"mit einmal auf hören, überhaupt gewesen zu seyn\": \"überhaupt\" could go with \"gewesen zu seyn\" (\"to have been at all\") or with the ceasing; left as close to the German as possible."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-028":["It is correct, there is no time between which there is not always again an infinite series of times, there is no state between which and another there would not again be a state through which the transition would occur: but from this one cannot declare it impossible that the soul after death could not perish. Parts of the soul can, in its case, since it is simple, indeed not go under through dividing-up, but through an *evanescence*, i.e. a gradual remission of its forces, (opposed is intension of the forces) Just as the clarity of a representation can gradually obscure itself, so that finally the soul slumbers off over it, and thus the consciousness of it is gradually lost, so all degrees of the forces of the human soul can gradually slacken, and when they have diminished through all degrees, finally pass over into a nothing. Here there is no *saltus*, but it follows the laws of continuity, descending through ever smaller degrees, between which there is always again a time.",["\"für unmöglich erklären, daß die Seele ... nicht vergehen könne\": the negation is kept as in the German, which can be read either as \"that it could perish\" (pleonastic nicht) or as stated; the English keeps the double negation.","\"Theile der Seele können bey ihr\" is as printed; the parenthesis \"(opponirt ist intension der Kräfte)\" is left as it stands, without closing punctuation before \"So wie\". Kräfte = \"forces\"; Grad = \"degree\"; Zustand = \"state\"; *evanescenz*, *intension*, *Continuitaet* as in the text."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-029":["Of a corporeal thing one says, to be sure, *in sensu cosmologico* with right: the substance is persistent, while its accidents change, but also only in corporeal things can the persistence of substance be noticed, e.g. in heaviness, impenetrability, etc. In a simple being, however, we are unable to cognize a persistence, for even the I = self-consciousness of the human being = vanishes. Hence the principle of persistence can be applied to a body, not to a simple being, and in the soul, where everything is in flux, and everything can pass away, something be inferred from it.",["The last sentence is syntactically defective in the transcript (\"des princip\", and the construction of \"daraus etwas schließen\"); rendered as it stands. \"des\" is probably \"das\".","beharrlich/Beharrlichkeit = \"persistent/persistence\"; \"Accidenzien\" = \"accidents\" (Latinate form). \"Schwere\" = \"heaviness\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-030":["As for *ad 2.* the identity of the person of the soul, this would be the *memoria intellectualis*. To what extent such is to accrue to it after death, of this the necessity cannot be seen at all: one can indeed assume the possibility, but not prove it, consequently it cannot be inferred from it *a priori*. We find psychologically, rather, that the human being forgets what he was before.",["\"betritt\" read as \"betrifft\"; \"intellectuaiis\" read as \"intellectualis\" (OCR).","\"läßt sich a priori nicht darauf schließen\": \"a priori\" could attach to the inferring or to \"darauf\"; left unresolved."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-031":["To ground the state of the soul after death and its existence in general on psychology, since it is taken from the life of the human being in experience, thus does not work, and it is also clear of itself that from the *commercium* of the soul with the body, from the consciousness of both in the combination and given the impossibility of an attempt at even a momentary separation with continuance of consciousness, absolutely nothing can be cognized about what our soul will be after the removal of this combination. Now, besides the psychological proof from the nature of the soul, one has taken further a proof *a priori*, and indeed a metaphysical one, from *b.*, the concept of a principle of life in general.",["\"psycho -logischen\" read as \"psychologischen\"; \"commercio\" (ablative in the text) rendered as *commercium*.","\"sie\" (\"da sie ... hergenommen ist\") may refer to psychology or to the soul's state/existence; rendered \"it\", which keeps the ambiguity. \"geht also nicht an\" = \"thus does not work\" (literally, does not go on).","Aufhebung = \"removal\" (not in glossary); \"b.\" is an enumeration mark from the transcript, kept."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-032":["Let it be shown that our life (*quoad principium*) does not depend on a combination of the soul with a matter, that it therefore exists without the same, and death must be regarded as a continuation of life. Namely, since matter is lifeless, there is no ground to assume that life could be dependent on a combination with it, i.e. that matter is a ground of the possibility of the principle of life; rather the combination with matter could make for the latter an obstacle in life, in that the soul, besides its own life, is compelled also to animate matter, or to bring forth an animal life.",["\"Es sey dargethan\" is a subjunctive of reported argument; \"Let it be shown\" is a literal rendering and could also be read as \"it is said to have been shown\".","\"dem letztem\" (the latter) is taken as the principle of life; \"derselben\"/\"dieselbe\" refer back to Materie. \"thierisches Leben\" = \"animal life\"."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-033":["But one must distinguish the continuation of the principle of life, or of the faculty to live, from the *actu* of life itself. The former can possibly also continue more freely and unhindered after the separation from the body, but in order to assume the *actualitaet* of life itself, it nevertheless requires an experience whether the human being or his soul without combination with the body could exercise *actus* of life. Since the experience that we have of our state teaches us much rather on the contrary that we can think, will, desire etc. not otherwise than in combination with the body, in that the cooperation of the body manifests itself visibly through sensation as sequel of these actions of life, e.g. in each sacrifice of forces; in thinking we feel exhaustion etc., and it seems therefore that precisely its cooperation betrays a condition that lies at the ground of the exercise of life. It is therefore not proved that *actu* the soul lives after death, if the principle of life continues after death. One sees therefore that also metaphysically from the nature of the soul nothing can be proved about the *immortalitaet*, consequently all physiological arguments about this make nothing provable. Besides, one has sought the proof for the immortality of the soul in the order of ends, or taken it from the purposiveness, namely a. either derived from the ends of nature according to laws of nature. This is the teleological proof, b. or derived from the divine end according to moral (as considered divine) laws. This is the theological proof. The teleological proof (NB it is still the only one that grounds a possibility of the future life and deserves approval in metaphysical respect) one takes therefore:",["Fortdauer = continuation; Vermögen = faculty; Erfahrung = experience; Verbindung = combination; Zustand = state; Empfindung = sensation; Bedingung = condition; Grund = ground; Zweck = end; Beweis = proof; erweisen / erweislich = prove / provable.","*actu*, *actus*, *actualitaet*, *immortalitaet* are Latin or Latinized in the transcript and are kept as written (OCR 'immortaiitaet' read as 'immortalitaet'). Unsterblichkeit = immortality.","Zweckmäßigkeit = purposiveness; Beyfall = approval; Rücksicht = respect. The final colon marks a break: the passage continues in the next one."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-034":["a. There is no organic being in nature, whose existence can be thought possible otherwise than through and by virtue of an end to be effected through it. One must absolutely at the same time combine with it the idea of a being that has created the organic creature for some end. Let one take, e.g., with regard to the circulation of saps in trees, shrubs and plants and the combination of the parts of flowers, the way of their fertilization etc.",["Wesen = being; Daseyn = existence; Idee = idea; Verbindung / verbinden = combination / combine; Theile = parts.","The last sentence is kept as awkward as the German ('Man nehme ... auf ... Rücksicht'). 'Auf die Art ihrer Befruchtung' could attach to 'Verbindung der Theile' (as rendered) or to 'Rücksicht'; the English leaves this open only partly, since the German word order cannot be fully kept."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-035":["b. There are in nature no superfluous predispositions. In the case of minerals the purposiveness is indeed not yet (breaks off)",["'(bricht ab)' is the editor's remark in the Academy text; translated as it stands.","Anlagen = predispositions (not in the glossary); Zweckmäßigkeit = purposiveness."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-036":["to make possible communication of an appropriate happiness for the whole human race, and to bring the well-being of human beings into agreement with their good conduct: only, how God effects this, we do not know, but assume this presupposition only because on the one side moral good conduct is made a duty for the human race, but it lies outside its forces to procure happiness for itself. It shows itself indeed already according to laws of nature that observance of the moral laws in our conduct grants a contentment with ourselves, as well as the approval of others, but satisfaction with regard to the sensibly good it does not grant of itself. One sees from this very easily how very opposed to all concepts the assumption of the divine unconditional decrees or of the *decreti divini absoluti* is. It is this an *actus voluntatis consequentis dei*, by virtue of which God indeed *per voluntatem antecedentem*, i.e. *casu causa impulsiva erat generalis atqui omnibus subjectis generis humani communis*, wanted to know all human beings blessed, but *per voluntatem consequentem* determined himself at the creation of the world for one class to blessedness, for the other to damnation, and accordingly imparted to the first the means of grace necessary to it, but withdrew them absolutely from the latter, consequently, since by nature the human being is evil, some are to expect blessedness absolutely, others on the contrary are to be subjected absolutely to damnation. This is the doctrine of *praedestination*, from which the hypothetical *decretum divinum* is entirely distinguished in that according to it a blessedness or damnation is determined beforehand by God, after the subject has made itself worthy or unworthy of it through its moral conduct.",["The passage begins mid-sentence (infinitive clause with no governing verb) and is translated as it stands.","Glückseligkeit = happiness; Seligkeit = blessedness (selig = blessed); Verdammnis = damnation; Rathschlüße = decrees; Gnaden Mittel = means of grace; Wohlbefinden = well-being; Wohlverhalten = good conduct; Verhalten = conduct; Kräfte = forces; Beobachtung = observance (as 'Beobachtung' of laws); Lehre = doctrine; bestimmen = determine.","'Selig wißen wollte' is rendered literally as 'wanted to know ... blessed'.","OCR corrections: 'unbedingtenRathschlüße' (spacing), 'absolut i' read 'absoluti', 'Verdammmß' read 'Verdammnis', 'unterscheidt' read 'unterscheidet', 'erstem/letztem' read 'ersten/letzten', 'Natur gesetzen' read 'Naturgesetzen'.","The Latin clause 'casu causa impulsiva erat generalis atqui omnibus subjectis generis humani communis' is kept as transcribed; it may be corrupt and is left untranslated.","'Absolute' in 'einige absolute die Seligkeit erwarten' is adverbial in the German; rendered 'absolutely'."]],"metaphysik-k3-s10-037":["*finitum* — 20th Febr. 95.",["Fragment: *finitum* is presumably the end of a longer word (e.g. *definitum*/*infinitum*) carried over from the preceding passage; not completed here. '20ter febr. 95.' is a date note, rendered as written."]]};
